// Generated by authoring/psyc301/question-versions/build.py. Original questions stay in module2-data.js.
window.M2_QUESTION_VERSIONS = {"3001": {"college": {"stem": "A person explains a concept fluently while studying with a friend but, a week later, cannot bring it to mind when asked a direct question. Keeping encoding, storage, and retrieval distinct, which conclusion is justified?", "options": ["The recall failure fits a retrieval problem, but this single observation cannot by itself show which memory operation broke down.", "The recall failure proves encoding never happened, because information that was encoded will always come back whenever it is needed.", "The recall failure proves the information was erased from storage, because anything still held in storage can always be accessed on demand.", "The recall failure rules out a retrieval problem, because retrieval is the step that converts new input into a form suitable for storage."], "correct": 0, "why": "Encoding converts input into a usable form, storage holds it, and retrieval takes it back out; one failure to recall is consistent with a retrieval problem but does not pinpoint which step failed. The distractors wrongly treat encoded or stored information as always accessible, or confuse retrieval with encoding."}, "simple": {"stem": "In memory, what does retrieval mean?", "options": ["Taking information back out of memory storage when it is needed.", "Converting new information into a usable form so it can be stored.", "Holding information in memory over a period of time.", "Repeating information silently so it stays in mind longer."], "correct": 0, "why": "Retrieval is taking memories out of storage. Converting information into a usable form is encoding, holding it is storage, and silent repetition is maintenance rehearsal."}}, "3002": {"college": {"stem": "Episodic and semantic memory are both branches of declarative long-term memory on the memory tree. Which statement correctly matches each one to the kind of content it holds?", "options": ["Episodic memory holds personally experienced events, while semantic memory holds general facts and knowledge.", "Episodic memory holds general facts and world knowledge, while semantic memory holds personally experienced events.", "Episodic memory holds practiced motor skills, while semantic memory holds brief echoes of recent sounds.", "Episodic memory holds fleeting visual images, while semantic memory holds phone numbers being repeated."], "correct": 0, "why": "Episodic memory stores events a person experienced (Tulving's “mental time travel”), and semantic memory stores knowledge of facts. The second option reverses the two; motor skills are procedural memory, and brief visual or auditory traces belong to sensory memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "What kind of information does episodic memory hold?", "options": ["Memories of specific events that you experienced.", "General facts and knowledge about the world around you.", "Learned motor skills, such as how to ride a bike.", "Very brief copies of sights and sounds just sensed."], "correct": 0, "why": "Episodic memory is memory for personally experienced events, a kind of mental time travel. General facts are semantic memory, skills are procedural memory, and brief sensory copies are sensory memory."}}, "3003": {"college": {"stem": "In Atkinson and Shiffrin's (1968) modal model, a person looks up a phone number, repeats it to themselves, and later recalls it without looking again. Which statement correctly separates the model's memory stores from its control processes?", "options": ["Sensory, short-term, and long-term memory are the stores; attending to and rehearsing the number are control processes acting on it.", "Attention, rehearsal, and retrieval are the three stores; sensory and long-term memory are processes that move the number between them.", "Sensory memory permanently stores the whole directory, and rehearsal only decides which entry is currently visible to the person.", "Short-term memory holds only incoming sensory input, so recalling a previously learned number must bypass short-term memory entirely."], "correct": 0, "why": "The modal model has three stores (sensory, short-term, long-term) plus control processes such as attention and rehearsal; retrieval can bring long-term information back into short-term memory. The distractors swap stores and processes or give sensory memory a permanence it lacks."}, "simple": {"stem": "Atkinson and Shiffrin's (1968) modal model divides memory into which three stores?", "options": ["Sensory memory, short-term memory, and long-term memory, in that order.", "Encoding, storage, and retrieval, each holding a different kind of information.", "Episodic, semantic, and procedural memory, each lasting about 20 seconds.", "Iconic, echoic, and working memory, all lasting less than one second."], "correct": 0, "why": "The modal model's three stores are sensory, short-term, and long-term memory. Encoding, storage, and retrieval are memory operations, and episodic, semantic, and procedural are kinds of long-term memory."}}, "3004": {"college": {"stem": "While scanning a long list of numbers, a person singles out one number and then repeats it under their breath until they finish dialing. In modal-model terms, which pairing matches each action with the process it reflects?", "options": ["Singling out the number reflects attention, and repeating it until dialing reflects maintenance rehearsal.", "Singling out the number reflects maintenance rehearsal, and repeating it until dialing reflects attention.", "Singling out the number reflects procedural learning, and repeating it until dialing reflects sensory registration.", "Singling out the number reflects retroactive interference, and repeating it until dialing reflects elaborative rehearsal."], "correct": 0, "why": "Selecting one item for further processing is attention, and repeating it without adding meaning is maintenance rehearsal. The second option swaps the two, and the last mislabels plain repetition as elaborative rehearsal."}, "simple": {"stem": "You pick one phone number out of a list and keep repeating it until you dial. What is the repeating called?", "options": ["Maintenance rehearsal, which keeps the number active in short-term memory.", "Elaborative rehearsal, which links the number to things you already know.", "Retroactive interference, which pushes older phone numbers out of memory.", "Sensory memory, which holds an exact copy of the number for years."], "correct": 0, "why": "Repeating information over and over to keep it in short-term memory is maintenance rehearsal. Elaborative rehearsal would link the number to existing knowledge, which simple repetition does not do."}}, "3005": {"college": {"stem": "A person dials a number they memorized last month by bringing it to mind and holding it there while pressing the keys. Which statement about the modal model fits this situation?", "options": ["Information can be retrieved from long-term memory back into short-term memory, where it is held for immediate use.", "Information flows only from sensory to short-term to long-term memory, so a number learned earlier cannot become active again.", "The number must have stayed in sensory memory for the whole month in order to guide the person's dialing now.", "Retrieving the number erases the difference between short-term and long-term memory, since both now serve one function."], "correct": 0, "why": "In the modal model, retrieval brings a number from long-term memory back into short-term memory, where it is remembered long enough to make the call. Flow is not one-way, sensory memory lasts less than a second, and interaction between stores does not make them identical."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the modal model, what happens when you remember an old phone number to call it again?", "options": ["The number is retrieved from long-term memory back into short-term memory.", "The number moves out of short-term memory and back into sensory memory.", "The number is encoded again from scratch, as if it were never seen.", "The number stays in long-term memory and never enters short-term memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "Retrieval moves the number from long-term memory back into short-term memory so it can be used. Information does not flow back into sensory memory, and the number must be active in short-term memory to guide dialing."}}, "3006": {"college": {"stem": "Sensory memory includes both an icon and an echo. Which statement correctly characterizes each of them?", "options": ["An icon is a fleeting visual image, and an echo is a brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system.", "An icon is a brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system, and an echo is a fleeting visual image.", "An icon is a personally experienced episode, and an echo is a general fact stored in long-term memory.", "An icon is repetition that keeps information active, and an echo is linking new information to prior knowledge."], "correct": 0, "why": "An icon is a brief visual sensory trace and an echo is a brief auditory one. The second option swaps the modalities; the others describe long-term memory types or rehearsal, not sensory memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is an echo in sensory memory?", "options": ["A brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system.", "A fleeting visual image that remains after a picture disappears.", "A memory of a sound that is kept in storage for many years.", "Repeating a word aloud to keep it in short-term memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "An echo is the brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system. A fleeting visual image is an icon, and sensory traces last well under a second, not years."}}, "3007": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to test Sperling's (1960) claim that more of a briefly flashed letter array is available at first than participants can report before it fades. Which design best tests this claim?", "options": ["Compare whole report with report of one randomly chosen row signaled right after the display ends, with exposure time held constant.", "Compare whole report with report of one row identified before the display, so participants can focus on just that row during the exposure.", "Compare whole report after a brief exposure with partial report after a much longer exposure, changing both time and task at once.", "Compare people who name the whole array with people who copy one row while the letters remain visible on the screen."], "correct": 0, "why": "A row cue given after the display ends samples information that was already registered, without letting participants choose a row in advance. An advance cue allows selective encoding, unequal exposure adds a confound, and copying a visible display does not test memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report method, what did participants do?", "options": ["After the letters flashed, they heard a tone telling them which row to report.", "Before the letters flashed, they were told which single row to memorize.", "They reported as many letters as they could from the entire display.", "They copied one row of letters while the display stayed on the screen."], "correct": 0, "why": "In partial report, a tone after the letter array told participants which row to report. Reporting the whole display is the whole report method, and an advance cue was not part of the procedure."}}, "3008": {"college": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) study, whole report yielded about 4.5 of 12 letters, yet when a tone right after the display signaled a row, participants reported about 3.3 of that row's 4 letters. What does this comparison best support?", "options": ["More letters were available at first than whole report captured, since most of whichever row was cued could be reported.", "Only about 4.5 letters were ever registered, since whole report directly measures everything available right after exposure.", "All twelve letters were transferred into long-term memory, since accurate partial report shows permanent storage of the array.", "Only the cued row was registered during the display, since a tone played afterward determines where attention was beforehand."], "correct": 0, "why": "High partial report for whichever row is cued implies that most of the array was briefly available, followed by rapid loss. It does not show permanent storage, and a cue that comes after the display cannot direct attention during it."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report method, about how many letters of the cued row did participants report?", "options": ["About 3.3 of the 4 letters in the cued row, or 82%.", "About 4.5 of the 12 letters in the whole display, or 37.5%.", "About 1.5 of the 4 letters in the cued row, or 37.5%.", "All 4 letters in the cued row every time, or 100%."], "correct": 0, "why": "With an immediate tone, participants reported about 3.3 of 4 letters (82%) from any cued row. About 4.5 of 12 (37.5%) was the whole report result."}}, "3009": {"college": {"stem": "In a follow-up to Sperling (1960), the letter display and the cued row stay the same, but the delay before the tone varies. Which result would most directly support a rapidly fading sensory trace?", "options": ["Partial-report accuracy drops as the tone is delayed and nears whole-report levels, even though exposure time is unchanged.", "Partial-report accuracy improves when the display is shown for longer, while the tone delay stays the same in every condition.", "Whole-report accuracy differs between groups given different instructions beforehand, with no change in the tone delay.", "Participants copy the cued row accurately while the letters stay visible, no matter how long they wait before copying."], "correct": 0, "why": "Varying only the tone delay isolates how long the trace lasts before a row can be selected; a decline toward whole-report levels shows rapid fading. The other outcomes change exposure or instructions, or let participants see the letters directly."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) delayed partial report condition, what happened as the tone came later after the display?", "options": ["People reported fewer letters, close to whole-report levels.", "People reported more letters from the cued row than with no delay.", "People reported every letter in the cued row without any errors.", "People's accuracy stayed the same across all of the different delays."], "correct": 0, "why": "As the tone was delayed, partial-report accuracy fell, and by about one second it was close to whole-report performance. This shows that sensory memory fades very quickly."}}, "3010": {"college": {"stem": "Someone claims that Sperling's (1960) whole-report result proves visual sensory memory holds only four or five letters. What is the strongest objection to this claim?", "options": ["Naming letters takes time, so the rest can fade before being reported; immediate partial report shows more was available at first.", "Whole report must measure long-term memory, since any spoken report depends on permanent storage rather than a sensory trace.", "The partial-report tone adds letters to the display, so the whole-report and partial-report conditions cannot fairly be compared.", "The result shows sensory capacity is unlimited, since reporting one row accurately guarantees accurate report of every row."], "correct": 0, "why": "People can report only four or five items before the trace decays, but partial report shows the whole scene was encoded at first. The claim confuses how much can be reported with how much was initially available; it does not follow that capacity is unlimited or storage permanent."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Sperling (1960) conclude about visual sensory memory?", "options": ["It has a large capacity, but the information in it decays very fast.", "It has a small capacity of four or five items and decays slowly.", "It has a small capacity, and the information in it lasts for years.", "It has a large capacity, and the information in it never decays."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sperling concluded that sensory memory has a large capacity but fast decay, with most of it gone within about one second. The four-or-five-item limit reflects what can be reported before decay, not what was encoded."}}, "3011": {"college": {"stem": "Two people study an unfamiliar term for equal time. One spells it over and over; the other works out how it relates to ideas they already know. How are these strategies best labeled?", "options": ["The first is mainly maintenance rehearsal; the second is elaborative rehearsal because it links the term to existing knowledge.", "The first is mainly elaborative rehearsal; the second is maintenance rehearsal because existing knowledge blocks new encoding.", "Both are maintenance rehearsal, because any deliberate study is defined by its repetition rather than by the links it builds.", "Both rely on sensory persistence, because keeping attention on the term removes any need for short-term control processes."], "correct": 0, "why": "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information; elaborative rehearsal connects it to knowledge in long-term memory. Spending equal time does not make the two strategies the same, and neither relies on sensory memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is elaborative rehearsal?", "options": ["Linking new information to knowledge already stored in long-term memory.", "Repeating information silently to keep it in short-term memory.", "Holding an exact sensory copy of information for under a second.", "Forgetting older information because newer information gets in the way."], "correct": 0, "why": "Elaborative rehearsal links new information with existing knowledge in long-term memory and is a good way to transfer it there. Silent repetition is maintenance rehearsal."}}, "3012": {"college": {"stem": "Research on short-term memory focuses on two issues, duration and capacity. Which statement correctly defines both and names the process that tends to extend each?", "options": ["Duration is how long information stays in memory, extended by rehearsal; capacity is how much fits at once, extended by chunking.", "Duration is how much fits in memory at once, extended by chunking; capacity is how long information stays there, extended by rehearsal.", "Duration is how many items are retrieved, extended by interference; capacity is how many items are shown, extended by decay.", "Duration is repetition without meaning, extended by encoding; capacity is linking to knowledge, extended by retrieval."], "correct": 0, "why": "Duration is how long things stay in memory, and rehearsal seems to extend it; capacity is how many things fit at a time, and chunking seems to extend it. The second option swaps the two definitions."}, "simple": {"stem": "In short-term memory, what does capacity refer to?", "options": ["How many things can fit in memory at one time.", "How long information stays in memory before fading.", "How quickly information can be pulled out of memory.", "How well new information links to older knowledge."], "correct": 0, "why": "Capacity is how many things fit in memory at a time. How long information stays is duration."}}, "3013": {"college": {"stem": "In the task used by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), participants receive three letters and a number, then count backward by threes from that number before recalling the letters. What is the counting task for?", "options": ["It keeps participants from repeating the letters during the delay, so retention can be measured without maintenance rehearsal.", "It turns the letters into meaningful chunks, so the task can measure how expertise helps people reorganize the letter sequence.", "It wipes out all effects of earlier letter sets, so any forgetting left over can be blamed purely on time-based decay.", "It serves as the recall response itself, so memory for the letters is scored from how accurately participants count."], "correct": 0, "why": "Counting backward blocks rehearsal of the letters during the delay. It does not remove interference from earlier trials, so forgetting cannot automatically be assigned to pure decay."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the task used by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), why did participants count backward by 3s?", "options": ["To stop them from rehearsing the three letters during the delay.", "To test how well they could do arithmetic under time pressure.", "To help them group the three letters into one meaningful chunk.", "To give them extra practice before recalling the three letters."], "correct": 0, "why": "Counting backward kept participants from rehearsing the letters, so the researchers could see how fast unrehearsed information is lost. It was not a test of arithmetic or a chunking aid."}}, "3014": {"college": {"stem": "To separate a purely time-based explanation of forgetting in the task used by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) from one involving built-up proactive interference, which comparison is most informative?", "options": ["Compare recall after matched short and long delays on the first trial and on later trials, with the same counting task throughout.", "Compare a group given an 18-second filled delay with a group given no delay at all, ignoring how many earlier trials each completed.", "Compare early trials that use three letters with later trials that use six letters, so trial history and load change together.", "Compare people who count backward during the delay with people allowed to repeat the letters, treating any gap as interference."], "correct": 0, "why": "Matching delays across trial positions tests whether earlier trials matter, which pure decay does not predict. The other comparisons ignore trial history or change load or rehearsal along with it."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), what did recall look like on the very first trial?", "options": ["Recall was high after both the 3-second and 18-second delays.", "Recall was high after 3 seconds but near zero after 18 seconds.", "Recall was near zero after both the 3-second and 18-second delays.", "Recall was much higher after 18 seconds than after 3 seconds."], "correct": 0, "why": "On the first trial, recall stayed high even after an 18-second delay. Poor recall at long delays appeared only after many trials, which points to interference from earlier letters."}}, "3015": {"college": {"stem": "In the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), recall after a long delay was fairly high on the first trial but worse on later trials. Which conclusion is most defensible?", "options": ["Earlier trials contribute to later forgetting, so the averaged delay curve cannot be read as evidence for time-based decay alone.", "Time has no effect on any kind of memory, because a first-trial advantage disproves every account based on gradual loss over time.", "The first trial produces permanent storage of every trigram, so later errors must come only from misunderstood instructions.", "The averaged delay curve separates decay from interference, because averaging cancels the effects of earlier trials on memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "The trial-position effect shows that earlier trigrams add to forgetting, so the averaged curve is not pure decay. It does not show that time never matters; forgetting in short-term memory involves both decay and interference."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) find about recall after long delays across a series of trials?", "options": ["Recall after long delays got worse as the series of trials went on.", "Recall after long delays got better as the series of trials went on.", "Recall after long delays stayed the same on every trial in the series.", "Recall after long delays was perfect on every trial after the first."], "correct": 0, "why": "Recall dropped with longer delays, but this depended on trial position: long-delay recall got worse as the series of trials got longer."}}, "3016": {"college": {"stem": "A follow-up to Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) keeps the delay and counting task identical but compares people who have already studied many similar trigrams with people on their very first trigram. Which result supports proactive interference?", "options": ["People with many earlier trigrams recall the current trigram less accurately than first-trial people at the same delay.", "Both groups recall the current trigram equally well, but one group counts backward noticeably faster than the other.", "First-trial people recall fewer letters, but only when given longer trigrams and a longer delay than the other group.", "People forget an earlier trigram after studying a new one, while their recall of the new trigram stays unaffected."], "correct": 0, "why": "Proactive interference is older information impairing recall of newer information, so prior trigrams should lower current recall at a matched delay. The last option describes the reverse direction, retroactive interference."}, "simple": {"stem": "Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) set out to study decay. What other kind of forgetting did their results reveal?", "options": ["Proactive interference, where older items make new items harder to recall.", "Retroactive interference, where new items make older items harder to recall.", "Sensory decay, where a visual icon fades in less than one second.", "Procedural forgetting, where a practiced motor skill is slowly lost."], "correct": 0, "why": "Letters from earlier trials made it harder to recall the current letters, which is proactive interference. Retroactive interference runs the other way, with new information disrupting old."}}, "3017": {"college": {"stem": "When newly learned information disrupts recall of older information, this is called ______, whereas older information disrupting recall of newer information is called ______.", "options": ["retroactive interference in the first case and proactive interference in the second", "proactive interference in the first case and retroactive interference in the second case", "maintenance rehearsal in the first case and elaborative rehearsal in the second", "encoding failure in the first case and sensory persistence in the second"], "correct": 0, "why": "Retroactive interference is new information disrupting old; proactive interference is old information disrupting new. Identify which memory is the target, not just which word comes first."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is retroactive interference?", "options": ["When new information makes it harder to remember old information.", "When old information makes it harder to remember new information.", "When information fades from memory just because time passes.", "When information is repeated over and over to keep it active."], "correct": 0, "why": "Retroactive interference is a new memory interfering with remembering old information, such as a new phone number blocking the old one. Old information blocking new is proactive interference."}}, "3018": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher defines digit span as the longest sequence a person recalls completely and in the correct order. Which procedure best matches that definition?", "options": ["Present lists of increasing length and score a list as correct only when every digit is reproduced in its original order.", "Present only four-digit lists and use the percentage of single digits recalled in any order as the person's longest span.", "Present lists of increasing length and score a list as correct whenever its digits are recognized, even if their order is reversed.", "Present one ten-digit list and estimate span from how long the person looks at it before beginning to respond."], "correct": 0, "why": "The definition requires lists that grow in length and complete recall in order. Partial unordered recall, recognition, and looking time do not measure that."}, "simple": {"stem": "How is a person's digit span measured?", "options": ["By the longest row of digits they recall completely right, in order.", "By the total number of digits they recall from all rows, in any order.", "By how many seconds they can look at a row before forgetting it.", "By how many digits they can recognize when shown them a second time."], "correct": 0, "why": "Digit span is the number of digits in the longest row recalled completely correctly. Counting scattered digits or recognition does not meet that rule."}}, "3019": {"college": {"stem": "Which interpretation of Miller's (1956) “seven, plus or minus two” best fits later findings on visual capacity and chunking?", "options": ["It counts capacity in items whose information content can vary, so it should not be applied as one fixed limit to every task.", "It shows that exactly seven sensory features are stored in every modality, regardless of familiarity, organization, or complexity.", "It states how many seconds every short-term trace lasts, so a visual estimate of four items measures the very same quantity.", "It rules out any knowledge-based gains in span, since combining small items into meaningful groups cannot change performance."], "correct": 0, "why": "What counts as one item matters: visual change-detection estimates are smaller, complex objects lower capacity, and chunking raises span. Capacity and duration are different quantities, so the third option confuses them."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Miller (1956) propose about short-term memory?", "options": ["People can hold about seven, plus or minus two, items at once.", "People can hold about three to four visual items at once.", "People can hold information for about seven seconds without rehearsal.", "People can hold an unlimited number of items for a short time."], "correct": 0, "why": "Miller (1956) proposed that people can remember about 7 ± 2 items, such as digits, words, or phrases. Three to four items is the later estimate for visual short-term memory from change detection."}}, "3020": {"college": {"stem": "Following Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004), a researcher asks whether visual capacity depends on how complex each object is rather than on a fixed number of equivalent objects. Which design best answers this?", "options": ["Compare change detection across matched set sizes for simple colored squares and complex objects, with timing and responses held constant.", "Compare simple objects at small set sizes with complex objects at large set sizes, judging the effect of complexity from overall accuracy.", "Compare immediate recognition of colored squares with delayed free recall of complex objects, treating any difference as due to complexity alone.", "Present only one type of object and ask participants whether it looks complicated, without measuring retention or comparison accuracy."], "correct": 0, "why": "Varying complexity while matching set size and task conditions isolates the effect of complexity. Changing set size, delay, or task type along with complexity leaves other explanations open."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) use to study short-term memory capacity?", "options": ["Change detection with colored squares and more complex objects.", "Digit-span tests using rows of digits of increasing length.", "Counting backward by threes between hearing letters and recall.", "Chess positions shown briefly to masters and to beginners."], "correct": 0, "why": "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) used the change detection procedure with colored squares and more complex objects. Digit span, backward counting, and chess positions belong to other studies."}}, "3021": {"college": {"stem": "A change-detection study finds accuracy falling sharply beyond about four colored squares, while digit-span tests allow more digits. How are these findings best reconciled?", "options": ["The tasks differ in stimuli and demands, so a visual estimate of about three to four items need not match span for digits or words.", "The visual result must be discarded, because Miller's (1956) proposal requires everyone to hold at least five objects in every task.", "The digit-span result proves visual memory holds exactly the same number of objects, so the accuracy drop cannot reflect retention.", "The visual result measures only how long rehearsal lasts, whereas digit span alone can estimate how much information is retained."], "correct": 0, "why": "Visual short-term memory measured by change detection may have a smaller capacity than memory for lists of digits or words, and there may be several modality-specific forms of short-term memory. The visual task still measures retained information."}, "simple": {"stem": "In change-detection studies of visual short-term memory, about how many items can people hold at once?", "options": ["About three to four items before accuracy falls off sharply.", "About seven, plus or minus two, items, as with digit lists.", "About twelve items, the size of the largest display tested.", "Only one item at a time, no matter how big the display is."], "correct": 0, "why": "Accuracy drops steeply with more than four items, suggesting a visual capacity of about three to four. Seven plus or minus two is Miller's estimate for items like digits and words."}}, "3022": {"college": {"stem": "Two arrays each contain four objects: one has simple colored squares and the other complex shaded cubes. Someone argues that equal item counts mean equal memory demands. Which response best fits Alvarez and Cavanagh's (2004) change-detection findings?", "options": ["Equal object counts need not mean equal information demands, since more complex objects lower change-detection performance.", "Equal object counts guarantee equal demands, since the visual system treats every object as one identical item regardless of features.", "Complex objects raise capacity, since their extra details give memory more independent storage slots to fill with information.", "Any difference must come from different retention intervals, since complexity cannot affect performance when item count is fixed."], "correct": 0, "why": "Same/different judgments depend on stimulus complexity: capacity is highest for colored squares and lowest for shaded cubes. Matching the number of objects does not match the amount of information to be held."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a change-detection task like Alvarez and Cavanagh's (2004), two displays each have four items: one has colored squares, the other shaded cubes. Which display is easier to hold in visual short-term memory?", "options": ["The colored squares, because simpler objects carry less information.", "The shaded cubes, because complex objects stand out more clearly.", "Both are equally easy, because each display has four items.", "Neither can be held, because four items is beyond visual capacity."], "correct": 0, "why": "Visual capacity is higher for simple colored squares than for complex shaded cubes, so the squares are easier to hold. Item count alone does not set the memory load."}}, "3023": {"college": {"stem": "Ericsson and colleagues (1980) trained a college student to reach a digit span of 79 by using familiar running times. Which explanation best separates this improvement from an unlimited increase in raw memory capacity?", "options": ["Familiar knowledge let the digits be recoded into larger meaningful chunks, raising span without raising capacity for all kinds of information.", "Training expanded each sensory store to hold 79 unrelated objects, so the same span should appear at once for unfamiliar visual patterns and words.", "The student stopped relying on long-term knowledge, showing that meaningful organization adds nothing to an unusually high digit span.", "The result shows that Miller's (1956) items must always be single digits, because trained and untrained people group digits identically."], "correct": 0, "why": "The student chunked digits into meaningful running times from a sport he knew, which depends on long-term knowledge. Better span for this kind of information does not show a general expansion of every memory limit."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Ericsson and colleagues' (1980) study, how did a college student reach a digit span of 79?", "options": ["By chunking digits into meaningful running times, a sport he knew well.", "By silently repeating every digit over and over until it was time to recall.", "By having an unusually large short-term memory store from birth.", "By counting backward by threes to block out other information."], "correct": 0, "why": "After training, the student chunked digits into meaningful running times. The gain came from chunking with existing knowledge, not from rehearsal or a naturally larger store."}}, "3024": {"college": {"stem": "To test the chunking explanation of Chase and Simon's (1973) chess findings, which design and predicted pattern give the strongest evidence?", "options": ["Compare masters and beginners on meaningful and random arrangements with matched piece counts; predict a master advantage mainly for meaningful ones.", "Compare masters on meaningful positions with beginners on random positions; attribute any master advantage entirely to the masters' meaningful chunking.", "Give masters more viewing time than beginners on meaningful positions; take higher master recall as evidence that viewing time is irrelevant.", "Compare masters and beginners only on meaningful positions; conclude that any difference must come from familiarity with piece patterns."], "correct": 0, "why": "Crossing expertise with arrangement type shows whether the master advantage depends on meaningful structure. The other designs confound group with arrangement or viewing time, or leave out the random condition needed for the test."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find when chess masters and beginners recalled random piece arrangements?", "options": ["Masters and beginners placed about the same number correctly.", "Masters placed far more pieces correctly than the beginners did.", "Beginners placed far more pieces correctly than the masters did.", "Neither group was able to place any of the pieces correctly."], "correct": 0, "why": "With random arrangements, masters and beginners performed about the same. The masters' large advantage appeared only for meaningful game positions, which they could chunk."}}, "3025": {"college": {"stem": "A learner arranges items along a familiar route (a “memory palace”) and deliberately shifts attention from one item to the next while recalling them. What do these strategies illustrate?", "options": ["Control processes, which are active strategies a person uses to focus on and make information more memorable.", "Purely passive storage, since directing attention and organizing information cannot change how memory is used.", "Sensory persistence, since a remembered route stays physically visible to the learner throughout the learning.", "Proactive interference, since the familiar route automatically blocks the new items from being remembered."], "correct": 0, "why": "Control processes are active processes a person controls, including rehearsal, strategies that make a stimulus more memorable, and attention strategies. A memory palace and deliberate attention shifts are both of this kind."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the modal model, what are control processes?", "options": ["Active processes a person can control, such as rehearsal and attention.", "Automatic processes that hold sensory copies for under a second.", "Permanent stores that keep information for years or even decades.", "Types of forgetting caused by old and new memories competing."], "correct": 0, "why": "Control processes are active processes the person controls, such as maintenance rehearsal, memory strategies, and attention strategies. They are not stores or types of forgetting."}}, "3051": {"college": {"stem": "A person recalls a specific birthday party, knows that Paris is the capital of France, and can still ride a bicycle. How does the memory tree sort these three memories?", "options": ["The party is episodic memory, the capital is semantic memory, and riding the bicycle is procedural memory.", "The party is procedural memory, the capital is episodic memory, and riding the bicycle is semantic knowledge.", "All three are sensory memories, because each one still influences how the person thinks or behaves now.", "All three are short-term memories, because the person can bring each one of them to mind right now."], "correct": 0, "why": "Personal events are episodic and facts are semantic (both declarative long-term memory), while skills are procedural. Being able to use a memory now does not make it a sensory or short-term store."}, "simple": {"stem": "On the memory tree, which two types fall under declarative long-term memory?", "options": ["Episodic memory for events and semantic memory for facts.", "Procedural memory for skills and episodic memory for events.", "Sensory memory for sights and short-term memory for numbers.", "Procedural memory for skills and semantic memory for facts."], "correct": 0, "why": "Declarative long-term memory branches into episodic and semantic memory. Procedural memory is the other branch of long-term memory, and sensory and short-term memory are separate stores."}}, "3052": {"college": {"stem": "After a bell stops ringing, its sound seems to linger for a moment; after a camera flash, an image of the scene briefly lingers too. Which pairing correctly names these two traces?", "options": ["The lingering sound is an echo and the lingering image is an icon, both forms of sensory memory.", "The lingering sound is an icon and the lingering image is an echo, both forms of sensory memory.", "Both are procedural memories, since each one continues after the stimulation itself has ended.", "Both are semantic memories, since their sensory sources are no longer present in the environment."], "correct": 0, "why": "An echo is the brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system, and an icon is a fleeting visual image. Lingering after the stimulus ends does not make a trace a procedural skill or semantic knowledge."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is an icon in sensory memory?", "options": ["A fleeting mental image that lasts less than a second.", "A brief continuation of a sound after it has ended.", "A detailed picture memory that lasts for many years.", "A meaningful symbol stored as a fact in long-term memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "An icon is a fleeting visual image held in sensory memory for less than a second. The continuation of a sound is an echo."}}, "3053": {"college": {"stem": "In a modified version of Sperling's (1960) task, participants are told which row to memorize before the letter array appears. Why would high accuracy for that row say less about broad initial availability than a cue given after the array?", "options": ["The advance cue lets participants focus on that row during exposure rather than drawing on an unknown row afterward.", "The advance cue guarantees that participants register every row of the letter array with equal accuracy at the start.", "The advance cue makes the letter array physically larger and adds to the number of letters actually displayed.", "The advance cue removes any need for the letters to be registered visually before participants report them."], "correct": 0, "why": "A cue after the display prevents participants from choosing in advance which row to encode. With an advance cue, accuracy for the cued row can come from focused attention without the other rows being retained."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report method, when did participants learn which row to report?", "options": ["After the letters disappeared, when a tone signaled the row.", "Before the letters appeared, when the row was named aloud.", "While the letters were on screen, when the row lit up brightly.", "They were never told and had to report every single row."], "correct": 0, "why": "In partial report, participants heard the tone only after viewing the letters, so they could not know in advance which row would be tested. Reporting every row is the whole report method."}}, "3054": {"college": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report method, accurate report is obtained no matter which row the tone signals. Why does the unpredictability of the cued row matter for interpreting this result?", "options": ["Success on an unpredictable row means information from several rows was available before the tone picked out one.", "Unpredictability means participants rehearse the cued row for a long while before the letter array is displayed.", "Unpredictability shows that the entire letter array is stored permanently after every brief exposure to it.", "Unpredictability means the number of letters recalled is the same in the whole and partial report conditions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Since any row could be cued after the display and still be reported well, most of the array must have been available at that moment. This does not require advance rehearsal or permanent storage."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report method, why did it matter that participants could be asked to report any of the rows?", "options": ["Doing well on any row meant letters from all rows were briefly available.", "It let participants rehearse one chosen row before the display appeared.", "It showed that all the letters were stored permanently in memory.", "It made the partial report and whole report scores come out the same."], "correct": 0, "why": "Because participants could report any row well, letters from the whole display must have been briefly held in sensory memory. The cue came after the display, so no row could be rehearsed in advance."}}, "3055": {"college": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) delayed partial report condition, the partial-report advantage shrinks as the tone is delayed while display time stays fixed. Which variable is changing the chance to select the requested letters?", "options": ["The interval between the end of the letter display and the tone that signals the row.", "The number of letters physically present in the display during the original exposure.", "The length of time that the original letter display stays visible to the participant.", "The sensory modality, visual or auditory, through which the letters themselves are delivered."], "correct": 0, "why": "The delay changes when selection can happen after the same exposure. As it grows, the sensory trace fades before the row is identified, so the advantage shrinks."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sperling's (1960) results, about how long did it take for most of visual sensory memory to fade?", "options": ["About one second after the display disappeared.", "About 15 to 20 seconds after the display disappeared.", "Several minutes after the display disappeared.", "Several hours after the display disappeared."], "correct": 0, "why": "Within about one second, most of sensory memory decays, leaving only what attention moved into short-term memory. About 15 to 20 seconds is the effective duration of short-term memory."}}, "3056": {"college": {"stem": "Someone argues that reporting four or five letters from a briefly flashed array measures exactly how many letters were registered at first. Which finding from Sperling (1960) shows this conclusion is incomplete?", "options": ["An immediately cued partial report recovers most of whichever row is requested after the display ends.", "A longer reporting period gives participants extra time to speak, even though the sensory trace keeps fading.", "The complete array contains more than four or five printed letters before it is ever shown.", "Participants already know that the letters are familiar symbols drawn from their own alphabet."], "correct": 0, "why": "Accurate report of an unpredictable row shows that more was registered than whole report recovers. Whole report is limited because letters fade while they are being named."}, "simple": {"stem": "Before Sperling (1960), many thought visual sensory memory held only 4–5 items. Which of his conditions showed this belief was incomplete?", "options": ["The partial report condition, where people reported most letters of any cued row.", "The whole report condition, where people reported about 4.5 letters in total.", "A condition where people named the letters while they stayed on the screen.", "A condition where people were told the row to report before the letters appeared."], "correct": 0, "why": "Partial report showed that most letters in any row were available, so the whole scene was encoded at first. Whole report produced the 4–5 item estimate; the last two conditions were not part of the study."}}, "3057": {"college": {"stem": "A learner spends ten minutes on a new vocabulary word. In one session they repeat its definition aloud; in another they connect the word to a related word they already know well. Which difference does this contrast capture?", "options": ["The second session uses elaborative rehearsal, while the first session relies mainly on maintenance rehearsal.", "The first session uses elaborative rehearsal, because repetition automatically adds meaningful relationships.", "Both sessions rely only on sensory memory, because the word itself is already quite familiar to the learner.", "The second session blocks long-term learning, because it requires retrieving knowledge already in storage."], "correct": 0, "why": "Connecting a word to existing knowledge is elaborative rehearsal; repeating it is maintenance rehearsal. Drawing on stored knowledge helps rather than blocks transfer to long-term memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which strategy is a good way to transfer information from short-term memory into long-term memory?", "options": ["Elaborative rehearsal, which links new information to existing knowledge.", "Maintenance rehearsal, which repeats information silently over and over.", "Counting backward by threes to keep other thoughts out of the way.", "Letting the information sit in sensory memory for a little longer."], "correct": 0, "why": "Elaborative rehearsal links new information with existing knowledge in long-term memory and is a good way to transfer it there. Maintenance rehearsal only prolongs its presence in short-term memory."}}, "3058": {"college": {"stem": "One group repeats a number throughout a delay, while another performs a task that prevents rehearsal. A researcher attributes any recall difference entirely to how long passive storage lasts. What else has this design changed?", "options": ["Whether a control process is available to keep the information active during the delay.", "The numerical values carried by each of the digits that make up the number.", "The definition of duration, from elapsed time to the number of items remembered.", "Whether the participants receive any information at all before they are asked to recall."], "correct": 0, "why": "Rehearsal is a control process that extends duration, so allowing it in only one group changes what happens during the delay. The difference cannot be read as passive duration alone."}, "simple": {"stem": "In short-term memory, which process seems to extend duration?", "options": ["Rehearsal, which keeps repeating the information over time.", "Chunking, which groups items into larger meaningful groups.", "Interference, which lets old memories compete with new ones.", "Decay, which lets the memory trace fade as time passes."], "correct": 0, "why": "Rehearsal seems to extend duration, while chunking seems to extend capacity. Interference and decay are causes of forgetting."}}, "3059": {"college": {"stem": "In the procedure used by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), participants count backward after receiving three letters. Which participant behavior would defeat the purpose of the retention interval?", "options": ["Silently repeating the letters instead of keeping up the required backward counting.", "Continuing the backward counting steadily until the signal to recall the letters.", "Reporting the letters when the interval ends rather than while they are still visible.", "Receiving filled delays of different lengths on different trials before recall."], "correct": 0, "why": "Counting backward is meant to block rehearsal. Repeating the letters brings back the maintenance rehearsal the procedure is designed to prevent."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the task used by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), what were the steps in order?", "options": ["Get three letters and a number, count backward by 3s for 3–18 seconds, then recall the letters.", "Get three letters, repeat them aloud for 3–18 seconds in silence, then recall the letters.", "Get a number, count forward by 3s, see three letters, then recall the original number.", "Get three letters and a number, recall the letters at once, then count backward by 3s."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants received three letters and a number, counted backward by 3s during a 3–18 second delay, and then recalled the letters. Repeating the letters would have allowed rehearsal, which the task was built to prevent."}}, "3060": {"college": {"stem": "In the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), long-delay recall is good on the first trial but worse after several earlier trials. Which comparison is needed to attribute this difference to trial history rather than to different retention times?", "options": ["Compare early and later trials at the same delay, using the same rehearsal-blocking counting task.", "Compare an early short-delay trial with a later long-delay trial while also changing the counting task.", "Compare one participant copying visible letters with another participant recalling absent letters.", "Compare only the overall average across all delays, setting aside the order in which trials occurred."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding delay and the counting task constant isolates the effect of earlier trials. Changing delay or task, or letting the letters stay visible, adds other explanations, and averaging hides trial order."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did recall after long delays get worse over a series of trials in the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)?", "options": ["Letters from earlier trials interfered with remembering the new letters.", "Participants grew better at counting backward as the trials went on.", "New letters pushed the earliest letters out of long-term memory.", "The delays before recall got much shorter as the trials went on."], "correct": 0, "why": "The pattern reflects proactive interference: letters already learned on earlier trials made the new letters harder to recall."}}, "3061": {"college": {"stem": "In the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the recall curve averaged over all trials declines with delay, but it combines first trials with later ones. Which conclusion takes the trial pattern into account?", "options": ["The average mixes trials with different amounts of built-up interference, so trial position also shapes the explanation.", "The average proves that every participant loses the same number of letters on every trial, purely because time passes.", "Averaging removes interference from the data, leaving a clean estimate of how passive decay unfolds over time.", "Having an average means that recall on the first trial cannot differ from recall on later trials."], "correct": 0, "why": "Later trials carry interference from earlier trigrams, and an average can hide that difference rather than remove it. Forgetting in short-term memory involves both decay and interference."}, "simple": {"stem": "When results were averaged over all trials in the studies by Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), what pattern appeared?", "options": ["Recall was much lower after an 18-second delay than after 3 seconds.", "Recall was about the same after the 3-second and 18-second delays.", "Recall was much higher after an 18-second delay than after 3 seconds.", "Recall was close to zero after both the 3-second and 18-second delays."], "correct": 0, "why": "Averaged over all trials, recall dropped sharply from the 3-second to the 18-second delay. Looking trial by trial showed the drop grew as the series went on."}}, "3062": {"college": {"stem": "After switching to a new phone number, a person has trouble recalling their old one. Which kind of interference does this show?", "options": ["Retroactive interference, because newer information is disrupting memory for older information.", "Proactive interference, because any interference involving a new number receives that label.", "Sensory persistence, because the old number is still physically in view for the person.", "Elaborative rehearsal, because the two numbers have been permanently merged into one memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "The target is the old number and the interfering information is newer, which defines retroactive interference. Proactive interference runs the other way, with old information disrupting new."}, "simple": {"stem": "You get a new locker combination and now can't remember your old one. What is this?", "options": ["Retroactive interference, where new information blocks old.", "Proactive interference, where old information blocks new information.", "Decay, where the old memory simply faded away with time.", "Chunking, where the two combinations merged into one memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "A new memory interfering with an old one is retroactive interference. Proactive interference would be the old combination making the new one hard to remember."}}, "3063": {"college": {"stem": "A commuter parks in a different spot in the same campus lot every weekday. On Friday afternoon, they keep recalling Thursday's spot instead of Friday's. Which account identifies the target memory and the interference?", "options": ["Friday's spot is the target memory, and the older spots from earlier days produce proactive interference.", "Thursday's spot is the target memory, and the newer spot from Friday produces retroactive interference.", "Friday's spot is the target memory, and sensory persistence keeps the older spots permanently in view.", "Parking is a procedural skill, so the order in which the spots were learned does not matter at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "The commuter wants the newer memory, and older similar memories compete with it, which is proactive interference. Routine events like daily parking are especially prone to this."}, "simple": {"stem": "Proactive and retroactive interference are worst for which kind of events?", "options": ["Routine events that happen in much the same way again and again.", "Unusual events that happen only once and stand out from the rest.", "Emotional events that happened long ago in early childhood.", "Events that happened less than one second ago."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both kinds of interference are worst for routine events. Unusual events are less prone to interference."}}, "3064": {"college": {"stem": "Interference theories hold that forgetting comes from memories competing, not from the mere passage of time. Which statement correctly contrasts retroactive and proactive interference?", "options": ["In retroactive interference new information disrupts old; in proactive interference old information disrupts new.", "In retroactive interference old information disrupts new; in proactive interference new information disrupts old.", "In retroactive interference longer rehearsal helps recall; in proactive interference shorter rehearsal helps recall.", "In retroactive interference visual traces turn auditory; in proactive interference auditory traces turn visual."], "correct": 0, "why": "The two types differ in whether the competing memory came after or before the target memory. They are not about rehearsal length or sensory modality."}, "simple": {"stem": "In interference theories, what causes forgetting?", "options": ["One memory competing with or replacing another memory.", "The mere passage of time causing the memory to fade.", "Damage to the brain areas that store older memories.", "Repeating information too many times during rehearsal."], "correct": 0, "why": "Interference theories say forgetting is caused by one memory competing with or replacing another, not by the mere passage of time."}}, "3065": {"college": {"stem": "A participant reproduces every digit of an eight-digit sequence but reverses two of them, after reproducing a seven-digit sequence perfectly in order. Under standard digit-span scoring, which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["The eight-digit trial counts as wrong under ordered scoring, so recalling its digits does not give a span of eight.", "The eight-digit trial counts as correct, because only the identity of the digits matters and their order is ignored.", "The participant has demonstrated an unlimited span, because every single digit in both of the sequences is a familiar symbol.", "The seven-digit trial cannot count, because shorter sequences never contribute anything to a span estimate."], "correct": 0, "why": "Digit span is the longest row recalled completely correctly, which requires the right order. Getting the digits without the order does not make the eight-digit row correct, so the span here is seven."}, "simple": {"stem": "Maria recalls the 6-digit row perfectly but mixes up two digits in the 7-digit row. What is her digit span?", "options": ["Six, since that is her longest row recalled completely right.", "Seven, since she remembered all the digits in that row.", "Thirteen, since that is the total number of digits in both rows.", "Zero, since she made at least one mistake somewhere overall."], "correct": 0, "why": "Digit span is the number of digits in the longest row recalled completely correctly. The 7-digit row had an error, so her span is six."}}, "3066": {"college": {"stem": "Miller's (1956) estimate of seven, plus or minus two, is compared with a visual change-detection estimate of about three or four items. Which interpretation is most defensible?", "options": ["The estimates come from different tasks and kinds of items, so one number should not be applied to every measure.", "The visual result is impossible, because Miller established a limit of exactly seven raw elements in every memory task.", "The digit estimate measures visual sensory persistence, while every visual task measures permanent long-term storage.", "The two estimates must be averaged together to obtain the single correct capacity that applies to every participant and every kind of task."], "correct": 0, "why": "Visual short-term memory measured by change detection may have a smaller capacity than memory for digits or words, and theorists debate how many specific forms of short-term memory exist. Neither estimate is sensory persistence or permanent storage."}, "simple": {"stem": "How does visual short-term memory capacity from change detection compare with Miller's (1956) 7 ± 2?", "options": ["It is smaller, about three to four items, than for digit lists.", "It is larger, about twelve items, than memory for digit lists.", "It is exactly the same, about seven items, as for digit lists.", "It cannot be measured at all, because visual items fade too fast."], "correct": 0, "why": "Change detection suggests visual short-term memory holds about three to four items, smaller than the 7 ± 2 estimate for digits or words."}}, "3067": {"college": {"stem": "A visual change-detection task shows display S1 for 100 ms, removes it for 900 ms, and then shows display S2. Which change would make the task a weaker test of short-term visual retention?", "options": ["Keeping S1 on screen beside S2, so participants can compare the two displays directly.", "Removing S1 before S2 appears and requiring a same-or-different judgment about them.", "Holding the exposure time of S1 constant while comparing displays of different sizes.", "Using the same relevant visual features, such as color, in both the first and second displays."], "correct": 0, "why": "If both displays are visible together, participants can compare them by looking rather than by holding S1 in memory. The original task requires keeping S1 in mind across the blank gap."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a change-detection task, what does the participant do?", "options": ["Sees a display, then after a gap judges if a second display is the same.", "Sees a display and then names every item in it aloud from memory.", "Sees two displays side by side and points out every difference between the two of them.", "Hears a row of digits and repeats them back in the original order."], "correct": 0, "why": "In change detection, a first display is shown briefly, removed, and followed by a second display; the participant judges same or different. Repeating digits in order is a digit-span task."}}, "3068": {"college": {"stem": "Change-detection accuracy falls sharply as display size grows beyond about four colored squares. What is the most appropriate interpretation for visual short-term memory?", "options": ["Holding the needed visual information becomes less successful once the display exceeds a limited capacity.", "All objects are retained permanently, and larger displays remove any need to compare the two displays.", "Participants lose the ability to see any of the colors once a display contains more than four separate items.", "Every kind of short-term memory task must have exactly the same limit of four items as this one."], "correct": 0, "why": "The drop reflects limited visual short-term memory, about three to four items. It does not mean participants cannot see the colors, that storage is permanent, or that every task has the same limit."}, "simple": {"stem": "In change-detection studies, what happened to accuracy when displays had more than four items?", "options": ["Accuracy dropped off sharply as more items were added.", "Accuracy stayed near perfect no matter how many items there were.", "Accuracy rose steadily as more and more items were added.", "Accuracy fell to zero as soon as a fifth item appeared."], "correct": 0, "why": "Accuracy fell off dramatically beyond four items, suggesting people can hold only about three to four items in visual short-term memory. It dropped but did not reach zero."}}, "3069": {"college": {"stem": "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) compared change detection for simple colored squares and more complex objects such as shaded cubes. Which result supports measuring capacity by amount of information rather than by number of items?", "options": ["Estimated capacity was higher for simple colored squares than for complex objects such as shaded cubes.", "Every object category gave exactly the same capacity estimate, whatever visual features had to be retained.", "Participants named every object while both displays stayed visible on the screen with no delay.", "The verbal label for an object set its capacity even when its visual features were never tested."], "correct": 0, "why": "Complex objects carry more information, and capacity was lower for them than for colored squares. If every item counted equally, capacity would not vary by object type."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) find about object complexity and visual short-term memory?", "options": ["The more complex the objects, the fewer could be held in memory.", "The more complex the objects, the more could be held in memory.", "Object complexity had no effect on how many could be held in memory.", "Only colored squares could be held; complex objects could not be held at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Capacity was highest for colored squares and lowest for shaded cubes, so complex objects reduced how many could be held. Complex objects still had a capacity above zero."}}, "3070": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to test, in the manner of Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004), whether stimulus complexity affects visual-memory capacity. Which comparison most directly does this?", "options": ["Compare change detection for simple and complex objects while keeping exposure, delay, and response demands comparable.", "Compare simple objects shown for a long time with complex objects shown briefly, and change the response instructions too.", "Ask one group to remember digits and another group to report whether the testing room is brightly lit.", "Keep complex objects visible throughout the response and compare them with simple objects that are removed."], "correct": 0, "why": "The relevant manipulation is object complexity under the same memory demands. Changing exposure, instructions, or visibility along with complexity creates other explanations."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Alvarez and Cavanagh's (2004) study, which kind of object had the lowest capacity?", "options": ["Shaded cubes, the most complex objects that were tested.", "Colored squares, the simplest objects that were tested.", "Chinese characters, the written symbols that were tested.", "Random polygons, the irregular shapes that were tested."], "correct": 0, "why": "Capacity was lowest for shaded cubes (under two items) and highest for colored squares (over four). Chinese characters and random polygons fell in between."}}, "3071": {"college": {"stem": "Ericsson and colleagues (1980) trained a student to reach a very large digit span using familiar running times. What is the basis of this gain?", "options": ["Knowledge-based chunking, which combines smaller units into larger meaningful units the student could hold.", "A growth in raw storage capacity that becomes equally unlimited for every unfamiliar kind of information.", "Replacing all long-term knowledge with a brief, unstructured sensory trace of the digits exactly as they appeared.", "Doing away with any representation of the original sequence when recalling the digits in order."], "correct": 0, "why": "Familiar running times let the student combine digits into larger meaningful chunks. This is chunking with existing knowledge, not a general expansion of every basic memory limit."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is chunking?", "options": ["Combining smaller units into larger meaningful units to hold more in memory.", "Repeating information over and over to keep it in short-term memory.", "Breaking a memory into pieces so that it can be forgotten more easily.", "Moving information from sensory memory straight into long-term memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "Chunking is combining smaller units into larger meaningful units, which lets more fit in short-term memory. Repeating information is maintenance rehearsal."}}, "3072": {"college": {"stem": "Chase and Simon (1973) compared chess masters and beginners recalling meaningful game positions and randomized positions. Which pattern supports expertise-based chunking?", "options": ["Masters had a large advantage for meaningful positions, which disappeared for random arrangements.", "Masters showed the same large advantage for random positions, regardless of any familiar relationships among pieces.", "Beginners outperformed masters, but only when both groups copied the pieces while the board stayed visible.", "Randomizing positions improved masters' recall, because familiar structure normally interferes with their memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "Masters use learned chunks from meaningful positions. Random arrangements keep the pieces but destroy the familiar patterns, and the masters' advantage vanished."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find when chess masters and beginners recalled pieces from meaningful game positions?", "options": ["Masters placed many more pieces correctly than beginners.", "Masters and beginners placed about the same number correctly.", "Beginners placed many more pieces correctly than masters.", "Neither group could place more than one or two pieces."], "correct": 0, "why": "For meaningful positions, masters placed far more pieces correctly because they chunk the board into meaningful patterns. This advantage did not appear for random arrangements."}}, "3073": {"college": {"stem": "In Chase and Simon's (1973) study, why are random chess arrangements useful for evaluating the claim that masters simply have a larger raw visual store?", "options": ["They keep the same kinds of pieces but break up the familiar patterns that support expert chunks.", "They allow the masters to rehearse meaningful positions for a longer time than the beginners can.", "They remove all visual information from the board and turn the task into a form of auditory recall.", "They guarantee that both groups know the same number of chess strategies before the test begins."], "correct": 0, "why": "If masters simply had a bigger raw store, they should excel with random arrangements too. Their advantage disappearing for random boards shows it depends on meaningful structure."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did Chase and Simon (1973) also test random chess arrangements?", "options": ["To see if masters' advantage depended on meaningful patterns.", "To give the beginners more time to study the chess pieces.", "To check whether masters could see the board more clearly.", "To find out how many chess rules each beginner already knew well."], "correct": 0, "why": "Random arrangements remove the meaningful patterns masters can chunk. Because masters lost their advantage, their skill came from chunking, not a bigger memory store."}}, "3074": {"college": {"stem": "A learner groups a long sequence into meaningful units and also keeps repeating those units during a delay. Which pairing correctly distinguishes what each control process contributes?", "options": ["Chunking packs more into each item held, while rehearsal prolongs how long the units stay available.", "Chunking measures how much time has passed, while rehearsal permanently removes all capacity limits.", "Both processes show that sensory memory retains every original detail of the sequence indefinitely.", "Rehearsal changes the physical input itself, while chunking stops long-term knowledge from contributing."], "correct": 0, "why": "Chunking seems to extend capacity by organizing information, and rehearsal seems to extend duration by maintaining it. Neither implies unlimited storage or lasting sensory traces."}, "simple": {"stem": "To remember 1-9-9-4-2-0-2-6, you group it as the years 1994 and 2026 and keep repeating them. What are you doing?", "options": ["Chunking to fit more in memory, and rehearsal to keep it there longer.", "Rehearsal to fit more in memory, and chunking to keep it there longer.", "Using sensory memory to hold an exact copy for many minutes.", "Using proactive interference to block out the earlier digits."], "correct": 0, "why": "Grouping digits into meaningful years is chunking, which extends capacity; repeating them is rehearsal, which extends duration. The second option swaps their roles."}}, "3075": {"college": {"stem": "A person pictures a familiar house and places each item to be remembered in a different room, then deliberately directs attention to each room while recalling. Which concept covers these active operations?", "options": ["Control processes, which a person uses to organize and manage information being remembered.", "A view of temporary memory as purely passive storage that no strategy is able to influence.", "An automatic guarantee that every sensory detail of the house and its rooms will enter permanent memory.", "Retroactive interference, which applies whenever information is tied to a spatial layout."], "correct": 0, "why": "The memory palace is a strategy for making information more memorable, and directing attention is an attention strategy; both are control processes. They concern how information is managed, not passive retention."}, "simple": {"stem": "Building a “memory palace” is an ancient strategy for making information more memorable. What kind of process is it?", "options": ["A control process that the person actively chooses to use.", "An automatic sensory process that the person cannot control.", "A kind of retroactive interference between new and old items.", "A form of decay that happens as more time passes."], "correct": 0, "why": "A memory palace is a strategy the person chooses, making it a control process. Control processes include rehearsal, memory strategies, and attention strategies."}}, "3101": {"college": {"stem": "Baddeley and Hitch (1974) proposed working memory because a purely passive short-term store seemed too simple. A person keeps an intermediate result in mind while carrying out the next step of a calculation. Which feature of their proposal best accounts for this?", "options": ["It combines temporary storage with active manipulation of information within a limited capacity.", "It treats short-term memory as one passive store that needs no control processes at all.", "It assigns every calculation to long-term memory and leaves only sensory traces in temporary storage.", "It adds separate stores that remove any need to direct attention during complex tasks."], "correct": 0, "why": "Working memory is a limited-capacity system for both temporary storage and manipulation, which is what holding a result while working on the next step requires. A purely passive store, the older short-term memory view, cannot explain the active operations."}, "simple": {"stem": "How did Baddeley and Hitch (1974) define working memory?", "options": ["A limited-capacity system that both stores and manipulates information for complex tasks.", "A passive store that only holds information briefly, with no control processes.", "A permanent store for all the facts and skills a person has learned.", "A sensory store that keeps an exact copy of whatever was just seen."], "correct": 0, "why": "Working memory is a limited-capacity system for temporary storage and manipulation of information during tasks such as comprehension, learning and reasoning. The passive-store idea is the simpler short-term memory view it replaced."}}, "3102": {"college": {"stem": "To multiply 43 by 6 mentally, a person computes 3 × 6 = 18, holds the 8, carries the 1, computes 6 × 4 = 24, adds the carried 1 to get 25, and combines 25 with the held 8 to reach 258. Which statement correctly separates storage from processing in this sequence?", "options": ["Holding the 8 is storage, while carrying the 1 and adding it to 24 are active processing.", "Carrying the 1 is passive storage, while holding the 8 is the only active processing step.", "Only the final answer uses working memory, while every intermediate result goes to long-term memory.", "Visualizing the digits rules out verbal processing, so the calculation needs no executive control."], "correct": 0, "why": "The 8 simply has to stay available while other steps happen, which is storage; carrying, multiplying and adding change the information, which is active processing. The second option reverses the two roles."}, "simple": {"stem": "When multiplying 43 by 6 in your head, which step is pure storage rather than active processing?", "options": ["Holding the 8 in mind while working on the tens digit.", "Carrying the 1 over so it can be added to the next product.", "Multiplying 6 by 4 to get 24 in the second step.", "Adding the carried 1 to 24 to get a total of 25."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding the 8 only keeps a value available until it is needed, so it is storage. Carrying, multiplying and adding all change the numbers, so they are active processes."}}, "3103": {"college": {"stem": "A student claims that Baddeley and Hitch's (1974) original working-memory model had four parts: a central executive, a phonological loop, a visuospatial sketchpad and an episodic buffer. Which correction is accurate?", "options": ["The first three parts were in the 1974 model, and Baddeley added the episodic buffer in 2000.", "The episodic buffer was in the 1974 model, and the central executive was added later in 2000.", "The phonological loop replaced the visuospatial sketchpad in 2000 instead of working alongside it.", "All four parts are different names for the single short-term store of the modal model."], "correct": 0, "why": "The 1974 model had three components (central executive, visuospatial sketchpad, phonological loop); Baddeley added the episodic buffer as a fourth in 2000. The loop and sketchpad are separate, coexisting stores, not replacements or synonyms."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which component did Baddeley add in 2000 to the working-memory model of Baddeley and Hitch (1974)?", "options": ["The episodic buffer, which binds information into integrated episodes.", "The central executive, which directs and maintains attention.", "The phonological loop, which holds verbal and auditory information.", "The visuospatial sketchpad, which holds visual and spatial information."], "correct": 0, "why": "The episodic buffer was added in 2000 as a fourth component. The central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad were all part of the original 1974 model."}}, "3104": {"college": {"stem": "The phonological loop has two parts, the phonological store and the articulatory rehearsal process. Which statement correctly describes the division of labor between them?", "options": ["The store holds verbal and auditory information, and rehearsal refreshes it so it does not decay.", "The store holds visual locations, and rehearsal binds a meaningful story into one integrated episode.", "The store suppresses irrelevant information, and rehearsal holds all long-term knowledge of language.", "The store repeats speech aloud, and rehearsal permanently fixes the meaning of each repeated item."], "correct": 0, "why": "The phonological store holds verbal and auditory information, and articulatory rehearsal keeps those items from decaying. Suppressing irrelevant information belongs to the central executive, and binding episodes belongs to the episodic buffer."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the articulatory rehearsal process do in the phonological loop?", "options": ["It repeats verbal information so it does not decay from the phonological store.", "It holds visual and spatial information such as shapes and locations.", "It moves every repeated word permanently into long-term memory.", "It directs attention and coordinates all the other working-memory components."], "correct": 0, "why": "Articulatory rehearsal repeats verbal items to keep them in the phonological store. Holding visual information is the sketchpad's job, and directing attention is the central executive's."}}, "3105": {"college": {"stem": "A person silently repeats a short list of words while also keeping in mind the appearance and layout of a shape. How should these two kinds of temporary information be assigned to working-memory components?", "options": ["The repeated words use the phonological loop, and the shape and its layout use the visuospatial sketchpad.", "Both sit in the phonological store, because everything a person is aware of is coded verbally.", "The shape uses the phonological loop, while the silently repeated words are held on the visuospatial sketchpad.", "Both belong to the central executive, because the temporary stores hold only sounds heard aloud."], "correct": 0, "why": "Verbal and auditory information is held in the phonological loop, and visual and spatial information in the visuospatial sketchpad. Silent rehearsal still uses the loop, and the central executive coordinates rather than stores every kind of content."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which working-memory component holds visual and spatial information, such as the shape and location of objects?", "options": ["The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information.", "The phonological loop holds visual and spatial information.", "The episodic buffer holds only visual and spatial information.", "The central executive is the main store for visual shapes and locations."], "correct": 0, "why": "The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information. The phonological loop holds verbal and auditory information, and the central executive mainly directs attention and coordinates the stores."}}, "3106": {"college": {"stem": "In Baddeley and Hitch's (1974) working-memory diagram, the central executive exchanges information with long-term knowledge in both directions, and the phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad are also linked to it. A person solving a familiar sum combines a remembered arithmetic fact with digits currently held in mind. What do these links imply?", "options": ["Previously learned information can be brought in and coordinated with temporary representations during the task.", "Long-term knowledge can contribute only after all temporary processing on the task has finished.", "Having a learned fact available makes the loop and the sketchpad unnecessary for any calculation.", "Retrieving a familiar fact turns the central executive into a permanent storage site for that fact."], "correct": 0, "why": "The model links working-memory components with long-term knowledge, and the central executive pulls information from long-term memory while a task is under way. Using a known fact does not remove the need for temporary maintenance or make the executive a permanent store."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Baddeley and Hitch's (1974) model, how is working memory related to long-term knowledge?", "options": ["Working memory can pull in and use information from long-term knowledge.", "Working memory has no connection at all to long-term knowledge.", "Long-term knowledge replaces working memory once a task becomes familiar.", "Long-term knowledge is stored inside the phonological loop itself."], "correct": 0, "why": "The central executive pulls information from long-term memory, and the model's components are linked to long-term knowledge. Working memory is not cut off from long-term knowledge, nor replaced by it."}}, "3107": {"college": {"stem": "In Conrad's (1964) study, letters were flashed briefly and participants then wrote them down. Errors tended to swap E with B rather than with F. Why is this error pattern informative?", "options": ["The letters were held partly by sound, so a similar-sounding name competed despite a different shape.", "The letters were heard rather than seen, so the errors simply reflect mishearing of the original items.", "The most similar written shapes were confused, which supports a purely visual code for the letters.", "Every letter was already stored permanently, and handwriting habits decided which letter was written."], "correct": 0, "why": "E and B have similar spoken names, while E and F look alike. Sound-based errors after purely visual presentation show the letters were recoded and held by sound, not by a visual code alone."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Conrad's (1964) study of briefly flashed letters, what kind of mistakes did participants usually make?", "options": ["They confused letters that sound alike, such as E and B.", "They confused letters that look alike, such as E and F.", "They left out letters at random, with no pattern to their errors.", "They wrote the right letters but always in reverse order."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants rarely replaced a letter with one that looked like it but often replaced it with one that sounded like it. This is the phonological similarity effect, even though the letters were seen rather than heard."}}, "3108": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher extends Conrad's (1964) flashed-letter procedure with two letter sets: one whose names sound alike but whose shapes differ, and one whose names sound distinct. Display time and recall instructions are the same for both. Which outcome would most directly support a sound-based code in memory?", "options": ["More sound-based substitutions occur for the similar-sounding set, even with viewing conditions matched.", "Whichever set is displayed for longer is remembered better, even though the amount of sound similarity stays the same.", "Participants copy both sets accurately because the letters stay visible during the whole response.", "Errors follow only how alike the written shapes are and never how the letters are pronounced."], "correct": 0, "why": "The key manipulation is sound similarity with presentation conditions held constant, so extra sound-based errors for that set point to a phonological code. A display-time difference would be a confound, copying visible letters involves no memory, and shape-only errors would indicate a visual code."}, "simple": {"stem": "Conrad (1964) compared memory for letters that sound alike with letters that do not. Which result shows that people hold letters by their sound?", "options": ["More errors happen with the similar-sounding letters than with the distinct ones.", "Equal numbers of errors happen with both sets of letters.", "More errors happen only with letters that have similar shapes.", "No errors happen with either set when letters are flashed."], "correct": 0, "why": "If letters are held by sound, letters with similar names should be confused more often. Equal errors, or errors driven by shape, would not point to a sound-based code."}}, "3109": {"college": {"stem": "One student argues that the phonological loop is involved only when letters are heard, not when they are seen. Which finding from Conrad's (1964) study most directly challenges this claim?", "options": ["Visually flashed letters were confused with letters that have similar spoken names, not mainly similar shapes.", "Participants could identify a visible letter but could not keep it once the display had disappeared.", "Letters that are seen and letters that are heard reach the senses in physically different forms.", "Letters with different written shapes sometimes received different written responses from the participants in the study."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sound-based confusions occurred even though the letters were shown visually, so the phonological loop must have converted the visual input into sound. Input modality and the code used to hold information can differ."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Conrad's (1964) study show about letters that are seen rather than heard?", "options": ["Seen letters are still converted into sounds by the phonological loop.", "Seen letters are stored only as visual shapes in the sketchpad.", "Seen letters cannot be remembered once the display has disappeared.", "Seen letters are confused only with other letters that look similar to them."], "correct": 0, "why": "Even visually presented letters were confused by sound, showing the phonological loop converts what is seen into a sound code. If they were held only as shapes, look-alike errors would dominate."}}, "3110": {"college": {"stem": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) found that lists of short words are recalled better than lists of long words. A student explains this by the shorter time needed to rehearse short words. Which observation fits that explanation?", "options": ["Fewer long words than short words can be repeated within the brief time available for refreshing a list.", "Long words always carry less meaning than short words, so they cannot enter working memory in the first place.", "Short words bypass the phonological store and are held only as visual shapes on the sketchpad.", "Recall of long words depends only on how many letters are visible, regardless of pronunciation."], "correct": 0, "why": "Long words take longer to rehearse and to say during recall, so fewer fit into the time available for refreshing the list. The account is about pronunciation time, not meaning, visual storage or letter count."}, "simple": {"stem": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) described the word-length effect. What is it?", "options": ["People remember more short words than long words from a list.", "People remember more long words than short words from a list.", "People remember words better when someone else reads the list aloud.", "People remember the same number of words whatever their length."], "correct": 0, "why": "In the word-length effect, memory for lists is better for short words than for long words, because long words take longer to rehearse and to produce. The reverse pattern and a no-difference pattern are both wrong."}}, "3111": {"college": {"stem": "Ellis and Hennelly (1980) found that Welsh–English bilingual children have a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English, although both tasks require remembering digits in order. Which interpretation explains the difference?", "options": ["Welsh number names take longer to say, so fewer of them can be rehearsed within a brief interval.", "The children have two fixed numerical capacities that are unrelated to the names used for the digits.", "The English task measures visual memory, whereas the Welsh task measures only long-term knowledge.", "The digits have different numerical values in the two languages, which changes how many are presented."], "correct": 0, "why": "Welsh number words take longer to pronounce, so fewer can be rehearsed in the available time, which shortens span. Both tasks remain verbal digit recall, and the digits themselves do not change value."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did Welsh–English bilingual children have a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English (Ellis & Hennelly, 1980)?", "options": ["Welsh number words take longer to pronounce than English ones.", "The children had learned English numbers earlier than Welsh ones.", "Welsh number words look more alike when they are written down.", "The Welsh test used larger numbers than the English test did."], "correct": 0, "why": "Welsh numbers take longer to say, so fewer fit into rehearsal, which is the word-length effect applied to digits. The explanation is pronunciation time, not order of learning, spelling or number size."}}, "3112": {"college": {"stem": "A student hears that a person's digit span is likely to equal the number of words they can say in 1.5–2.0 seconds and concludes that everyone's span is exactly two words. Which revision states the relationship correctly?", "options": ["What matters is how many items can be spoken in that interval, so slower-to-say items reduce span.", "The interval sets a fixed capacity of two items, no matter how quickly the items can be spoken.", "The interval is the time needed to move every remembered word into permanent long-term storage.", "The relationship holds only for visual shapes, because the spoken forms of items have no effect on digit span."], "correct": 0, "why": "The time window limits how much can be rehearsed, not a fixed count of items: short, quickly spoken items fit more often than long ones. That is why word length and number-name duration change span."}, "simple": {"stem": "A person's digit span is likely to equal the number of words they can say in about 1.5–2.0 seconds. What follows from this?", "options": ["Words that take longer to say will lower how many can be remembered.", "Everyone can remember exactly two words, regardless of word length.", "How fast a person speaks has no effect on how many words can be remembered.", "Only visual items, not spoken words, can be held for about 1.5–2.0 seconds."], "correct": 0, "why": "If span is set by how much can be said in a fixed time, slower-to-say words mean fewer items fit. The interval is a time limit, not a fixed two-word limit."}}, "3113": {"college": {"stem": "Participants memorize lists of short and long words either in silence or while speaking continuously (articulatory suppression). Which pattern of results is expected?", "options": ["Speaking lowers overall recall and shrinks the usual recall advantage for short words.", "Speaking raises overall recall and enlarges the short-word advantage by adding extra rehearsal.", "Speaking leaves recall unchanged because producing speech draws only on the visuospatial sketchpad.", "Speaking changes only how the final answers are pronounced and cannot affect maintenance of the list."], "correct": 0, "why": "Speaking occupies the articulatory rehearsal process, so recall gets worse and the word-length effect is reduced. Speech uses the phonological loop, not the sketchpad, and it does not add useful rehearsal."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is articulatory suppression, and what does it do to memory?", "options": ["Speaking while memorizing, which keeps the loop busy and makes recall worse.", "Speaking while memorizing, which adds extra rehearsal and makes recall better.", "Staying silent while memorizing, which blocks the sketchpad and makes recall worse.", "Pointing while memorizing, which frees the loop and makes recall better."], "correct": 0, "why": "Articulatory suppression means speaking while memorizing; it keeps the phonological loop busy, so rehearsal is disrupted and memory is reduced. It does not help rehearsal."}}, "3114": {"college": {"stem": "Visually presented letter lists normally produce confusions between similar-sounding letters. When participants speak while memorizing, this sound-similarity difference becomes smaller, yet overall recall worsens. Which explanation accounts for both changes?", "options": ["Speaking occupies rehearsal and the recoding of letters into sound, lowering recall and the similarity effect.", "Speaking increases memory capacity but leads participants to report fewer letters on purpose.", "The smaller similarity effect shows that the visible letters never reached the senses in the first place.", "Speaking makes the phonological store more accurate while leaving the rehearsal process fully free."], "correct": 0, "why": "Articulatory suppression keeps the phonological loop busy, so letters are rehearsed and converted into sound less well; recall drops and sound-based confusions matter less. A smaller effect here reflects a disrupted process, not better memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "What happens to the phonological similarity effect when people speak while memorizing letters?", "options": ["The effect gets smaller because the phonological loop is kept busy.", "The effect gets larger because speaking adds sound-based rehearsal.", "The effect stays the same because speaking uses only the sketchpad.", "The effect reverses, so similar-looking letters are confused instead."], "correct": 0, "why": "Articulatory suppression reduces both the phonological similarity effect and the word-length effect because the loop is occupied by speaking. Speaking does not add useful rehearsal or rely on the sketchpad."}}, "3115": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to test whether the recall advantage for short words depends on articulatory rehearsal. Which design addresses that question most directly while keeping the word lists comparable?", "options": ["Cross word length with the presence or absence of a concurrent speaking task, using matched presentation.", "Show long words for longer and short words for shorter durations, then compare recall of the two resulting lists.", "Compare one group recalling word lists with a different group judging whether shapes are rotated.", "Measure pronunciation speed in one language and use another group's visual accuracy as digit span."], "correct": 0, "why": "Crossing word length with suppression tests whether the short-word advantage shrinks when rehearsal is blocked. The other designs add exposure-time confounds, unrelated tasks or different participant groups."}, "simple": {"stem": "To test whether the word-length effect depends on rehearsal, what should a researcher compare?", "options": ["Short and long word lists, each learned with and without speaking aloud.", "Short word lists only, each learned both with and without speaking aloud.", "Long word lists shown briefly and short word lists shown for longer.", "Word lists learned by one group and shapes learned by another group."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both word lengths are needed to measure the word-length effect, and the speaking manipulation shows whether that effect depends on rehearsal. Using one length only, or unequal display times, cannot answer the question."}}, "3116": {"college": {"stem": "Which pairing correctly matches each working-memory component with the kind of content it maintains?", "options": ["The sketchpad maintains an object's shape and layout, while the loop maintains the sounds of a word list.", "The sketchpad directs all of attention, while the loop maintains an object's visual shape and spatial layout.", "The sketchpad maintains the sounds of a word list, while the loop suppresses irrelevant responses.", "The sketchpad binds a story with knowledge, while the loop stores that same story permanently."], "correct": 0, "why": "The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information, and the phonological loop holds verbal and auditory information. Directing attention and suppressing responses are executive functions, and binding with knowledge is the episodic buffer's role."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which mental activity relies most on the visuospatial sketchpad?", "options": ["Forming a visual image of an object in your mind.", "Silently repeating a phone number to keep it in mind.", "Switching attention back and forth between two tasks.", "Recalling the meaning of a word learned years ago."], "correct": 0, "why": "The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information and is involved in visual imagery. Repeating a number uses the phonological loop, and switching attention is a central executive function."}}, "3117": {"college": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) sentence experiment, participants memorized a sentence and then indicated for each word whether it was a noun. Why was pointing easier than speaking these judgments?", "options": ["The sentence occupied the phonological loop, so spoken answers competed with it while pointing did not.", "Pointing rehearsed the sounds of the sentence more effectively than any spoken response could.", "The sentence was stored only as a spatial outline on the sketchpad, which made spoken answers impossible to produce.", "Speaking is harder than pointing in every task, whatever information is being remembered."], "correct": 0, "why": "The phonological loop was busy with the sentence, so spoken responses competed for it, while the sketchpad was free for pointing. The F task shows speaking is not harder in general."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) sentence experiment, which way of responding was easier, and why?", "options": ["Pointing, because the phonological loop was busy with the sentence.", "Speaking, because the visuospatial sketchpad was busy with the sentence.", "Pointing, because the sketchpad was busy with the sentence.", "Speaking, because the phonological loop was free during the task."], "correct": 0, "why": "Pointing was easier because the sentence kept the phonological loop busy, leaving the sketchpad free for a pointing response. A sentence is verbal, so it does not occupy the sketchpad."}}, "3118": {"college": {"stem": "A research team repeats Brooks's (1968) sentence task with the same sentences and the same noun judgments, changing only whether answers are spoken or given by pointing. What does this comparison test?", "options": ["Whether changing the response mode changes interference with the same verbal memory load.", "Whether sentences with easier meanings always increase the number of stored visual objects.", "Whether the noun judgments disappear when participants are given a longer retention interval.", "Whether participants become more accurate because they can look back at the sentence."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding the sentence and the judgment constant isolates the effect of response mode on interference. The design does not vary meaning or delay, and the sentence is held in memory rather than visible."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) sentence experiment, what was the one thing that differed between the two conditions?", "options": ["Whether participants gave their answers by speaking or by pointing.", "Whether participants memorized a sentence or memorized a shape.", "Whether the sentence was displayed for a short or a long time.", "Whether participants judged the nouns or the verbs in the sentence."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both conditions used a memorized sentence and noun judgments; only the response changed (speaking versus pointing). The sentence-versus-shape contrast comes from comparing this study with the F task."}}, "3119": {"college": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) F task, participants held an F-shaped outline in mind and judged each successive corner as an inside or an outside corner. Which statement gives the result and its explanation?", "options": ["Speaking was easier, because pointing added a spatial demand while the sketchpad was busy with the shape.", "Pointing was easier, because the shape occupied the phonological loop and left spatial processing unused.", "Speaking was easier, because the shape and the spoken labels were both held in one verbal component.", "Pointing was easier, because it removed the need to remember the shape before judging the corners."], "correct": 0, "why": "The remembered F occupied the visuospatial sketchpad, so pointing competed with it while speaking could use the free phonological loop. This is the opposite of the sentence result."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) F task, which way of responding was easier, and why?", "options": ["Speaking, because the sketchpad was busy with the image of the F.", "Pointing, because the sketchpad was busy with the image of the F.", "Speaking, because the phonological loop was busy with the image of the F.", "Pointing, because the phonological loop was free during the task."], "correct": 0, "why": "Speaking was easier because the image of the F kept the sketchpad busy, while the phonological loop was free for spoken answers. A visual image does not occupy the phonological loop."}}, "3120": {"college": {"stem": "In the pointing condition of Brooks's (1968) F task, participants pointed to OUT or IN on a response sheet for each corner of the remembered outline. A student assumes the sheet already shows the correct answer for each corner. Which description of the task is accurate?", "options": ["OUT and IN are only response choices, and each corner must be judged from the shape held in mind.", "Each row states the correct answer, so the participant can finish without remembering the shape.", "The sheet turns the F into a verbal sentence, which removes the visual and spatial memory demand.", "Each response refers to a newly displayed corner, so there is no remembered outline to follow."], "correct": 0, "why": "Every row offers the same two alternatives, OUT and IN, as places to point; the answer has to come from mentally tracing the remembered F. The sheet adds a spatial response demand rather than giving the solution."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Brooks's (1968) F task, what did participants have to do for each corner of the F?", "options": ["Judge from memory whether it was an inside or an outside corner.", "Copy the corner while the F stayed visible in front of them.", "Name aloud the letter that the corner looked most like.", "Count how many corners the F had before giving any answer at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants memorized the F and then indicated, corner by corner, whether each was an inside or outside corner. The F was held in mind, not copied from view."}}, "3121": {"college": {"stem": "Brooks (1968) found pointing easier when a sentence was being remembered but speaking easier when an F shape was being remembered. Which conclusion accounts for this reversal?", "options": ["Interference depends on whether the response and the remembered information compete for the same verbal or visuospatial resources.", "Pointing is easier in every memory task, but participants reversed the instructions for the shape task.", "A single passive store is disrupted to the same degree by every possible way of making a response.", "The response advantage depends entirely on whether the correct answer for each item is yes or no."], "correct": 0, "why": "Tasks are easier when the held information and the response use different types of short-term memory, which is why the advantage flips with the type of content. This supports partly separate verbal and visuospatial capacities, not a fixed ranking of responses."}, "simple": {"stem": "What general conclusion follows from Brooks's (1968) sentence and F experiments?", "options": ["Tasks are easier when the memory and the response use different types of short-term memory.", "Tasks are easier when the memory and the response use the same type of short-term memory.", "Pointing is always an easier way to respond than speaking aloud.", "Speaking is always an easier way to respond than pointing."], "correct": 0, "why": "Pointing was easier for the sentence and speaking was easier for the F, so the easier response is the one that uses the other type of short-term memory. Neither response is always easier."}}, "3122": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher claims that Brooks's (1968) sentence result shows speaking is always harder than pointing. Which additional condition would test this claim most strongly?", "options": ["Have participants judge inside and outside corners of a remembered F, comparing spoken and pointing responses.", "Repeat the same sentence task, but use speaking as the only response condition in the study.", "Make every sentence longer while also giving participants more time to point at their answers.", "Remove the memory demand entirely and ask participants which way of responding they prefer."], "correct": 0, "why": "The F task changes the remembered information to visuospatial and reverses the advantage, which directly refutes a general speaking disadvantage. Testing speaking alone, or changing length and timing together, cannot show a reversal."}, "simple": {"stem": "Brooks (1968) found pointing easier for a remembered sentence. What happened when participants remembered an F shape instead?", "options": ["The result reversed, and speaking became easier than pointing.", "Pointing stayed easier, just as it had been for the remembered sentence.", "Speaking and pointing became equally hard for participants.", "Participants could not remember the F well enough to respond."], "correct": 0, "why": "With the F, speaking was easier than pointing, the opposite of the sentence result. The sketchpad was busy with the image, while the phonological loop was free."}}, "3123": {"college": {"stem": "In one task a person holds a sentence in mind while responding; in another, the person holds a mental image of a shape while responding. Based on Brooks's (1968) findings, which pairing of response modes should produce the least interference?", "options": ["Pointing while holding the sentence, and speaking while holding the image of the shape.", "Speaking while holding the sentence, and pointing while holding the image of the shape.", "Speaking for both tasks, because producing language never uses any temporary processing.", "Pointing for both tasks, because spatial responses never compete with mental images."], "correct": 0, "why": "Interference is lowest when the response uses a different type of short-term memory from the held content: pointing for verbal content, speaking for visual content. The sentence and F results show opposite advantages, so neither single mode is best for both."}, "simple": {"stem": "Someone holds a mental picture of a map while answering questions about it. Based on Brooks (1968), which way of answering would interfere least?", "options": ["Answering out loud, since speech uses the phonological loop instead.", "Pointing to locations, since pointing uses the phonological loop.", "Pointing to locations, since the sketchpad is free during mental imagery.", "Answering out loud, since speech uses the visuospatial sketchpad."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental map occupies the visuospatial sketchpad, so a spoken answer, which uses the phonological loop, interferes least. Pointing is spatial and would compete with the image, as in the F task."}}, "3124": {"college": {"stem": "Verbal and visuospatial short-term memory are considered somewhat independent of each other. Which interpretation states that claim at the right strength?", "options": ["The two have at least partly separate capacities, so interference depends on the kinds of information and operations involved.", "The two systems can never interact with each other or with executive control during any task.", "Each system gains unlimited capacity whenever the other system is also being used at the same time.", "All temporary representations are identical, so the type of task cannot affect how much interference occurs."], "correct": 0, "why": "Somewhat independent means at least separate capacities, which is why interference depends on whether tasks share a type of memory. It does not mean complete isolation, since the central executive coordinates both, nor unlimited capacity."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does it mean that verbal and visuospatial short-term memory are somewhat independent?", "options": ["They have at least partly separate capacities that can be used together.", "They are one system that shares a single capacity for everything.", "They never interact with each other or with the central executive.", "Each of them has an unlimited capacity of its own."], "correct": 0, "why": "Somewhat independent means they have at least separate capacities, so verbal and visual tasks interfere less with each other. They still interact, coordinated by the central executive, and neither has unlimited capacity."}}, "3125": {"college": {"stem": "Shepard and Metzler (1971) asked participants to judge whether two block figures shown at different orientations were the same object. For picture-plane pairs, reaction time rose roughly linearly with the angle between the figures. Which account best fits this relation?", "options": ["A larger angle requires more mental rotation before the two figures can be compared in alignment.", "A larger angle speeds the judgment because less visual information has to be held in mind.", "Reaction time is unrelated to rotation, because the measure counts how many words are recalled.", "Every orientation is recognized instantly, and the times only reflect how fast figures are named."], "correct": 0, "why": "Reaction time increases steadily with angle, consistent with mentally rotating a spatial representation step by step until the figures line up. Faster judgments at larger angles or instant recognition would not produce a rising line."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Shepard and Metzler's (1971) mental rotation study, how did reaction time change as the angle of rotation increased?", "options": ["Reaction time increased steadily as the angle got larger.", "Reaction time decreased steadily as the angle got larger.", "Reaction time stayed the same at every angle tested.", "Reaction time rose only once the angle passed 180 degrees."], "correct": 0, "why": "For picture-plane pairs, reaction time rose in a near-straight line from about 0 to 180 degrees. Larger rotations took longer, which suggests a mental rotation process."}}, "3126": {"college": {"stem": "Two pairs of block figures like those used by Shepard and Metzler (1971) are matched in construction but differ in picture-plane rotation: one pair is 20 degrees apart and the other 160 degrees apart. Which prediction follows from their results?", "options": ["The 160-degree pair should generally take longer to judge than the 20-degree pair.", "The 160-degree pair should generally take less time to judge than the 20-degree pair.", "Both pairs should take the same time, because the objects themselves are held constant.", "Only the number of digits a participant can remember should change with the angle."], "correct": 0, "why": "Reaction time increases with angular separation, so the larger rotation should take longer. Matching the objects does not remove the extra mental rotation that a larger angle requires."}, "simple": {"stem": "Based on Shepard and Metzler (1971), which pair of rotated figures should take longest to compare?", "options": ["A pair rotated 160 degrees apart from each other.", "A pair rotated 20 degrees apart from each other.", "A pair rotated 60 degrees apart from each other.", "A pair that is not rotated apart from each other at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Reaction time rises with the angle of rotation, so the 160-degree pair takes longest. An unrotated pair is the fastest to compare."}}, "3127": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher proposes that comparing picture-plane pairs in Shepard and Metzler's (1971) task involves no time-consuming spatial transformation. With the objects and the judgment kept comparable, which result would most directly challenge this proposal?", "options": ["Judgment time increases systematically as the angle between the two objects increases.", "Participants report that the block figures look unfamiliar before the task begins.", "One display has more objects and also allows participants a longer time to respond.", "Participants accurately name a letter while it is visible, with no orientation judgment."], "correct": 0, "why": "A systematic rise in time with angle is the signature of a transformation that takes longer the farther it must go. Familiarity reports, confounded displays and unrelated naming tasks do not isolate rotation time."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Shepard and Metzler's (1971) results suggest people do when comparing rotated objects?", "options": ["They mentally rotate one object, which takes longer for larger angles.", "They recognize every orientation instantly, without any mental transformation.", "They name each object aloud and then compare the names instead.", "They memorize every object at every possible angle in advance."], "correct": 0, "why": "Because reaction time grows with the angle, people appear to rotate a mental image until the objects line up. Instant recognition would give the same time at every angle."}}, "3128": {"college": {"stem": "Shepard and Metzler (1971) used both picture-plane rotation and depth rotation. Which statement correctly distinguishes the two?", "options": ["Picture-plane rotation turns an object within the flat viewing plane, while depth rotation turns it in three dimensions.", "Picture-plane rotation changes an object's color, while depth rotation changes how many blocks it contains.", "Picture-plane rotation repeats an object's verbal label, while depth rotation stores its sound without an image.", "Picture-plane rotation moves the response button, while depth rotation moves attention away from the object."], "correct": 0, "why": "The distinction is about the axis of rotation: within the plane of the picture versus through depth. Color, block count, verbal labels and response buttons are not what differs between the two."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Shepard and Metzler's (1971) study, what is depth rotation?", "options": ["Turning an object in three dimensions rather than within the flat picture.", "Turning an object only within the flat surface of the picture.", "Making an object larger or smaller on the display screen.", "Moving an object closer to or farther from the viewer."], "correct": 0, "why": "Depth rotation turns the object through three-dimensional space, while picture-plane rotation turns it within the flat picture. Changing size or distance is not rotation."}}, "3129": {"college": {"stem": "During a mental calculation, a person replaces an old running total with a new one, switches from multiplying to adding, and ignores a nearby conversation. Which executive functions identified by Miyake and colleagues (2000) correspond to these three demands?", "options": ["Updating the held information, shifting between operations, and inhibiting irrelevant information.", "Storing a visual trace, permanently learning a fact, and lengthening the duration of sensory input.", "Phonological storage, visuospatial storage, and episodic storage, without any control operation.", "Repeating the same total, keeping the same operation, and letting every distraction into memory."], "correct": 0, "why": "Replacing the total is updating, switching operations is shifting, and ignoring the conversation is inhibition. The other options name storage or sensory processes, not executive control."}, "simple": {"stem": "Miyake and colleagues (2000) split the central executive into three functions. What are they?", "options": ["Updating information, shifting between tasks, and inhibiting distractions.", "Storing sounds, storing images, and binding them together into episodes.", "Encoding, storing, and retrieving information in long-term memory.", "Seeing, hearing, and touching incoming sensory information."], "correct": 0, "why": "The central executive is often split into updating, shifting and inhibition (Miyake et al., 2000). Storing sounds and images belongs to the loop and sketchpad, not the executive."}}, "3130": {"college": {"stem": "The central executive is often criticized as theorizing a 'homunculus' in the brain. Miyake and colleagues (2000) split executive control into updating, shifting and inhibition. How does this split respond to the criticism?", "options": ["It names specific control operations instead of giving all control to one unexplained inner agent.", "It establishes that an actual miniature person directs each of the working-memory components.", "It replaces the phonological store with three separate permanent stores for unrelated kinds of knowledge.", "It removes the need to explain attention, because all contents are selected automatically."], "correct": 0, "why": "The homunculus criticism is that calling something an executive names a controller without explaining how it works. Splitting it into distinct operations specifies what the control consists of, rather than positing a literal little person."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why is the idea of a central executive often criticized?", "options": ["It can sound like a little person in the brain doing the work.", "It cannot hold any verbal information such as words or numbers.", "It was added to the model late, in the year 2000.", "It holds only visual images and ignores every other kind of information."], "correct": 0, "why": "The central executive is criticized as theorizing a homunculus, a little person in the head who does the work without explanation. It was part of the original 1974 model; the episodic buffer was the 2000 addition."}}, "3131": {"college": {"stem": "A participant can repeat a verbal list accurately but struggles to keep unrelated information from intruding while using that list. Which function is most directly implicated in the difficulty?", "options": ["Executive inhibition, which suppresses irrelevant information while held contents are being used.", "The visual appearance of the list before any information has reached the sensory system.", "Permanent storage of the exact list in memory for the rest of the participant's life.", "A single process in which verbal maintenance and attentional control are really one and the same operation."], "correct": 0, "why": "Repeating the list is phonological rehearsal, which works; suppressing irrelevant information is a central executive function (inhibition). Maintenance and executive control are separate demands."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which part of working memory controls the suppression of irrelevant information?", "options": ["The central executive, which directs attention and suppresses distractions.", "The phonological store, which holds verbal and auditory information.", "The visuospatial sketchpad, which holds visual and spatial information.", "The articulatory rehearsal process, which repeats words aloud or silently to prevent decay."], "correct": 0, "why": "The central executive directs and maintains attention and controls suppression of irrelevant information. The stores and the rehearsal process hold or refresh information but do not filter it."}}, "3132": {"college": {"stem": "Baddeley (2000) added the episodic buffer partly because people can immediately recall far more words from a meaningful sentence than from a list of unrelated words. Which proposed function addresses that difference?", "options": ["Binding temporary verbal and visuospatial contents with long-term knowledge into integrated episodes.", "Holding every word as a separate item without drawing on any existing knowledge of language.", "Replacing long-term memory with a temporary visual image of the entire sentence.", "Removing the capacity limit of the phonological store whenever a sentence is grammatical."], "correct": 0, "why": "The buffer binds information from the loop, sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes, so knowledge of meaning lets many words be held as a few units. Holding words as separate items would predict ordinary unrelated-word span."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000) do?", "options": ["It binds information from the loop, sketchpad, and long-term memory into episodes.", "It holds only the sounds of words so that they can be rehearsed.", "It directs attention and switches between different tasks.", "It stores every experience permanently with unlimited capacity."], "correct": 0, "why": "The episodic buffer is a limited-capacity store that binds information from the phonological loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Directing attention belongs to the central executive."}}, "3133": {"college": {"stem": "A person immediately recalls about 16 words of a meaningful sentence. A student concludes that the episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000) cannot be limited to about four chunks. Which response reconciles the two facts?", "options": ["A single chunk can group several related words, so recalled words can outnumber the integrated units.", "A chunk is always exactly one word, so a sentence longer than four words could never be recalled immediately.", "Meaningful sentences bypass temporary memory and are never available for immediate recall at all.", "Recalling sixteen words shows that every form of temporary storage has unlimited capacity."], "correct": 0, "why": "Chunks are meaningful units, not single words, so about four chunks can hold a much longer sentence. The limited capacity and the 16-word recall are therefore compatible."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the approximate capacity of the episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000)?", "options": ["About 4 chunks of integrated information.", "About 16 separate, unrelated words at once.", "About 7 plus or minus 2 single letters.", "An unlimited amount of information at once."], "correct": 0, "why": "The episodic buffer has a limited capacity of about four chunks. About 16 words is how much of a meaningful sentence people recall, which is possible because each chunk can hold several words."}}, "3134": {"college": {"stem": "Some amnesic patients can immediately recall a story they have just heard. Which part of Baddeley's (2000) episodic-buffer account most directly addresses this observation?", "options": ["An integrated episode can be held temporarily even when longer-term memory is impaired.", "Immediate recall proves that every long-term memory function is intact in all amnesic patients.", "The story must be held only as isolated speech sounds with no meaningful links among them.", "The episodic buffer permanently repairs the long-term memory impairment each time a story is heard."], "correct": 0, "why": "Immediate story recall in amnesia suggests a temporary store that holds integrated episodes, which is the buffer's role. It does not imply intact long-term memory or a permanent repair."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which observation helped motivate adding the episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000)?", "options": ["Some amnesic patients can immediately recall a story they just heard.", "People confuse letters that sound alike even when they see them.", "People remember fewer long words than short words from a list.", "Reaction time increases as two objects are rotated farther apart from each other."], "correct": 0, "why": "Immediate story recall by some amnesic patients, together with recall of about 16 words of a meaningful sentence, motivated the buffer. The other findings support the phonological loop and the sketchpad."}}, "3135": {"college": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) task, an arrow cue was followed by a brief memory display of red bars, sometimes with blue bars added as irrelevant items, then a blank delay and a test display. What did participants need to retain across the delay?", "options": ["The orientations of the relevant red bars on the cued side, ignoring the blue bars.", "Every visible bar equally, because the blue bars were extra targets in the distractor condition.", "Only the color name of each bar, because the test never concerned the bars' orientations.", "Nothing from the memory display, because it stayed visible throughout the test display."], "correct": 0, "why": "The cue picks the relevant side, and the red bars there are the items to remember; blue bars are distractors to be filtered out. The memory display disappears during the delay, so the targets must be held in mind."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) task, what were the blue bars?", "options": ["Irrelevant distractors that participants were supposed to ignore.", "Extra targets that participants had to remember as well.", "Cues that showed which side of the screen to attend.", "Feedback signals telling participants that their previous answer was correct."], "correct": 0, "why": "The red bars were the items to remember, and the blue bars were irrelevant distractors. The cue showing which side to attend was an arrow."}}, "3136": {"college": {"stem": "Why did Vogel and colleagues' (2005) procedure include a blank retention interval between the brief memory display and the test display?", "options": ["The bars had to be held while absent, so memory-related activity could be measured after visual exposure ended.", "The interval let participants copy the original bars while the memory display was still visible.", "The interval turned the irrelevant blue bars into relevant red targets before each response.", "The interval ensured that every participant had exactly the same working-memory capacity."], "correct": 0, "why": "During the retention interval the screen is blank, so any later comparison depends on information held in working memory, and the activity recorded then reflects maintenance. A delay neither allows copying nor equalizes capacity."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) task, what happened during the retention interval?", "options": ["The screen was blank, so participants had to hold the bars in mind.", "The bars stayed on the screen so participants could keep looking at them.", "Participants named each bar's color out loud to rehearse it.", "A new set of bars appeared for participants to compare."], "correct": 0, "why": "The memory display was replaced by a blank screen (about 900 ms), so the bars had to be held in working memory until the test display. Brain activity continued well into this interval."}}, "3137": {"college": {"stem": "Vogel and colleagues (2005) compared a display of two red targets with a display of two red targets plus two irrelevant blue bars. What is held constant, and what extra demand is added?", "options": ["The number of relevant targets stays the same, while the distractor display adds a need to filter out irrelevant objects.", "The total number of objects stays the same, while the distractor display removes every selection demand.", "The number of relevant targets doubles, while the distractor display adds no irrelevant objects at all.", "The memory demand disappears entirely, while the distractor display measures only the naming of colors."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both displays require remembering two targets, so the added blue bars test exclusion of irrelevant items rather than a larger relevant load. The total number of objects is what changes."}, "simple": {"stem": "Vogel and colleagues (2005) added two blue distractors to two red targets. What did this addition test?", "options": ["How well participants could filter out irrelevant items.", "How many more targets participants could remember.", "How quickly participants could name the colors shown.", "How long participants could look at the display."], "correct": 0, "why": "Adding distractors while keeping two targets tests filtering, that is, keeping irrelevant items out of working memory. A test of how many targets can be held would add relevant items instead."}}, "3138": {"college": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) high-capacity group, brain activity for two targets plus two distractors resembled activity for two targets alone rather than for four targets. Which inference fits?", "options": ["High-capacity participants filtered out the distractors instead of holding every visible object in memory.", "High-capacity participants held every distractor, producing the same load as four relevant objects.", "High-capacity participants never registered the memory display and responded without any target information.", "The two-target and four-target conditions placed identical memory loads on every participant."], "correct": 0, "why": "Adding distractors had no effect for high-capacity people, so they kept only the relevant items in memory. Their activity was greater for four relevant items, so the two-item match is not a failure to register load."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) study, what happened to high-capacity participants' brain activity when blue distractors were added?", "options": ["It stayed about the same as with the two red targets alone.", "It rose to about the level seen with four red targets.", "It dropped well below the level seen with two targets.", "It disappeared completely during the blank retention interval."], "correct": 0, "why": "For high-capacity people, adding irrelevant blue items had no effect on activity. Low-capacity people were the ones whose activity increased."}}, "3139": {"college": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) low-capacity group, adding blue distractors to two relevant red items increased memory-related activity toward the level seen with four relevant items. Which account explains this?", "options": ["Irrelevant items were let into working memory, using up resources alongside the two targets.", "The distractors improved selection so much that fewer objects were processed than with two targets.", "The relevant red items physically vanished from the screen as soon as blue items were added.", "The larger response shows low-capacity people remembered the targets better than the high group."], "correct": 0, "why": "The increase indicates poorer filtering: distractors entered working memory and added to the load. A larger load signal does not mean better memory for the targets."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) study, what happened for low-capacity participants when blue distractors were added?", "options": ["Brain activity increased, as if they were also storing the distractors.", "Brain activity stayed the same, as if they ignored the distractors.", "Brain activity decreased, as if they had stopped storing the red targets.", "Brain activity stopped, as if they had forgotten the whole display."], "correct": 0, "why": "Adding irrelevant blue items increased activity for low-capacity people, suggesting the distractors were held along with the targets. Activity staying the same was the high-capacity pattern."}}, "3140": {"college": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) EEG plots, negative voltage is plotted upward. A student assumes that a higher trace means a more positive voltage and therefore better memory. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": ["A higher trace is a larger negative response, and whether targets or distractors drive it matters.", "Plotting upward guarantees a positive voltage and therefore accurate recall of every target.", "The sign of the plotted voltage reveals whether a bar on the screen was red or blue.", "Every condition that produces a larger deflection must contain only relevant items and never any distractors."], "correct": 0, "why": "The y-axis has −2 µV at the top, so a higher trace is a more negative response. In the low-capacity group a higher trace came from distractors being processed, so it does not mean better memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) EEG graphs, which direction on the y-axis shows negative voltage?", "options": ["Up, because −2 µV is marked at the top of the axis.", "Down, because −2 µV is marked at the very bottom of the axis.", "Down, because voltage always increases toward the top.", "Neither, because the graph shows only reaction times in seconds."], "correct": 0, "why": "The axis is labeled −2 µV at the top and +2 µV at the bottom, so negative voltage is plotted upward. The x-axis is time in milliseconds, not reaction time."}}, "3141": {"college": {"stem": "Which pair of conditions in Vogel and colleagues' (2005) task most directly tests filtering efficiency, rather than just the effect of adding more relevant targets?", "options": ["Two relevant targets alone versus the same two relevant targets with irrelevant blue distractors.", "Two relevant targets versus four relevant targets, with no distractors present in either condition.", "A visible display copied immediately versus an unrelated verbal sentence recalled at a later time.", "One target condition with a short delay versus another target condition with a longer exposure."], "correct": 0, "why": "Keeping the number of targets constant while adding irrelevant objects isolates filtering. Two versus four targets measures relevant load instead, and changing delay or exposure adds confounds."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) study, which comparison shows how well someone filters out distractors?", "options": ["Two red targets alone versus two red targets plus two blue bars.", "Two red targets alone versus four red targets with no blue bars.", "A short delay versus a long delay with the same red targets.", "Four red targets versus four blue bars with no red targets."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding two targets constant and adding blue bars shows whether the distractors get into memory. Comparing two with four red targets measures how activity changes with more relevant items."}}, "3142": {"college": {"stem": "Vogel and colleagues (2005) reported that measured working-memory capacity is correlated with an EEG measure of filtering efficiency. Which statement gives the direction of this finding?", "options": ["Participants with higher measured capacity tended to exclude irrelevant items more efficiently.", "Participants with higher measured capacity tended to hold more irrelevant items at the cost of targets.", "Participants with lower measured capacity always had the highest filtering-efficiency values.", "Filtering efficiency was unrelated to capacity, because irrelevant items never appeared in the task."], "correct": 0, "why": "People with higher capacity were also less distracted by irrelevant items, so capacity and filtering efficiency rise together. The opposite direction contradicts the scatterplot, and distractors were part of the task."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Vogel and colleagues (2005) find about people with high working-memory capacity?", "options": ["They were better at ignoring the irrelevant items.", "They stored more of the irrelevant blue items as well.", "They were worse at ignoring the irrelevant items.", "They showed no brain activity during the delay."], "correct": 0, "why": "Capacity was correlated with filtering efficiency: people with higher capacity were less distracted by irrelevant items. They did show activity during the delay, which grew with more relevant items."}}, "3143": {"college": {"stem": "Two participants must hold the same number of relevant bars in mind. One also processes many irrelevant blue bars and recalls the targets less well. How does the filtering account of Vogel and colleagues (2005) interpret the lower score?", "options": ["Irrelevant items can take up limited capacity, leaving less room for the relevant targets.", "A low target score shows the participant had no memory-related electrical response at all.", "A low target score means no relevant information ever reached the participant's eyes.", "Extra irrelevant items must raise effective capacity, because more objects were on the screen."], "correct": 0, "why": "Working memory is limited, so distractors that get in use capacity that the targets need. Differences in capacity may partly reflect how well people keep irrelevant items out, not sensory failure or absent activity."}, "simple": {"stem": "Based on Vogel and colleagues (2005), why might someone have a lower working-memory capacity?", "options": ["They let irrelevant items use up some of their limited capacity.", "They see fewer of the items that appear on the screen in the first place.", "They have no brain activity during the delay period.", "They store the relevant items permanently instead of briefly."], "correct": 0, "why": "Lower-capacity people were more distracted by irrelevant items, which then took up limited working-memory space. They still saw the display and showed delay activity."}}, "3144": {"college": {"stem": "A person recalls similar-sounding letters poorly, remembers fewer long words than short words, and does worse when speaking during memorization. Which labels match these three findings in order?", "options": ["The phonological similarity effect, the word-length effect, and articulatory suppression, in that order.", "The word-length effect, articulatory suppression, and the phonological similarity effect, in that order.", "Articulatory suppression, the phonological similarity effect, and the word-length effect, in that order.", "Mental rotation, episodic binding, and permanent long-term storage of the items, in that order."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sound-based confusion is the phonological similarity effect, poorer memory for long words is the word-length effect, and worse memory while speaking is articulatory suppression. All three are evidence for the phonological loop."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the phonological similarity effect?", "options": ["Letters or words that sound alike are easily confused in memory.", "Letters or words that look alike are easily confused in short-term memory.", "Short words are remembered better than long words in a list.", "Speaking while memorizing makes it harder to remember a list."], "correct": 0, "why": "The phonological similarity effect is the confusion of letters or words that sound similar. The other options describe a visual confusion, the word-length effect and articulatory suppression."}}, "3145": {"college": {"stem": "Short and long words differ strongly in recall during ordinary memorization, but the difference shrinks under articulatory suppression. Which additional result is needed to avoid interpreting the smaller difference as improved memory?", "options": ["Overall recall is lower under suppression, even though the gap between word lengths is smaller.", "The words are now stored permanently, so no recall measure is needed in the suppression condition.", "Participants now remember every long word perfectly, because all rehearsal has stopped.", "The original word lists have been replaced with unrelated visual shapes during suppression."], "correct": 0, "why": "A smaller effect can come with worse overall performance; under suppression people remember less and the word-length effect is reduced. Showing lower overall recall rules out the idea that memory improved."}, "simple": {"stem": "Under articulatory suppression, the word-length effect gets smaller. What happens to overall recall?", "options": ["Overall recall gets worse when people speak while memorizing.", "Overall recall gets better when people speak while memorizing.", "Overall recall stays exactly the same as when memorizing in silence.", "Overall recall improves for the long words but not for the short words."], "correct": 0, "why": "Speaking while memorizing keeps the phonological loop busy, so people get worse at remembering. The word-length effect shrinks because rehearsal is disrupted, not because memory improves."}}, "3146": {"college": {"stem": "A proposed study compares sentence memory with spoken answers against shape memory with pointed answers and finds both difficult. Why would adding the other response mode within each memory task, as Brooks (1968) did, be useful?", "options": ["It tests whether the easier response flips with the kind of remembered information, rather than comparing unlike tasks.", "It guarantees that sentence memory and shape memory are equally difficult before either one is measured.", "It removes the need to keep response instructions consistent across all of the conditions in the study.", "It converts the visual memory task into a purely auditory task in every one of the conditions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Brooks's evidence comes from comparing speaking and pointing within each type of content and finding the advantage reverses. That ties interference to shared processing rather than to an uncontrolled difference between two tasks."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did Brooks (1968) test both speaking and pointing within each memory task?", "options": ["To see whether the easier response changed with the type of information held.", "To make the sentence task and the F task exactly equal in difficulty.", "To check whether participants generally preferred speaking or pointing.", "To turn the F task into a verbal task for every participant."], "correct": 0, "why": "Testing both responses for each task showed that pointing was easier for the sentence and speaking was easier for the F. That reversal is what links interference to the type of short-term memory being used."}}, "3147": {"college": {"stem": "A model has separate verbal and visuospatial stores but no way to represent their contents together with long-term knowledge. Which addition by Baddeley (2000) fills this gap?", "options": ["A limited-capacity episodic buffer that binds those sources into integrated episodes.", "An unlimited phonological store that replaces every other working-memory component.", "A longer-lasting sensory trace that stores all meaningful episodes permanently.", "A response rule that forbids verbal and visual information from being used in one task."], "correct": 0, "why": "The episodic buffer binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Enlarging one store or lengthening sensory traces does not provide that binding."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which working-memory component links the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory (Baddeley, 2000)?", "options": ["The episodic buffer, which binds them into integrated episodes.", "The phonological store, which holds verbal and auditory information.", "Sensory memory, which briefly holds raw input from the senses.", "The articulatory rehearsal process, which repeats verbal items."], "correct": 0, "why": "The episodic buffer, added in 2000, binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory. The phonological store and rehearsal process are parts of the loop itself."}}, "3148": {"college": {"stem": "A student treats the strongest retention-interval response as the best memory performance in every condition. Which result from Vogel and colleagues (2005) most clearly challenges that view?", "options": ["Low-capacity participants show increased activity when irrelevant blue items accompany the targets.", "High-capacity participants show a stronger response to four relevant items than to two relevant items.", "The memory display appears before the retention interval rather than after the test display.", "The cue tells participants which side of the display is relevant before the memory display appears."], "correct": 0, "why": "For low-capacity people, activity rose when distractors were added, reflecting irrelevant items being held rather than better target memory. The four-versus-two result is consistent with the student's view, so it cannot challenge it."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Vogel and colleagues' (2005) study, does more brain activity during the delay always mean better memory?", "options": ["No, because low-capacity people's activity rose when distractors were added.", "Yes, because more activity always means more targets are remembered.", "Yes, because high-capacity people always showed the most activity.", "No, because brain activity was never measured during the delay."], "correct": 0, "why": "Low-capacity people showed more activity with distractors, which reflects holding irrelevant items, not better memory. Activity was measured and continued well into the delay."}}, "3149": {"college": {"stem": "A follow-up to Vogel and colleagues' (2005) study compares two relevant items alone, four relevant items alone, and two relevant items plus distractors. What role does the four-item condition play in interpreting the distractor condition?", "options": ["It gives a higher relevant-load benchmark for judging whether distractors produce activity like holding more objects.", "It makes the distractors relevant in every condition, so they no longer need to be told apart from targets.", "It ensures that all electrical activity occurs only while the memory display is physically on the screen.", "It substitutes a verbal span test for the visual comparison and removes the retention interval entirely."], "correct": 0, "why": "The four-item condition shows what activity looks like when more objects are genuinely held. A distractor response close to it suggests the irrelevant objects also entered working memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did Vogel and colleagues (2005) include a condition with four red targets?", "options": ["To show what brain activity looks like when more items are truly stored.", "To make the blue distractors count as targets in every condition.", "To measure how quickly participants could name four different colors aloud.", "To give participants an easier task than the two-item condition."], "correct": 0, "why": "The four-target condition shows the activity produced by holding more relevant items, so it can be compared with the two-targets-plus-distractors condition. Activity was greater for four relevant items than for two."}}, "3150": {"college": {"stem": "A participant holds verbal information accurately but loses track of which operation to perform when the task changes. Which interpretation is most consistent with the working-memory model?", "options": ["Accurate verbal maintenance can coexist with a problem in executive shifting between operations.", "Accurate verbal maintenance guarantees that every one of the executive control operations is fully intact.", "Shifting is the phonological store's passive retention of every sound heard in the room.", "A change of task must be handled entirely by sensory memory, with no executive involvement."], "correct": 0, "why": "The model separates storage of verbal content (the phonological loop) from executive operations such as shifting. Success at one does not guarantee the other."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which executive function lets a person switch from one task or operation to another?", "options": ["Shifting, which moves control between tasks or operations.", "Updating, which replaces old information with new information.", "Inhibition, which suppresses irrelevant or distracting information.", "Rehearsal, which repeats verbal information to keep it active."], "correct": 0, "why": "Shifting is switching between tasks or operations. Updating changes the held contents, inhibition suppresses distraction, and rehearsal is part of the phonological loop rather than an executive function."}}, "4001": {"college": {"stem": "Someone knows exactly what the finished tile arrangement should look like but cannot see how to get there from the current arrangement. What makes this situation a problem in the cognitive sense?", "options": ["There is an obstacle between the present state and the goal, and no solution is immediately obvious.", "The goal is unclear, and a situation stops being a problem as soon as the desired end state is known.", "Some tiles are missing, and a problem can exist only when the needed parts are unavailable.", "The person has already made a wrong move, which is what turns any task into a problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "A problem is an obstacle between a present state and a goal whose solution is not immediately obvious. The goal can be perfectly clear; missing parts and an earlier error are not part of the definition."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a problem in cognitive psychology?", "options": ["An obstacle between where you are now and a goal, with no obvious solution.", "Any task where the goal is unknown and has to be guessed first.", "Any task that you have tried once before and gotten wrong.", "Any task that needs more objects than you have on hand."], "correct": 0, "why": "A problem is an obstacle between a present state and a goal, where the solution is not immediately obvious. Knowing the goal, past errors and missing objects do not define it."}}, "4002": {"college": {"stem": "Gestalt psychologists extended their interest from perception to learning and problem solving (Koffka, 1935) and took a perceptual approach to all of them. Two people look at the same Kohler circle problem, and only one sees that line x is a diagonal of a rectangle. How would a Gestalt psychologist explain the difference?", "options": ["The two people represent the same figure differently, and restructuring that representation reveals the solution.", "The two people must have been shown figures that contained different geometric information about the circle.", "The successful person searched through more legal moves before ever inspecting the figure than the other person did.", "Seeing the rectangle physically changes the radius rather than changing how the lines relate to each other."], "correct": 0, "why": "For Gestalt psychologists, problem solving depends on how the problem is represented in the mind and on restructuring that representation. The figure itself is the same for both people; only its mental organization differs."}, "simple": {"stem": "Gestalt psychologists (Koffka, 1935) took a perceptual approach to problem solving. For them, problem solving was mainly about what?", "options": ["How a problem is represented in the mind and how that representation is restructured.", "How many legal moves a person searches through before finally reaching the goal state.", "How quickly a person can repeat a procedure that worked on earlier problems.", "How much reward a person receives for reaching the correct answer quickly."], "correct": 0, "why": "The Gestalt approach treated problem solving as representing a problem in the mind and restructuring that representation. Searching through legal moves belongs to the later information-processing approach."}}, "4003": {"college": {"stem": "In Kohler's circle problem, line x is one diagonal of a rectangle drawn inside a circle of radius r, and the rectangle's other diagonal runs from the circle's center to the circle itself. Why does noticing that other diagonal solve the problem?", "options": ["The other diagonal runs from center to circle, so it equals r, and a rectangle's two diagonals are equal.", "The other diagonal is a diameter, so line x must equal 2r regardless of any property of the rectangle.", "Every line drawn inside a circle equals the radius, so the rectangle adds nothing to the argument.", "Line x equals half the radius because the rectangle fills only one quarter of the whole circle."], "correct": 0, "why": "A line from the center to the circle is a radius, and the diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so x = r. The other diagonal is not a diameter, and not every interior line equals the radius."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Kohler's circle problem, the key is seeing that line x is one diagonal of a rectangle whose other diagonal is a radius. How long is line x?", "options": ["Line x equals r, because both diagonals of a rectangle are the same length.", "Line x equals 2r, because any diagonal drawn inside a circle is a diameter.", "Line x equals r/2, because the rectangle fills one quarter of the circle.", "Line x cannot be found without measuring it directly with a ruler."], "correct": 0, "why": "The other diagonal reaches from the center to the circle, so it is a radius. A rectangle's diagonals are equal, so x also equals r."}}, "4004": {"college": {"stem": "Which description best captures insight as Gestalt psychologists used the term in problem solving?", "options": ["A sudden realization of the solution, often following a reorganization of how the problem is represented.", "A steady rise in felt closeness to the answer after each successful intermediate step in the procedure is completed.", "Repeated reliance on a previously successful procedure even when a simpler one is available.", "Selection of any legal move that reduces the number of operators still available to the solver."], "correct": 0, "why": "Gestalt psychologists linked insight to restructuring: a sudden realization of a problem's solution, the 'Aha' experience. A steady rise describes noninsight progress, and reliance on an old procedure is a mental set."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is insight in problem solving?", "options": ["A sudden realization of a problem's solution, the 'Aha' experience.", "A slow, step-by-step approach to the answer over a long time.", "Sticking with a method that worked on earlier problems.", "Using an object only for its usual, familiar purpose."], "correct": 0, "why": "Insight is the sudden realization of a solution, often called the 'Aha' experience. Sticking with an old method is a mental set, and limiting an object to its usual use is functional fixedness."}}, "4005": {"college": {"stem": "A participant solves one problem through a sequence of algebraic steps and another after suddenly seeing the objects in a new way. How do these cases fit the Gestalt view of insight and the objection other researchers raise to it?", "options": ["Only the second case shows an insight experience; many problems, like the first, are solved without one.", "Both cases must involve the same sudden experience, since both end with a correct solution.", "Only the algebra case counts as real problem solving, because the other answer was not reached gradually.", "The sudden solution shows that no mental activity took place before the moment of the answer."], "correct": 0, "why": "Gestalt psychologists tied insight to sudden restructuring, but other researchers point out that much problem solving involves no 'Aha' experience. A sudden answer also does not mean nothing was processed beforehand."}, "simple": {"stem": "Gestalt psychologists thought solutions come to people all of a sudden. What objection do other researchers raise to this view?", "options": ["A lot of problem solving happens without any sudden 'Aha' experience.", "Insight problems are actually solved more slowly than algebra problems.", "People never reorganize how they represent a problem while solving it.", "Sudden solutions happen only when someone else gives away the answer."], "correct": 0, "why": "Other researchers do not necessarily agree that solutions arrive suddenly; much of our problem solving does not require an 'Aha' experience."}}, "4006": {"college": {"stem": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) gave participants insight problems (a triangle problem and a chain problem) and noninsight algebra problems, collecting warmth judgments every 15 seconds. What hypothesis did this procedure test?", "options": ["Felt closeness should rise gradually for algebra but stay low until a sudden solution on insight problems.", "Both problem types should show identical gradual increases, since warmth reflects only final accuracy.", "Insight solvers should predict their progress more precisely, because restructuring is consciously anticipated.", "Algebra solvers should be unaware of their progress, because methodical procedures hide the distance to solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "The hypothesis was that the methodical noninsight process lets solvers know how close they are, whereas insight solvers should be poor at predicting how near they are to a solution. Repeated warmth ratings tracked felt closeness over time."}, "simple": {"stem": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) asked people solving problems to give a warmth rating every 15 seconds. What did these ratings measure?", "options": ["How close the person felt to reaching the solution at that moment.", "How many correct answers the person had given up to that point.", "How much the person enjoyed working on that particular problem.", "How difficult an expert had judged the problem to be in advance."], "correct": 0, "why": "Warmth judgments were self-reports of closeness to the solution; 'hot' ratings meant the participant felt close to solving."}}, "4007": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher repeats Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) comparison of insight and algebra problems but records only whether each problem is eventually solved. What critical measure is missing?", "options": ["Repeated ratings, taken during the attempt, of how close the participant feels to the solution.", "A second accuracy check after the participant has already given the same correct answer.", "A count of the dots in the triangle puzzle, without any report at all of the participant's thoughts.", "A replacement of the algebra problems with another copy of the same triangle puzzle."], "correct": 0, "why": "Whether a problem is solved says nothing about how felt progress changed along the way. The warmth ratings every 15 seconds were what allowed gradual and sudden approaches to be told apart."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) study, why were warmth ratings collected again and again during solving instead of once?", "options": ["To track how the feeling of closeness changed over time before the solution.", "To check a second time whether the final answer was actually correct.", "To count how many problems each participant was able to finish.", "To give participants short rest breaks between the separate problems they solved."], "correct": 0, "why": "Repeated ratings showed how felt closeness changed over time, which is what distinguishes a gradual approach from a sudden one."}}, "4008": {"college": {"stem": "Suppose a study modeled on Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) collects a single warmth rating for each insight problem, immediately after it is solved. What is the key flaw?", "options": ["A high final rating cannot tell a sudden jump apart from a gradual rise in the period before solution.", "A rating obtained after success proves that the participant consciously predicted the answer in advance.", "A final rating measures the number of legal operators rather than the participant's felt closeness.", "Collecting ratings after solution automatically turns every insight problem into an algebra problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "The predicted difference is in the path before solution. Gradual and sudden paths both end with a high rating, so only repeated ratings before success can separate them."}, "simple": {"stem": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) predicted that people would be poor at judging how close they were to solving insight problems. Why?", "options": ["Insight solutions arrive suddenly, so there is little feeling of gradual progress.", "Insight problems are easier, so people stop paying close attention while solving them.", "Insight problems use numbers, which make progress hard to estimate.", "Insight solvers are not allowed to look at the problem while working."], "correct": 0, "why": "Insight problems were expected to be solved suddenly, so solvers should not sense how near they are. Methodical noninsight problems were expected to let solvers track their progress."}}, "4009": {"college": {"stem": "In the chain problem, four separate three-link pieces must be joined into one closed loop. Opening a link costs 2 cents, closing a link costs 3 cents, and only 15 cents is available. Which plan works?", "options": ["Open all three links of one piece and use them to join the other three pieces into a loop.", "Open and reclose one end link of each of the four pieces, joining the pieces in turn.", "Join the four pieces in a straight line with three links and leave the two outer ends open.", "Leave every piece intact and treat the gaps between pieces as if they were closed links."], "correct": 0, "why": "Opening and closing three links costs 3 x 5 = 15 cents, and the three freed links can join the other three pieces into a closed loop. Four open-and-close operations would cost 20 cents, and an open line does not meet the goal."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the chain problem, opening a link costs 2 cents, closing one costs 3 cents, and there are 15 cents. What is the key to solving it?", "options": ["Take apart one whole piece and use its three links to join the others.", "Open one link at the end of each of the four pieces.", "Join the pieces into a straight line rather than a loop.", "Ask for more money so that more links can be opened."], "correct": 0, "why": "Taking apart one piece gives three links; opening and closing three links costs exactly 15 cents and joins the remaining three pieces into a loop. Opening one link per piece would cost 20 cents."}}, "4010": {"college": {"stem": "A participant insists the chain problem is impossible because 'each original piece has to stay intact.' What has the participant added to the actual task?", "options": ["A self-imposed restriction that blocks using one piece's links as connectors for the others.", "The stated requirement that opening a link and then closing it again costs 5 cents in total.", "The stated requirement that the finished chain must form one single closed loop.", "The correct observation that three links can be opened and closed within the budget."], "correct": 0, "why": "Nothing in the task says a piece must stay intact. That added assumption blocks the solution; restructuring the problem so that one piece can supply the connecting links removes it."}, "simple": {"stem": "A person thinks the chain problem cannot be solved because they assume each piece must stay whole. In Gestalt terms, what do they need to do?", "options": ["Restructure how they represent the problem so a piece can be taken apart.", "Search through every possible order of opening links until one of them works.", "Accept that the goal cannot be reached with the money available.", "Ask for a different problem that uses fewer pieces of chain."], "correct": 0, "why": "The obstacle is the person's representation, not the task rules. Restructuring the representation lets them see that one piece can be broken into three connecting links."}}, "4011": {"college": {"stem": "In the blue-dot problem, ten dots form an upward-pointing triangle with rows of one, two, three and four dots. A solver moves the top dot below the bottom row and moves the two end dots of the bottom row out to the ends of the two-dot row. What makes this a valid solution?", "options": ["The triangle now points downward, and only three dots have moved while the other seven stay put.", "The new arrangement contains extra dots, which allows a new downward tip to be added.", "All four original rows keep their exact positions, so the upward-pointing outline is fully preserved.", "Every dot shifts downward by the same distance, moving the triangle without inverting it."], "correct": 0, "why": "The task is to make the triangle point down by moving exactly three of the ten dots. The rearrangement gives rows of four, three, two and one from top to bottom, with seven dots unmoved."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the blue-dot triangle problem, what is the solver asked to do?", "options": ["Move only three dots so the triangle points toward the bottom.", "Add several new dots until the triangle becomes a larger square shape.", "Move every dot so the whole triangle shifts to the right.", "Remove three dots so the triangle has fewer rows than before."], "correct": 0, "why": "The goal is to move three of the blue dots so the triangle points to the bottom. No dots are added or removed."}}, "4012": {"college": {"stem": "In Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) results, median warmth for insight problems stayed at 2 until 30 seconds before solution, rose to 3 at 15 seconds, then jumped to 7 at solution, while algebra ratings rose steadily. What does this pattern show?", "options": ["An insight solution can be close at hand without any matching gradual rise in felt closeness.", "Low warmth proves that insight solvers were objectively farther from the answer than algebra solvers.", "The higher middle ratings for algebra show that algebra problems always take less total time.", "A sudden jump in warmth shows that insight requires no reorganization of the problem's representation."], "correct": 0, "why": "Insight problems were solved suddenly (a big jump from 3 to 7) and algebra problems gradually. Warmth is a subjective rating, so the pattern says nothing about objective distance, total time or an absence of restructuring."}, "simple": {"stem": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) tracked warmth ratings over the final minute before solution. What did they find?", "options": ["Insight ratings jumped suddenly at the end, while algebra ratings rose gradually.", "Algebra ratings jumped suddenly at the end, while insight ratings rose gradually.", "Insight and algebra ratings both rose at the same gradual rate.", "Neither type of problem showed any change in warmth before solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "Insight problems were solved suddenly, with a big jump from 3 to 7 just before solution; algebra problems showed a gradual increase."}}, "4013": {"college": {"stem": "Which hypothetical outcome of Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) warmth-rating study would most directly undercut the proposed difference between insight and noninsight problems?", "options": ["Both problem types show similar gradual increases in warmth across the period before solution.", "Both problem types eventually reach a high warmth rating once the solution is found.", "Insight puzzles and algebra problems use different physical objects during the attempt.", "Some participants manage to solve more than one of the problems they are given."], "correct": 0, "why": "The prediction concerns different paths before solution. Matching gradual rises would contradict it, while high ratings at the moment of solution fit either a gradual or a sudden path."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which result would have argued against Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) conclusion that insight problems are solved suddenly?", "options": ["Insight ratings rising gradually, just as the algebra ratings did.", "Insight ratings staying low until just before the solution.", "Algebra ratings rising steadily during the minute before solution.", "Both kinds of rating ending high at the moment of solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "If insight ratings had risen gradually like algebra ratings, there would be no evidence of a sudden solution. The other outcomes match what was actually found."}}, "4014": {"college": {"stem": "A participant thinks of a matchbox only as a holder for matches and never considers using it to support a candle. Which statement describes this correctly?", "options": ["It is functional fixedness, a type of fixation in which an object is limited to its familiar use.", "It is means-end analysis, because the box's familiar use automatically reduces the distance to the goal.", "It is the goal state, because recognizing the box's usual purpose completes the mounting task.", "It is an operator rule, because the task explicitly forbids attaching the matchbox to the corkboard."], "correct": 0, "why": "Functional fixedness, restricting an object to its familiar function, is a form of fixation. Nothing in the candle problem forbids using the box as a support."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is functional fixedness?", "options": ["Restricting the use of an object to its familiar function.", "Using the same method that worked on earlier problems.", "Breaking a problem into smaller subgoals to reach the goal.", "Suddenly realizing the answer after seeing the problem differently."], "correct": 0, "why": "Functional fixedness means restricting an object to its familiar function. Reusing an old method is a mental set, and subgoals belong to means-end analysis."}}, "4015": {"college": {"stem": "In the candle problem, some participants receive matchboxes filled with matches and others receive empty matchboxes; the empty-box group is twice as likely to solve it. What does this manipulation reveal?", "options": ["Presenting the box as a familiar container can keep people from seeing it as a possible candle support.", "Giving empty boxes removes the stated requirement that the candle burn without dripping wax onto the floor.", "The filled-box group has no box that could physically be attached to the corkboard at all.", "Giving empty boxes makes the task easier by supplying a smaller and lighter candle to mount."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both groups face the same goal with the same objects. Seeing the matchboxes as containers inhibited using them as supports, so removing the matches made the support use easier to notice."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the candle problem, people given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve it. Why?", "options": ["Boxes full of matches made people think of them only as containers.", "Empty boxes were larger and could hold more than one candle at once.", "People with empty boxes were also told the answer before starting.", "Full boxes were too heavy to attach to the corkboard with tacks."], "correct": 0, "why": "Seeing the matchboxes as containers inhibited using them as supports. Empty boxes weakened that familiar function, which is functional fixedness at work."}}, "4016": {"college": {"stem": "A solver empties the matchbox, tacks it to the corkboard, and stands the candle on it. Which change in thinking most directly overcomes functional fixedness here?", "options": ["The box is treated as a supporting platform rather than only as a container for the matches.", "The goal is changed from mounting the candle on the wall to standing it on the floor.", "Every object is assumed to keep exactly the use it had before the task began.", "The matchbox is treated as a brand-new tool that was missing from the original set of objects."], "correct": 0, "why": "The box was always among the objects given; the change is in the function assigned to it. The goal stays the same, and assuming each object keeps its usual use is the fixedness itself."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the solution to the candle problem?", "options": ["Empty the matchbox, tack it to the corkboard, and set the candle on it.", "Melt the bottom of the candle and stick it directly onto the floor below.", "Push the matches into the corkboard and balance the candle on them.", "Hold the candle against the corkboard until the wax begins to drip."], "correct": 0, "why": "The matchbox serves as a platform tacked to the corkboard. Seeing the box only as a container is what makes this hard to find."}}, "4017": {"college": {"stem": "An investigator argues that the empty-matchbox result shows functional fixedness causes every failure to solve any problem. Which conclusion actually fits the evidence?", "options": ["The result supports a role for the box's familiar container function in this candle task.", "The result shows that past experience cannot influence how people approach a water-jug problem.", "The result shows that every insight solution requires a box that is empty at the start.", "The result shows that the Tower of Hanoi can be solved without obeying its disk rules."], "correct": 0, "why": "The manipulation changed only how the box was offered within the candle problem, so it supports a functional-fixedness account of that task. It cannot establish a universal cause of failure or say anything about other tasks' rules."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the empty-matchbox finding in the candle problem show?", "options": ["An object's familiar use can block people from seeing other ways to use it.", "People solve every kind of problem faster when given fewer objects to work with.", "Past experience never affects how people approach a new problem.", "The candle problem can be solved only by people with prior training."], "correct": 0, "why": "Seeing the boxes as containers held people back from using them as supports, which is functional fixedness. The finding does not extend to every problem."}}, "4018": {"college": {"stem": "In the two-string problem, a participant holding one string cannot reach the other. A pair of pliers is in the room. Which use of the pliers solves the problem while keeping the goal intact?", "options": ["Tie the pliers to one string as a weight, swing it, and catch it while holding the other string.", "Keep the pliers for gripping and simply wait for the two hanging strings to drift close enough to reach.", "Use the pliers to cut one string down so only a single end remains to be tied.", "Set the pliers on the floor and count touching either string once as a success."], "correct": 0, "why": "Used as a weight, the pliers turn a string into a pendulum that swings within reach. Their familiar gripping function can hide that use, and cutting a string or redefining success abandons the goal."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the two-string problem, how can a pair of pliers help tie the two strings together?", "options": ["Tie them to one string and swing it like a pendulum.", "Use them to pull the ceiling attachment points closer together.", "Use them to cut both strings into shorter pieces first.", "Use them to grip one string more tightly while reaching."], "correct": 0, "why": "The pliers can serve as a weight, turning one string into a pendulum that swings within reach of the other."}}, "4019": {"college": {"stem": "In the two-string problem, what separates functional fixedness from the plain fact that the strings hang too far apart to grab at once?", "options": ["The distance is the physical obstacle; fixedness is seeing the pliers only as a tool, not as a weight.", "The distance itself proves functional fixedness, even if the solver immediately uses the pliers as a weight.", "Fixedness is a stated task rule that forbids attaching any object to either of the two strings.", "Fixedness disappears only when the strings hang close enough that no pendulum motion is needed."], "correct": 0, "why": "The distance creates the problem; functional fixedness is the representation of the pliers that blocks a useful response to it. The solution does not require changing the distance."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the two-string problem, what keeps people from seeing the solution?", "options": ["They think of the pliers only as a tool, not as a weight.", "They are not allowed to touch either string with their hands.", "They are given too many objects to choose from in the room.", "They do not know that the strings must be tied together."], "correct": 0, "why": "The familiar function of the pliers gets in the way of seeing them as a weight for a pendulum. That is functional fixedness."}}, "4020": {"college": {"stem": "During the two-string problem, the experimenter 'accidentally' sets one string swinging, and most participants then solve it. How does the Gestalt approach interpret this hint?", "options": ["It makes the pendulum idea available and lets solvers restructure the problem under the same goal.", "It changes the goal to describing the experimenter's action instead of tying the two strings together.", "It creates a new rule allowing the solver to move the strings' ceiling attachments closer together.", "It replaces each participant's own attempt with a complete spoken description of the answer."], "correct": 0, "why": "In Gestalt terms, the solution occurs when the solver restructures the representation; the swinging string points toward a pendulum. The goal and the room stay the same, and no answer is stated aloud."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the two-string problem, what happened after the experimenter 'accidentally' set a string in motion?", "options": ["Most participants were then able to solve the problem.", "Most participants gave up because the swinging string distracted them.", "Most participants asked to be given a different set of objects.", "Most participants said the problem was impossible to solve."], "correct": 0, "why": "Once the hint was given by setting the string in motion, most participants solved the problem, which Gestalt psychologists saw as restructuring."}}, "4021": {"college": {"stem": "Which situation illustrates a mental set rather than functional fixedness?", "options": ["A solver tackles a new water-jug problem with the same procedure that worked on several earlier ones.", "A solver cannot think of the matchbox as anything other than the container that originally held the matches.", "A solver thinks of the pliers only as a gripping tool and never considers their weight.", "A solver lists the legal disk moves allowed by the stated Tower of Hanoi rules."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental set is a preconceived approach based on past experience with similar problems. The matchbox and pliers cases restrict an object's use, which is functional fixedness, and listing legal moves is neither."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a mental set?", "options": ["A preconceived way of approaching a problem, based on past experience.", "Limiting an object to only its usual, familiar function.", "A sudden realization of the answer to a problem.", "The full set of states between the start and the goal."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental set is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones. Limiting an object's use is functional fixedness."}}, "4022": {"college": {"stem": "In a water-jug problem, jug A holds 21 quarts, jug B holds 127 quarts and jug C holds 3 quarts, and the target is 100 quarts. Which sequence produces the target?", "options": ["Fill B, pour off one A-sized amount, then pour off two C-sized amounts: 127 - 21 - 3 - 3.", "Fill A, add one C-sized amount, and treat the result as equal to the full capacity of B.", "Fill B and pour off one C-sized amount, treating the 124 quarts that remain as the 100 required.", "Combine A and B, then pour off C once, giving 21 + 127 - 3 as the target amount."], "correct": 0, "why": "B - A - 2C takes 127 to 106, then 103, then 100 quarts. The other sequences either stop at the wrong amount or add jugs together instead of measuring out the target."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a water-jug problem, jug A holds 21, jug B holds 127 and jug C holds 3. How do you get exactly 100?", "options": ["Fill B, then pour out A once and C twice.", "Fill A, then pour in C once and B once.", "Fill B, then pour out C once and stop there.", "Fill A and C together, then add them to B."], "correct": 0, "why": "127 - 21 = 106, then 106 - 3 = 103, then 103 - 3 = 100. This is the B - A - 2C formula."}}, "4023": {"college": {"stem": "After solving several problems with B - A - 2C, a participant gets jug A = 15, B = 39, C = 3 and a target of 18. The familiar formula works, but the participant never notices A + C. What does this show?", "options": ["The earlier successful procedure keeps guiding the approach and blocks recognition of the simpler 15 + 3.", "The participant made an arithmetic error, because 39 - 15 - 6 does not equal 18.", "The target has no solution unless a new 18-quart jug is physically added to the set.", "The participant shows functional fixedness only because water is held in jugs rather than other containers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both 39 - 15 - 6 and 15 + 3 give 18, but the second is simpler. A mental set from earlier success inhibited participants from using the simpler solution."}, "simple": {"stem": "After many water-jug problems solved with B - A - 2C, people often missed a simpler solution on later problems. What causes this?", "options": ["A mental set built from past success with the longer method.", "Functional fixedness about the familiar uses of each jug.", "A sudden insight that the later problems had no solution.", "Means-end analysis that splits each of the problems into subgoals."], "correct": 0, "why": "Repeated success with B - A - 2C created a mental set, a preconceived approach based on past experience, which inhibited use of the simpler solution."}}, "4024": {"college": {"stem": "To test whether repeated water-jug practice creates a mental set, which comparison is most informative?", "options": ["Compare use of an available simpler solution by people with and without prior B - A - 2C practice.", "Compare the number of letters in the jug labels while keeping every participant's practice history identical.", "Give one group a solvable problem and another an unsolvable one, then credit any difference to practice.", "Change all jug capacities and targets between groups without keeping a simpler solution available."], "correct": 0, "why": "The proposed cause is experience with a successful procedure, so practice must vary while a simpler solution stays available to both groups. Changing solvability or the problems themselves would confound the comparison."}, "simple": {"stem": "How could a researcher show that practice on water-jug problems created a mental set?", "options": ["Show that practiced people miss a simpler solution more often than unpracticed people.", "Show that practiced people solve every problem faster than unpracticed people.", "Show that unpracticed people cannot solve any of the water-jug problems.", "Show that all people use the same formula on the very first problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental set shows up when past practice makes people overlook a simpler solution, so the key comparison is practiced versus unpracticed solvers on a problem with a simpler answer."}}, "4025": {"college": {"stem": "Newell and Simon (1956), who developed the Logic Theorist, described problem solving as a search. Beyond the starting conditions and the final answer, what does their problem space include?", "options": ["The intermediate states and possible routes linking the initial state to the goal state.", "Only the solver's final warmth rating once the answer has been given aloud.", "Only a sudden perceptual change, ignoring the states passed through during the attempt.", "The physical look of the objects, without saying what would count as reaching the goal."], "correct": 0, "why": "Newell and Simon described problem solving as a search that occurs between the posing of the problem and its solution. The problem space includes the initial state, intermediate states and the goal state."}, "simple": {"stem": "Newell and Simon (1956) described problem solving as a search. What are the three kinds of states in a problem space?", "options": ["The initial state, intermediate states, and the goal state.", "The fixation state, the insight state, and the solution state.", "The perception state, the restructuring state, and the Aha state.", "The cold state, the warm state, and the solved state."], "correct": 0, "why": "A problem space has an initial state (conditions at the beginning), intermediate states, and a goal state (the solution)."}}, "4026": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher records a puzzle's starting board, every temporary arrangement reached during the attempt, and the required final board. In Newell and Simon's (1956) terms, what are the temporary arrangements?", "options": ["Intermediate states that can form part of a route from the initial state to the goal state.", "Operators, because a state and the rule that changes it are the same concept.", "Additional goal states, because every arrangement reached along the way satisfies the original task.", "Warmth judgments, because tile positions directly report the solver's felt closeness."], "correct": 0, "why": "Situations reached along the way are intermediate states. Operators are the rules that move the problem from one state to another, and a temporary arrangement does not satisfy the goal."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a problem space, what is the goal state?", "options": ["The solution of the problem, where the solver wants to end up.", "The conditions at the very beginning of the problem, before any moves.", "Any arrangement reached partway through solving the problem.", "A rule that says which moves are allowed or not."], "correct": 0, "why": "The goal state is the solution of the problem. The beginning conditions are the initial state, partway arrangements are intermediate states, and move rules are operators."}}, "4027": {"college": {"stem": "Which statement correctly contrasts the Gestalt idea of restructuring with Newell and Simon's (1956) search account?", "options": ["Restructuring reorganizes a mental representation; search maps routes through initial, intermediate and goal states.", "Restructuring physically alters the puzzle objects themselves; search concerns only the solver's final spoken answer.", "Both require every solution to feel sudden, so both predict the same warmth pattern for algebra.", "Search removes the need to represent a goal; restructuring involves no inner representation at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Gestalt psychologists focused on reorganizing the problem's representation, while Newell and Simon described a search between the posing of the problem and its solution. Neither claim involves physical change to the objects."}, "simple": {"stem": "How did Newell and Simon's (1956) approach to problem solving differ from the Gestalt approach?", "options": ["It treated problem solving as a step-by-step search from start to goal.", "It treated problem solving as a sudden 'Aha' with no steps at all in between.", "It treated problem solving as purely a matter of luck and guessing.", "It treated problem solving as perception of physical objects only."], "correct": 0, "why": "Instead of looking only at the initial structure and the restructured one, Newell and Simon described problem solving as a search between the problem and its solution."}}, "4028": {"college": {"stem": "In the Tower of Hanoi, a solver wants to move the bottom disk while two smaller disks still sit on top of it. Which rule forbids this move?", "options": ["A disk can be moved only when there are no other disks resting on top of it.", "A disk can be moved only if it is the largest disk anywhere in the puzzle.", "Every disk must go directly from the first peg to the goal peg, never using the middle peg.", "A smaller disk is never allowed to be placed on top of a larger disk."], "correct": 0, "why": "Only a disk with nothing on top of it can move. The real rules allow smaller disks on larger ones and allow use of the middle peg."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which of these is one of the Tower of Hanoi rules?", "options": ["A larger disk can never be placed on a smaller disk.", "A smaller disk can never be placed on a larger disk.", "Two disks may be moved together if both are on top.", "Every disk must be moved directly to the goal peg."], "correct": 0, "why": "A larger disk cannot be placed on top of a smaller disk. Disks are also moved one at a time, and intermediate pegs may be used."}}, "4029": {"college": {"stem": "A participant lifts a stack of three disks together, sets it on the goal peg, and ends with the correct final arrangement. Why is this not a valid Tower of Hanoi solution?", "options": ["The move breaks the one-disk-at-a-time rule, even though the final arrangement matches the goal.", "Reaching the goal makes any physically possible movement count as an allowed operator.", "The goal requires the disks to stay on the starting peg, so moving any of the disks at all is strictly forbidden.", "The rules forbid a small disk on a large one, no matter how the stack was moved."], "correct": 0, "why": "Operators constrain how one state may change into another, not just what the end looks like. Moving the whole stack at once violates the rule that disks are moved one at a time."}, "simple": {"stem": "In problem solving, what are operators?", "options": ["Rules that say which moves are allowed and which are not.", "The conditions at the start of the problem before any move.", "Sudden realizations that reveal the solution to the problem.", "The final arrangement that the solver is trying to reach."], "correct": 0, "why": "Operators are rules specifying which moves are allowed; they take the problem from one state to another, such as the one-disk-at-a-time rule in the Tower of Hanoi."}}, "4030": {"college": {"stem": "Which proposed Tower of Hanoi move is legal?", "options": ["Moving a single smaller top disk onto a peg whose top disk is larger.", "Moving a larger disk onto a smaller one because the result is nearer the goal peg.", "Moving a disk that still has a smaller disk resting on top of it.", "Moving two top disks together because both must end up on the same peg."], "correct": 0, "why": "A smaller disk may rest on a larger one. Progress toward the goal does not override the rules against a larger disk on a smaller one, moving a disk with another on top, or moving two disks at once."}, "simple": {"stem": "A Tower of Hanoi solver wants to move a small top disk onto a peg whose top disk is larger. Is this allowed?", "options": ["Yes, because a smaller disk may sit on a larger disk.", "No, because a smaller disk may never sit on a larger disk.", "No, because disks may be moved only to the goal peg.", "Yes, but only if two disks are moved together at once."], "correct": 0, "why": "The rule only forbids a larger disk on a smaller one, so a smaller disk may go on a larger one, one disk at a time."}}, "4031": {"college": {"stem": "A 64-disk Tower of Hanoi would take an enormous amount of time to finish even if every move were correct and took only one second. What feature of the task explains this?", "options": ["The rules force many repeated intermediate rearrangements of the smaller disks along the way.", "The solver must wait a fixed and fairly long interval between moves, even when each move is correct.", "Adding disks removes the rule against placing a larger disk on a smaller one.", "The goal state becomes undefined once the puzzle has more than three disks."], "correct": 0, "why": "Because only one exposed disk can move and larger disks cannot rest on smaller ones, the number of required moves grows enormously as disks are added. Fast moves cannot make up for that many moves."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why would a 64-disk Tower of Hanoi take an enormously long time, even at one correct move per second?", "options": ["The rules require a huge number of moves as disks are added.", "Each move must be checked by an experimenter before the next one.", "Larger towers allow disks to be placed in any order at all.", "The goal state changes every time another disk is moved."], "correct": 0, "why": "The disk rules force many intermediate moves, and the number of moves grows enormously as more disks are added."}}, "4032": {"college": {"stem": "Two 8-tile boards hold the same numbered tiles, but only one has the empty space then 1 and 2 across the top row, 3-4-5 in the middle row, and 6-7-8 in the bottom row. Why is only that board the goal state?", "options": ["A state is defined by where each tile and the empty space are, not just which numbers are present.", "A goal state is any board that contains all eight numbers, whatever positions they happen to occupy on it.", "The number of tiles on the board must increase as the solver approaches the goal.", "The empty space is left out of state descriptions because it holds no numbered tile."], "correct": 0, "why": "The task requires one specific arrangement of the same tiles. Tile positions and the location of the empty space distinguish states, so having all eight numbers is not enough."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the 8-tile puzzle, how does the initial state differ from the goal state?", "options": ["They have the same tiles arranged in different positions.", "The goal state has more tiles than the initial state.", "The initial state has no empty space on the board.", "The goal state uses a larger board than the initial state."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both boards have the same eight tiles and one empty space; only the arrangement differs."}}, "4033": {"college": {"stem": "In the search tree for the 8-tile puzzle, the first board has its empty space in the bottom-middle position, and moving each adjacent tile into that space produces three branches. What does each branch represent?", "options": ["A legal transition from one board state to a possible next state.", "A report that the solver now feels one point warmer than before.", "A change in the goal requirement each time another tile is moved.", "A separate physical puzzle that uses a different set of numbered tiles."], "correct": 0, "why": "Branches connect arrangements reachable by one legal move. The tile set and the goal stay the same, and a branch is not a warmth judgment."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a search tree for the 8-tile puzzle, what does each board drawn below another board show?", "options": ["A state that can be reached from the one above by one legal move.", "A completely new puzzle with its own different set of numbered tiles.", "The goal state that the solver is trying to reach in the end.", "A board arrangement that breaks the rules of the puzzle."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each lower board is a state reached by one legal move from the board above it; the tree maps the problem space."}}, "4034": {"college": {"stem": "A drawn search tree for the 8-tile puzzle links two boards that differ only in two numbered tiles trading places, with the empty space unchanged. What must be checked before counting that link as part of a search route?", "options": ["Whether the puzzle's allowed moves can actually produce that change in a single step.", "Whether both boards contain the same number of tiles, since that alone guarantees a legal move.", "Whether the later board is drawn lower in the diagram, since vertical position establishes legality.", "Whether the solver calls the link an insight, since that label makes the transition legal."], "correct": 0, "why": "A route through a problem space is limited by the operators. A plausible-looking successor with the same tiles is not a legal next state unless an allowed move produces it."}, "simple": {"stem": "In a problem space, what decides which states can be connected to each other?", "options": ["The operators, meaning the rules for which moves are allowed.", "The solver's feelings of warmth at each point in time.", "How similar the two states happen to look to the person solving.", "Whether the solver has seen the same puzzle before."], "correct": 0, "why": "Operators are the rules that take the problem from one state to another, so they determine which states connect."}}, "4035": {"college": {"stem": "Which description best fits means-end analysis as Newell and Simon (1956) described it?", "options": ["Search by reducing differences between the current state and the goal, using subgoals as stepping stones.", "Pick an object's most familiar use and rule out any other use for the entire attempt.", "Record only the final answer and skip the states that made reaching it possible.", "Reuse the first successful procedure on every later problem without ever checking whether it still fits the problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Means-end analysis reduces the difference between initial and goal states by creating subgoals, intermediate states closer to the goal. The other options describe functional fixedness, ignoring the search, and a mental set."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is means-end analysis?", "options": ["Reducing the difference between start and goal by setting subgoals.", "Waiting for the answer to come suddenly in an 'Aha' moment.", "Using an object only for the job it is usually used for.", "Saying your thoughts aloud while you work on a problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Means-end analysis reduces the difference between the initial and goal states, using subgoals that create intermediate states closer to the goal."}}, "4036": {"college": {"stem": "In Newell and Simon's (1956) account, a Tower of Hanoi solver first works out how to free the large disk before trying to move it to the third peg. Why is freeing the disk a subgoal?", "options": ["It creates an intermediate condition that a required move toward the overall goal depends on.", "It is the complete goal itself, so the final arrangement on the third peg no longer matters at all.", "It suspends the operator rules until the large disk has reached its final position.", "It turns the puzzle into one with no initial state and no intermediate states."], "correct": 0, "why": "A subgoal creates an intermediate state closer to the goal by removing an obstacle to a needed move. It does not replace the final goal or suspend the move rules."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Newell and Simon's (1956) means-end analysis of the Tower of Hanoi, what is a subgoal?", "options": ["An intermediate state that brings the solver closer to the goal.", "The final arrangement of all disks on the third peg.", "A rule saying a larger disk cannot sit on a smaller one.", "A sudden realization that shows the whole solution to the solver at once."], "correct": 0, "why": "Subgoals create intermediate states closer to the goal, such as freeing up the large disk. The final arrangement is the goal state, and move rules are operators."}}, "4037": {"college": {"stem": "A Tower of Hanoi solver has freed the largest disk on the first peg, but a smaller disk still sits on the third peg. What subgoal must be met before the largest disk can go to the third peg?", "options": ["Free up the third peg so the largest disk will not land on top of a smaller disk.", "Rebuild the entire starting stack on the first peg before making any further moves.", "Move the smaller disk and the largest disk together as a single allowed move.", "Treat the third peg as permanently off-limits so the largest disk never has to move."], "correct": 0, "why": "Freeing the large disk meets one constraint, but its destination must also allow the move. Clearing the third peg is the next subgoal under the rule against a larger disk on a smaller one."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Tower of Hanoi, what are the three subgoals for moving the large disk to the third peg, in order?", "options": ["Free up the large disk, free up the third peg, then move the large disk.", "Free up the third peg, move the large disk, then free up the large disk.", "Move the large disk, free up the large disk, then free up the third peg.", "Move all disks together, free up the third peg, then move the large disk."], "correct": 0, "why": "Subgoal 1 frees up the large disk, subgoal 2 frees up the third peg, and subgoal 3 moves the large disk onto the third peg."}}, "4038": {"college": {"stem": "While solving the Tower of Hanoi, a person moves a small disk to the middle peg instead of the goal peg. How does means-end analysis explain this move?", "options": ["The temporary move can free a needed disk or peg and so contribute to a route to the goal.", "Any move away from the goal peg is illegal, because every subgoal must already be a finished solution.", "Reaching a temporary state removes the need to return the small disk to the finished stack.", "Using the middle peg shows functional fixedness, because no object is given an unusual function."], "correct": 0, "why": "Subgoals often require temporary arrangements that make a later move possible. A move is useful if it removes an obstacle on the route to the goal, while the operators still apply."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why might a Tower of Hanoi solver move a disk away from the goal peg?", "options": ["To reach a subgoal that makes a later needed move possible.", "Because the rules require every disk to visit the middle peg.", "Because moving toward the goal peg is not allowed at first.", "Because the solver has given up on reaching the goal state."], "correct": 0, "why": "In means-end analysis, subgoals such as freeing up a peg can require moves that seem to lead away from the goal but make a later move possible."}}, "4039": {"college": {"stem": "Two versions of the mutilated-checkerboard problem keep the same task but draw attention to different features of the squares. What principle predicts that they may differ in difficulty?", "options": ["How a problem is stated can shape its representation and make a key relationship easier to see.", "Difficulty depends entirely on the total number of squares, regardless of how the board is shown.", "Any change in wording creates a different physical board with a different number of legal placements.", "Once the goal is stated, people stop forming any mental representation of the board at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Beyond specifying states and pathways, how a problem is stated can affect its difficulty. Versions of the same problem can differ in how readily the relevant constraint is noticed."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the mutilated-checkerboard problem show about problem solving?", "options": ["How a problem is stated can change how hard it is.", "Larger boards are always harder to solve than smaller ones.", "People solve problems faster when given no extra information.", "Every problem can be solved by trying all possible moves."], "correct": 0, "why": "The same problem was easier when information pointed toward the correct representation, showing that how a problem is stated affects its difficulty."}}, "4040": {"college": {"stem": "Someone argues that 31 dominoes must be able to cover the mutilated checkerboard because 31 x 2 = 62, the number of squares left after two opposite corners are removed. What relationship does this argument miss?", "options": ["Each domino spans one square of each color, but the removed corners share a color.", "Each domino can span only two same-colored squares, so even a full board cannot be tiled.", "Removing two corners still leaves 64 squares, so the area count is the only mistake.", "Dominoes can be placed only on corner squares, so none fit once a corner is removed."], "correct": 0, "why": "The area matches, but every domino needs one square of each color. Removing two same-colored corners leaves unequal numbers of the two colors, so no tiling is possible."}, "simple": {"stem": "Two opposite corners are removed from a checkerboard. Why can't 31 dominoes cover the remaining 62 squares?", "options": ["Each domino needs one square of each color, and both removed corners match.", "There are too few squares left for 31 dominoes to fit.", "Dominoes cannot be placed next to the edge of the board.", "Each domino covers three squares, so 31 is not enough."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each domino covers two squares of different colors. Removing two corners of the same color leaves unequal color counts, so the board cannot be tiled."}}, "4041": {"college": {"stem": "Which observation is enough to rule out any complete tiling of the mutilated checkerboard without trying every arrangement of dominoes?", "options": ["The removed corners are the same color, and every domino must sit on one square of each color.", "The first arrangement one solver tries leaves two squares without a domino on them.", "The solver reports low warmth during the first 15 seconds of the attempt.", "The remaining board has an even total number of squares left to tile."], "correct": 0, "why": "A property of every legal domino placement makes the unequal color counts decisive. One failed attempt cannot rule out all arrangements, and an even square count is necessary but not sufficient."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the key to seeing that the mutilated checkerboard cannot be fully tiled with dominoes?", "options": ["Each domino covers two squares, and those squares must differ in color.", "Each domino covers two squares, and those two squares must match in color.", "Each domino must be placed along the outer edge of the board.", "Each domino can go on a corner square only if placed first."], "correct": 0, "why": "The key is that each domino covers two squares of different colors, so removing two same-colored corners makes a full tiling impossible."}}, "4042": {"college": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard study, conditions used a blank board, a colored board, the words black and pink, or the words bread and butter. What had to stay the same across conditions for the comparison to concern representation?", "options": ["The removed-corner layout and the rule that each domino covers two adjacent squares.", "The exact visual and verbal labels that the conditions were designed to vary.", "A different number of dominoes for each group, so that every group faces its own task.", "Permission to place dominoes on adjacent squares, granted only to the bread-and-butter group."], "correct": 0, "why": "The conditions differed only in how much information the board gave about the squares. Changing the layout or the placement rule would add another reason for differences in difficulty."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard study, how did the four conditions differ?", "options": ["In how much information the board gave about the squares.", "In how many corner squares were removed from the board.", "In how many dominoes participants were allowed to use.", "In how much time participants were given to find the answer."], "correct": 0, "why": "The conditions were a blank board, a colored board, the words black and pink, and the words bread and butter; they differed in how much information they gave about the squares."}}, "4043": {"college": {"stem": "The words 'bread' and 'butter' add no squares and no dominoes to the checkerboard. Why can labeling the squares this way still help someone solve it?", "options": ["The labels make the two kinds of square, and the need to pair one of each, easier to notice.", "The labels change the placement rule so that two squares bearing the same label may be paired legally.", "The labels erase the difference between square types so that only total area matters.", "The labels allow a domino to be split into two halves that fill isolated squares."], "correct": 0, "why": "Bread and butter emphasize that the squares differ and naturally go together in pairs. The physical constraint is unchanged; what changes is how easily the pairing relationship enters the representation."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why did 'bread' and 'butter' labels on the checkerboard make the problem easier?", "options": ["They stressed that squares come in two kinds that go together in pairs.", "They gave participants the full answer written directly on the board itself.", "They removed the two corner squares in a more visible way.", "They made the board look smaller and less tiring to study."], "correct": 0, "why": "The bread-and-butter condition emphasized the difference between the squares, pointing toward the correct representation of the problem."}}, "4044": {"college": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard study, participants given the bread-and-butter board solved the problem twice as fast as those given the blank board. Which interpretation does this support?", "options": ["Information that points toward the right square-pairing representation can reduce solving time.", "The words create a physical tiling of a board that could not be tiled at all in the blank condition.", "Verbal labels improve performance on every possible problem by exactly the same amount.", "Solvers never notice visual color differences on a board unless written words are added."], "correct": 0, "why": "The board still cannot be tiled; what becomes easier is seeing why. The result concerns access to a useful representation, not a change in solvability or a universal effect of labels."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard study, how did the bread-and-butter condition compare with the blank-board condition?", "options": ["It was solved about twice as fast as the blank board.", "It was solved about half as fast as the blank board.", "It was solved at the same speed as the blank board.", "It was never solved by any of the participants at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants solved the bread-and-butter version twice as fast as the blank version, because the labels pointed toward the correct representation."}}, "4045": {"college": {"stem": "Which instruction matches the think-aloud protocol used to study problem solving?", "options": ["Say each new thought as it comes during the attempt instead of narrating your physical actions.", "Stay silent until you reach an answer, then build a smooth account of how you must have found it.", "Report only how many dominoes you have moved and never mention any thought about the labels.", "Repeat one fixed sentence throughout so that every participant produces the same verbal record."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants are told not to describe what they are doing but to verbalize new thoughts, so the record shows what information they are attending to. A narration of actions would miss changes in representation."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a think-aloud protocol?", "options": ["Saying your thoughts out loud while solving a problem.", "Writing down the final answer after solving a problem.", "Describing your physical movements while solving a problem.", "Listening to another person explain how to solve a problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "In a think-aloud protocol, people say aloud what they are thinking while solving a problem, verbalizing new thoughts rather than describing their actions."}}, "4046": {"college": {"stem": "A checkerboard solver first talks about open spaces, then says that 'bread' has to go with 'butter' and sees why the board cannot be tiled. How is this change best interpreted?", "options": ["A representational shift brings the relationship between the two kinds of square into the solver's reasoning.", "The solver has changed the operator so that one domino may legally sit on two bread squares.", "The spoken report shows that the board itself changed color partway through the attempt.", "The report is only a list of physical actions and reveals nothing whatsoever about the solver's thinking or reasoning."], "correct": 0, "why": "The content shifts from positions to the required pairing of square types, a shift in how elements of the problem are perceived. This resembles the Gestalt idea of representational shift; the rules and board stay the same."}, "simple": {"stem": "Think-aloud records showed that solvers got the checkerboard problem after realizing that 'bread' and 'butter' mattered. This resembles which Gestalt idea?", "options": ["Restructuring, a shift in how the problem is represented.", "Functional fixedness, limiting an object to its usual use.", "Mental set, relying on a method that worked before.", "Means-end analysis, breaking the problem into subgoals."], "correct": 0, "why": "The realization reflects a shift in how elements of the problem are perceived, similar to the Gestalt idea of representational shift, or restructuring."}}, "4047": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to know at what point the square-pairing idea enters a participant's reasoning on the checkerboard problem. Which method is best suited?", "options": ["A think-aloud record of the participant's new thoughts throughout the checkerboard attempt.", "A count of the total squares on the board, taken before anyone begins solving.", "A single final success score with no information from the period during solving.", "The jug capacities from a separate water-jug problem solved by another participant."], "correct": 0, "why": "A think-aloud protocol captures thoughts during the attempt and can show when the bread-and-butter relationship is first mentioned. A final score or square count cannot locate that moment."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the goal of having people think aloud while solving a problem?", "options": ["To find out what information the solver is attending to.", "To make the solver finish the problem more quickly.", "To give the solver hints about the right answer.", "To test how well the solver can speak clearly in front of others."], "correct": 0, "why": "The goal of a think-aloud protocol is to learn what information the solver is attending to while solving the problem."}}, "4048": {"college": {"stem": "Someone interprets a warmth rating of 7 in Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) study as a count of how many legal moves remain. Why is that interpretation mistaken?", "options": ["Warmth is the participant's felt closeness, which can differ from how near an insight solution really is.", "Warmth records the exact number of operators, but only in the noninsight algebra condition.", "Warmth is a property of the physical chain links themselves rather than a judgment that the participant makes.", "Warmth can be recorded only after success, so it says nothing about the period before solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "Warmth ratings are subjective reports of closeness taken every 15 seconds. The insight result shows low felt closeness shortly before a solution, so a rating is not an objective count of remaining moves."}, "simple": {"stem": "On Metcalfe and Wiebe's (1987) warmth scale, what did a rating of 7 mean?", "options": ["The participant had solved the problem.", "The participant felt completely cold, far from the answer.", "The participant had seven moves left before solving it.", "The participant had made seven errors so far."], "correct": 0, "why": "The scale ran from 0 (cold) to 7 (solved), so a rating of 7 marked the solution. Warmth is felt closeness, not a count of moves or errors."}}, "4049": {"college": {"stem": "A solver practiced on B - A - 2C now gets jug A = 28, B = 59, C = 3 with a target of 25. Which response shows that the mental set has been overcome?", "options": ["Use A - C, so 28 - 3 = 25, instead of the longer familiar three-jug sequence.", "Reject A - C on the grounds that any method using fewer than three jugs is not allowed.", "Change the target to 28 so that no measuring step has to be carried out at all.", "Apply B - A - C and treat 59 - 28 - 3 as if it equaled the required 25."], "correct": 0, "why": "A - C reaches 25 directly. B - A - 2C (59 - 28 - 6) also gives 25, but practice with it can inhibit seeing the simpler method; no rule requires all three jugs, and B - A - C gives 28."}, "simple": {"stem": "With jug A = 28, B = 59, C = 3 and a target of 25, what simpler solution do people with a mental set tend to miss?", "options": ["Fill A and pour out C once, 28 - 3 = 25.", "Fill B and pour out A once, 59 - 28 = 31.", "Fill A and add C twice, 28 + 3 + 3 = 34.", "Fill C eight times and add, 3 x 8 = 24."], "correct": 0, "why": "A - C gives 25 in one step. People practiced on B - A - 2C tend to keep using the longer formula and miss this simpler route."}}, "4050": {"college": {"stem": "A student repeats the checkerboard task but removes one square of each color instead of two same-colored corners. What happens to the original impossibility argument?", "options": ["It no longer applies, because removing one square of each color leaves equal numbers of both colors.", "It still applies unchanged, because removing any two squares from the board always leaves unequal color counts.", "It now shows that each domino must cover two squares of the same color instead.", "It is replaced by proof that any even-numbered board can be tiled in every arrangement."], "correct": 0, "why": "The impossibility argument depends on removing two squares of the same color. Removing one of each keeps the color counts equal, so this particular reason for impossibility disappears; equal counts alone do not prove a tiling exists."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why does it matter that the two corners removed from the mutilated checkerboard are the same color?", "options": ["It leaves more squares of one color than the other.", "It leaves an odd number of squares on the board.", "It makes the board too small to hold any dominoes.", "It means each domino now covers squares of one color."], "correct": 0, "why": "Since each domino covers one square of each color, removing two same-colored corners leaves an unequal number of each color, so the board cannot be fully tiled. The 62 remaining squares are an even number."}}, "5001": {"college": {"stem": "Two versions of the mutilated-checkerboard problem describe an identical board, the same two removed corners and the same goal of tiling it with dominoes, but only one version stresses that each domino lies on one square of each color. Why can the second version be easier to solve?", "options": ["Stating the problem that way makes the one-of-each-color pairing constraint easier to represent.", "Identical starting and goal states force every solver to follow the same sequence of moves regardless of wording.", "Emphasizing color physically alters how many squares remain on the board to be tiled.", "Once a goal is stated, solvers no longer need to represent any relationships among the problem's elements."], "correct": 0, "why": "How a problem is stated can affect its difficulty by changing which relationships the solver represents. The board is physically unchanged, and the same start and goal do not dictate one route."}, "simple": {"stem": "Two descriptions of the same puzzle use the same board and goal but emphasize different details. Why might one description be easier to solve?", "options": ["The way a problem is stated can make the key idea easier to notice.", "Puzzles with the same start and goal are always exactly equally hard.", "Changing the wording changes the actual pieces of the puzzle itself.", "Once the goal is stated, the wording of the problem no longer matters."], "correct": 0, "why": "How a problem is stated can affect its difficulty, because wording changes what the solver notices. The puzzle itself stays the same."}}, "5002": {"college": {"stem": "In the Russian marriage problem, 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women were matched into 32 marriages, and then two bachelors died. Someone argues that 31 heterosexual marriages are possible because the 62 survivors divide evenly into 31 pairs. What does this argument ignore?", "options": ["Every marriage needs one man and one woman, and only 30 men remain to fill that role.", "All existing marriages must remain intact, even though the matchmaker is free to rearrange the surviving villagers.", "The matchmaker's actual goal is 32 marriages rather than the 31 that the problem asks about.", "Survivors whose earlier partnerships ended can no longer be counted toward any new marriage."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sixty-two people is enough for 31 pairs by headcount, but each pair must contain one man and one woman, and only 30 men survive. No rearrangement removes the 30-versus-32 imbalance."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Russian marriage problem, two of the 32 bachelors die, leaving 30 men and 32 women. Why can't the matchmaker arrange 31 heterosexual marriages?", "options": ["Each marriage needs one man, and only 30 men are left.", "The matchmaker is not allowed to rearrange any existing couples.", "Sixty-two people cannot be split evenly into pairs of two.", "The problem requires 32 marriages rather than 31 marriages."], "correct": 0, "why": "With only 30 men, at most 30 man-woman pairs are possible. Sixty-two people do split evenly into pairs, but not into 31 pairs that each contain one man and one woman."}}, "5003": {"college": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard problem, two opposite corner squares of the same color are removed, and each domino must lie on two adjacent squares of different colors. Why will no amount of rearranging the dominoes produce a complete tiling?", "options": ["Every possible placement still pairs one square of each color, but the two colors now have unequal counts.", "Rearranging the dominoes changes how many squares of each color are on the board before tiling even begins.", "Only the first domino placed causes the difficulty, so a different first placement would solve the problem.", "Removing two squares leaves an odd number of squares, so no domino can be placed anywhere on the board."], "correct": 0, "why": "The 62 remaining squares are an even number, so area is not the barrier. The barrier is 32 squares of one color and 30 of the other under a one-of-each-color rule, which holds for every arrangement."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the mutilated-checkerboard problem, two corner squares of the same color are removed. Why can dominoes never tile the remaining board?", "options": ["Each domino covers one square of each color, but the colors are now unequal.", "The remaining board has an odd number of squares left on it.", "The dominoes are too large to fit along the edges and corners of the board.", "Only the very first domino placement causes the whole problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each domino needs one square of each color, and removing two same-color squares leaves 32 of one color and 30 of the other. The 62 squares are an even number, so the count is not the issue."}}, "5004": {"college": {"stem": "A solver who has already worked through the Russian marriage problem uses its logic to see why the mutilated checkerboard cannot be tiled. Which description of the problems' roles is correct?", "options": ["The marriage problem serves as source, the checkerboard as target, and carrying the pairing logic across is analogical transfer.", "The checkerboard serves as source because it is the problem currently being worked on, and the marriage problem is therefore the target.", "Both problems count as targets, since a source problem must contain the same physical objects as the new problem.", "The marriage problem is merely a surface feature of the board, so no relationship between categories is transferred."], "correct": 0, "why": "The target is the problem being solved now; a source problem is another problem that shares some similarities with it. Moving the solution from one to the other is analogical transfer, even though people and squares are different objects."}, "simple": {"stem": "In analogical problem solving, what is the target problem?", "options": ["It is the new problem that the person is currently trying to solve.", "It is the earlier problem whose solution the person already knows.", "It is any problem that contains exactly the same objects as another.", "It is the final answer reached after an analogy has been used."], "correct": 0, "why": "The target problem is the one being solved. A source problem is a different, similar problem whose solution can guide the solution to the target."}}, "5005": {"college": {"stem": "In Duncker's radiation problem, a high-intensity ray would destroy the stomach tumor but also the healthy tissue along its path, whereas a low-intensity ray would spare the tissue but leave the tumor intact. Which procedure satisfies both constraints?", "options": ["Aim several low-intensity rays from different directions so that they converge on the tumor at once.", "Send one high-intensity ray along a single path and rely on its strength dropping just before it reaches the tumor.", "Use a single low-intensity ray on its own, without combining its effect with any other ray.", "Spread weak rays across different parts of the body so that they never meet at the tumor."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each path carries only a harmless low intensity, while simultaneous convergence adds up to a destructive intensity at the tumor. Any single ray, strong or weak, fails one of the two constraints."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the solution to Duncker's radiation problem?", "options": ["Several weak rays aimed from different directions meet at the tumor.", "One very strong ray is aimed straight at the tumor.", "The doctor operates first and then uses one weak ray.", "Weak rays are spread out across the body so they never reach the tumor together."], "correct": 0, "why": "Weak rays spare the healthy tissue they pass through, and meeting at the tumor gives a strong combined effect there. One strong ray would destroy healthy tissue, and operating is impossible."}}, "5006": {"college": {"stem": "In the fortress story, the general's whole army could capture the fortress, but any large force on a road would set off the mines, while small groups could pass safely. What makes a successful attack possible?", "options": ["Small groups travel along different roads and arrive together, combining into a full-strength force at the fortress.", "The full army marches down one road after the general confirms that the fortress is the true objective.", "Each small group can capture the fortress entirely on its own, so the timing and coordination of the groups' arrival do not matter.", "The general must first weaken the fortress from a distance before dividing the army in any way."], "correct": 0, "why": "Dividing the army keeps each road's traffic small enough to avoid the mines, and simultaneous arrival restores the full force at the fortress. A single small group would not be strong enough alone."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the fortress story, how could the general capture the fortress without setting off the mines?", "options": ["Split the army into small groups that come down different roads at once.", "Send the whole army down the one road that the dictator left without mines.", "Wait until the dictator removes the mines from all of the roads.", "Send one small group, which can capture the fortress by itself."], "correct": 0, "why": "Small groups can cross the mines safely, and arriving together gives the full army's strength at the fortress. Every road was mined, and one small group would be too weak."}}, "5007": {"college": {"stem": "When the fortress story is mapped onto the radiation problem, which correspondence keeps the relationship that produces the solution?", "options": ["The small groups of soldiers correspond to low-intensity rays that combine at the central target.", "The surrounding villages correspond to the tumor, since both have to be destroyed for the plan to succeed.", "The fortress corresponds to a single incoming ray, because each of them travels along a road.", "The full army on one road corresponds to several weak rays coming in from different directions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both problems share separate, safe contributions that converge into a strong effect at the center. The villages correspond to healthy tissue rather than the tumor, and the full army on one road corresponds to one strong ray."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the analogy between the fortress story and the radiation problem, what do the small groups of soldiers correspond to?", "options": ["They correspond to the weak rays that meet at the tumor.", "They correspond to the tumor that must be destroyed.", "They correspond to the healthy tissue that must be protected.", "They correspond to the doctor who chooses the treatment."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each small group, like each weak ray, is harmless on its own path but strong when combined at the target. The fortress, not the soldiers, corresponds to the tumor."}}, "5008": {"college": {"stem": "In Gick and Holyoak's research on the radiation problem, about 30% of participants solved it after encountering the fortress story, but about 75% did when told to think about that story. Which step of analogical problem solving does this gap most directly point to?", "options": ["Noticing that a known story is relevant to a new problem can fail even after the story has been encountered.", "The fortress story contains a usable solution only if the hint changes the way the story ends.", "Encountering any source story automatically guarantees that its solution will be recognized and then applied to the target problem.", "The hint makes mapping unnecessary, since the tumor and the fortress become the same object."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both groups had the fortress story; the rise from about 30% to about 75% with a hint shows that noticing the analogous relationship, the crucial and most difficult step, often fails without help."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Gick and Holyoak's research, what happened when participants who knew the fortress story were told to think about it while solving the radiation problem?", "options": ["Success rose to about 75%, compared with about 30% without the hint.", "Success fell, because the hint distracted them from the medical problem.", "Success stayed near 10%, the same as for people without the story.", "Every single participant solved the problem once given the hint."], "correct": 0, "why": "Being told to think about the story raised success from about 30% to about 75%. The story helped much more once people noticed that it applied."}}, "5009": {"college": {"stem": "Which item reflects surface features rather than the shared structure of the fortress and radiation problems?", "options": ["The fortress and the tumor, as the particular objects that occupy the central target role.", "The need to combine several weak contributions so that they act together at one destination.", "A constraint that makes a single strong approach dangerous along its path.", "The link between separate safe approaches and a strong combined effect at the target."], "correct": 0, "why": "Surface features are the specific elements of a problem, such as a fortress versus a tumor. The other options describe the underlying principle that governs the solution, which is structural."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which of these is a surface feature of the fortress and radiation problems?", "options": ["One story involves a fortress and the other involves a tumor.", "Both stories require several weak forces to meet at one point.", "In both stories, one strong force would cause damage on the way.", "Both stories are solved by splitting a force into smaller parts."], "correct": 0, "why": "Surface features are the specific objects in a problem, like a fortress or a tumor. The other choices describe the shared underlying principle, which is a structural feature."}}, "5010": {"college": {"stem": "Ruth's sealed lightbulb cannot be opened. A high-intensity laser would fuse the broken filament but shatter the glass, and a low-intensity laser would do neither. Which procedure repairs the filament while keeping the glass intact?", "options": ["Point several low-intensity lasers at the filament from different directions so that they converge at once.", "Keep using one high-intensity laser and simply accept the broken glass as a reasonable side effect of completing the repair.", "Use one low-intensity laser by itself, with no way to raise its effect at the break in the filament.", "Aim each laser at a different, unrelated spot on the bulb so that their effects never combine."], "correct": 0, "why": "Convergence supplies enough combined intensity at the filament while each spot on the glass receives only a weak beam. The goal requires both fusing the filament and keeping the glass intact."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the lightbulb problem, how can Ruth fuse the filament without breaking the glass?", "options": ["Aim several weak lasers so they all meet at the filament.", "Use one strong laser aimed straight at the filament.", "Open the glass bulb and fix the filament by hand.", "Use one weak laser aimed at the glass instead of the filament."], "correct": 0, "why": "Weak beams do not break the glass, but where they meet their combined effect fuses the filament. One strong beam would break the glass, and the bulb is sealed."}}, "5011": {"college": {"stem": "In the lightbulb study, 81% of participants who already knew Duncker's radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with 10% of controls. What makes this pairing of problems useful to compare with transfer from the fortress story to the radiation problem?", "options": ["The radiation and lightbulb problems share beam-like surface elements as well as the same convergence principle.", "The lightbulb problem has no surrounding glass that needs protecting, unlike the radiation problem.", "Knowing the radiation solution physically repairs the bulb before the participant even starts.", "The two problems share no structural relationship at all, so only the length of each written story could affect solving."], "correct": 0, "why": "Rays and lasers aimed at an internal target are similar surface elements, unlike a fortress and a tumor. The comparison asks whether greater surface similarity helps people notice a source that also shares the useful structure."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the lightbulb study, how did knowing Duncker's radiation problem affect solving the lightbulb problem?", "options": ["About 81% of those who knew it solved the lightbulb problem, compared with about 10% of controls.", "About 10% of those who knew it solved the lightbulb problem, which was exactly the same rate as controls.", "Knowing it made people worse, because the two stories seemed too different.", "Everyone solved the lightbulb problem whether or not they knew it."], "correct": 0, "why": "Knowing the radiation problem raised success on the lightbulb problem from about 10% to about 81%. The two problems have similar surface features, which made the link easier to notice."}}, "5012": {"college": {"stem": "Two lightbulb stories involve the same bulb, filament and lasers but differ in why a single beam cannot do the job. How should their structural and surface features be distinguished?", "options": ["Why one beam fails is a structural feature, while the bulb, filament and lasers are surface features.", "Any number mentioned in a story is structural, whereas every relationship between elements is surface detail.", "A named laser is structural, while protecting the surrounding glass is only a difference in wording.", "Structural differences require different objects, so two lightbulb stories cannot differ in structure."], "correct": 0, "why": "Structural features are the underlying principles that govern the solution, such as the reason convergence is needed. The same objects can appear in stories with different structures, as in fragile glass versus too-weak lasers."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a structural feature of a problem?", "options": ["It is the underlying principle that governs how the problem is solved.", "It is a specific object or character that appears in the problem.", "It is the setting where the story of the problem takes place.", "It is the number of words used to state the problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Structural features are the underlying principles that govern the solution. Specific objects, characters and settings are surface features."}}, "5013": {"college": {"stem": "In the fragile-glass version, Ruth's converging low-intensity lasers fuse the filament without harming the glass. Which element of this story corresponds to the healthy-tissue constraint in the radiation problem?", "options": ["Each beam must stay weak enough along its path to avoid damage before the beams combine at the target.", "The filament and the tumor must both stay undamaged, because each of them serves as a protective surrounding for the target.", "A single strong beam can pass safely through the surroundings in both of the problems.", "The glass must be opened first, just as surgery is needed before the tumor can be treated."], "correct": 0, "why": "Fragile glass and healthy tissue both limit how intense any one approach can be. Converging weak beams protects those surroundings while supplying a strong effect at the target."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the fragile-glass version of the lightbulb problem, what was the danger of using one strong laser?", "options": ["The strong laser would break the fragile glass around the filament.", "The strong laser would be too weak to fuse the filament.", "The strong laser would miss the tiny filament inside the sealed bulb entirely.", "The strong laser would damage the other lasers around the bulb."], "correct": 0, "why": "In this version a strong beam could fuse the filament but would also break the glass, just as a strong ray would destroy healthy tissue in the radiation problem."}}, "5014": {"college": {"stem": "Ruth uses converging lasers in both the insufficient-intensity and the fragile-glass versions. Why do the two versions differ structurally?", "options": ["In one, convergence is needed because the laser is too weak, not because a strong beam would damage the glass.", "In one, all of the objects change from bulbs and lasers into armies, roads and fortresses.", "In one, the beams must avoid meeting, so the two solutions have opposite spatial layouts.", "In one, there is no target that needs a stronger combined effect of any kind."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both versions use the same objects and the same converging procedure. Only the fragile-glass version shares the radiation problem's key constraint that a strong approach damages the surroundings."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the insufficient-intensity version of the lightbulb problem, why did Ruth need several lasers?", "options": ["Her laser made only weak beams, too weak to fuse the filament alone.", "A strong beam would have shattered the fragile glass of the bulb.", "She needed to fix several broken filaments at the very same moment.", "The bulb was too large for a single laser beam to reach inside."], "correct": 0, "why": "In this version the problem is that the laser is too weak, so beams are combined to reach the needed intensity. Breaking the glass is the problem in the other, fragile-glass version."}}, "5015": {"college": {"stem": "Which activity best fits the definition of analogical encoding?", "options": ["Comparing two problems to determine which similarities they share in their underlying relationships.", "Memorizing the specific nouns in one story without looking at any second problem.", "Counting how many objects two different stories name while ignoring how those objects relate to one another in each story.", "Applying the first familiar procedure to a new problem without considering any other problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Analogical encoding is the process of comparing two problems and determining their similarities. Its value is that it draws attention to structural features that support problem solving."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is analogical encoding?", "options": ["Comparing two problems to find out how they are similar.", "Memorizing one story word for word before solving a problem.", "Solving a problem without thinking about any other problem.", "Turning a story problem into numbers so it can be calculated."], "correct": 0, "why": "Analogical encoding means comparing two problems and determining what they have in common, which helps people focus on structural features."}}, "5016": {"college": {"stem": "Which statement captures the analogical paradox?", "options": ["People often struggle to use analogies in laboratory tasks, yet they routinely use analogies in real-world settings.", "People solve every laboratory analogy easily but almost never draw on their prior experience when working on everyday problems.", "A source problem must always contain the same objects as the target for any analogy to work.", "Scientists cannot use analogies, because their lab meetings take place outside formal experiments."], "correct": 0, "why": "The paradox is the contrast between difficult analogy use in the laboratory and frequent analogy use in the real world, where scientists use about 3 to 15 analogies in a 1-hour lab meeting."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the analogical paradox?", "options": ["People find analogies hard to use in the lab but use them often in real life.", "People use analogies easily in the lab but almost never use them in real life.", "Analogies only work when two problems share exactly the same objects.", "Experts use fewer analogies than beginners do in their own field."], "correct": 0, "why": "The paradox is that analogies are hard to apply in laboratory tasks even though people, including scientists in lab meetings, use them routinely in the real world."}}, "5017": {"college": {"stem": "An experienced chess player solves chess problems quickly. Which claim is consistent with how expertise is defined?", "options": ["Extensive learning, practice and application of knowledge can produce recognized skill within that particular field.", "Being recognized as a chess expert implies equal expertise in every unrelated area of knowledge.", "A large number of practice hours, by itself and regardless of field, guarantees flexible success on every new type of problem.", "An expert must start every problem faster than a beginner, because analysis is no longer needed."], "correct": 0, "why": "An expert has devoted a large amount of time to learning, practicing and applying knowledge in a field and is acknowledged as highly skilled in that field. Expertise does not imply skill everywhere or a faster start."}, "simple": {"stem": "About how much time has a chess expert typically spent studying and playing chess?", "options": ["Somewhere around 10,000 to 20,000 hours of study and play.", "Fewer than 100 hours spent learning the rules of chess.", "Roughly 500 hours spread across a single year of play.", "No set amount, since chess skill is mostly inborn talent."], "correct": 0, "why": "A chess expert is someone who has spent 10,000 to 20,000 hours studying and playing chess. Fewer than 100 hours describes the beginner in the chess memory study."}}, "5018": {"college": {"stem": "Chase and Simon (1973) had a chess master and a beginner reproduce board arrangements viewed for 5 seconds, using either actual game positions or random placements. Why is the random condition important for interpreting the master's advantage?", "options": ["It removes familiar game patterns, testing whether stored, meaningful chunks support the master's better recall.", "It lets the master study the board for longer while the beginner gets less viewing time.", "It measures only how quickly pieces are moved, so memory plays no part in the task.", "It changes the number of squares on the board so that an entirely different game must be learned."], "correct": 0, "why": "The master did much better only with actual game positions, which were stored in long-term memory and accessed in chunks. Those chunks were not available for random arrangements, so the advantage reflects chess knowledge rather than a better general memory."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chase and Simon's (1973) study, when did the chess master remember piece positions much better than the beginner?", "options": ["When the pieces were arranged in positions from actual games.", "When the pieces were placed randomly on the chessboard.", "In both conditions, by about the same large margin.", "Only when the master could study the board for several minutes."], "correct": 0, "why": "The master did much better with real game positions, which could be recalled as chunks from long-term memory. Random arrangements offered no familiar chunks."}}, "5019": {"college": {"stem": "Chi and colleagues (1982) asked physics faculty members and students with one semester of physics to sort 24 physics problems into groups by similarity. Which pattern did they report?", "options": ["Novices grouped problems by their surface features, while experts grouped them by underlying physics principles.", "Experts grouped problems by how their physical objects looked, while novices grouped them by the underlying physics principles.", "Both groups were told to use the same named principle, so their groupings did not differ.", "The task actually compared how well each group memorized chessboard positions instead of physics problems."], "correct": 0, "why": "The sorting task shows how knowledge is organized. Experts grouped by structural features (underlying principles), while novices grouped by surface features (what the objects looked like)."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) study, how did physics experts sort physics problems?", "options": ["By the underlying physics principles needed to solve them.", "By what the objects in the problems looked like.", "By how long each problem took them to solve.", "By the order in which the problems were handed out to them."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts sorted by structural features, the underlying physics principles. Novices sorted by surface features, such as the objects shown."}}, "5020": {"college": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) sorting task, a novice puts problems 23 and 24 together because both show inclined planes, while an expert puts problems 21 and 24 together even though problem 21 involves a spring. What explains the expert's grouping?", "options": ["Problems 21 and 24 both call for conservation of energy as the principle used to solve them.", "The expert ignores how problems relate and groups them only by the identical look of their diagrams.", "The novice's grouping shows that an inclined plane and a spring are physically the same object.", "All three problem numbers refer to the same physical task with no difference in setup."], "correct": 0, "why": "The expert grouped by a shared physics principle across different-looking objects, while the novice grouped by the shared inclined plane. Problem 24 fits either grouping depending on which similarity is used."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) sorting task, why did the expert group problem 21 (a spring) with problem 24 (an inclined plane)?", "options": ["Both problems involve the same principle, conservation of energy.", "Both problems show objects that look almost exactly alike.", "Both problems were printed right next to each other.", "Both problems contain the same numbers in their diagrams."], "correct": 0, "why": "The expert grouped the problems by a shared physics principle, conservation of energy, even though their objects look different."}}, "5021": {"college": {"stem": "An expert spends longer than a novice analyzing a problem before starting, yet reaches a correct solution more efficiently overall. Which interpretation fits how experts solve problems?", "options": ["A slower start spent on analysis can reveal relevant structure, which usually pays off over the whole task.", "A slower start disproves the person's expertise, regardless of how good, accurate or quick the final solution turns out to be.", "Experts never analyze problems, so the observed analysis must reflect an error in measurement.", "The initial analysis guarantees that the same advantage will appear in every unrelated field."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts spend more time analyzing a problem, giving a slower start, but this strategy usually pays off. It does not extend their advantage to problems outside their field."}, "simple": {"stem": "How do experts usually begin working on a problem compared with novices?", "options": ["They spend more time analyzing it first, which usually pays off.", "They start right away and skip any analysis of the problem.", "They copy the approach that a novice would usually use on the same problem.", "They give up sooner whenever the problem seems difficult."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts spend more time analyzing the problem at the start. The slower start usually pays off."}}, "5022": {"college": {"stem": "A design team has brainstormed many possible approaches but has not yet evaluated them, planned a response or taken action. Which stages of creative problem solving remain unfinished?", "options": ["Problem solving, which includes evaluation and planning, and solution implementation, which involves taking action.", "None, since generating a large number of ideas already counts as the finished product of creative work, with nothing left to do.", "Only problem generation, because taking action is not considered part of creative problem solving.", "Only idea finding, because producing more ideas automatically evaluates the ones already proposed."], "correct": 0, "why": "Generating ideas is only one part of the creative process. Evaluation and planning (problem solving) and taking action (solution implementation) are separate later stages."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which stage of creative problem solving involves taking action?", "options": ["Solution implementation, the final stage of the process.", "Problem generation, which includes problem finding and fact finding.", "Problem formulation, which includes defining the problem and idea finding.", "Problem solving, which includes evaluation and planning."], "correct": 0, "why": "The four stages are problem generation, problem formulation, problem solving and solution implementation. Taking action belongs to solution implementation."}}, "5023": {"college": {"stem": "A highly knowledgeable solver keeps applying a standard procedure even though the task calls for a less familiar approach. How can this knowledge hinder creative problem solving?", "options": ["The familiar procedure can narrow the options considered and make it harder to adopt a better-fitting alternative.", "Any amount of knowledge makes it impossible to form a new connection between existing ideas.", "The standard procedure becomes correct simply because an experienced person prefers to use it.", "Flexible thinking requires throwing out all relevant knowledge entirely, rather than rethinking how that knowledge is being applied."], "correct": 0, "why": "Too much knowledge can sometimes hinder creative problem solving when a problem requires flexible thinking and rejecting accepted procedures. The issue is rigid application, not knowledge as such."}, "simple": {"stem": "How can too much knowledge sometimes hurt creative problem solving?", "options": ["It can make a person stick to usual methods when a new approach is needed.", "It always makes a person forget the basic facts and rules of their own field.", "It makes a person produce far too many ideas to choose from.", "It has no effect, since knowledge only ever helps creativity."], "correct": 0, "why": "Problems often require flexible thinking and rejecting accepted procedures. A lot of knowledge can make it harder to let go of the usual way of doing things."}}, "5024": {"college": {"stem": "When the marriage-problem solution is transferred to the mutilated checkerboard, which mapping keeps the critical pairing relationship?", "options": ["The men and women correspond to the two square colors, and each marriage corresponds to one domino.", "Each individual person corresponds to a whole domino, so each marriage corresponds to four squares.", "The two bachelors who died correspond to one removed square of each color.", "The matchmaker corresponds to one square color, and the two groups of people correspond to the board's total area."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both problems require pairs containing one member from each of two categories. Two bachelors (one category) dying corresponds to removing two squares of the same color, which creates the same imbalance."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the analogy between the marriage and checkerboard problems, what does one marriage correspond to?", "options": ["One domino, which always pairs one square of each color.", "One removed corner square of the whole checkerboard.", "The entire checkerboard, since all the villagers live together.", "A single color of square, since every marriage looks alike."], "correct": 0, "why": "A marriage pairs one man with one woman, just as a domino pairs one square of each color. The removed squares correspond to the two bachelors who died."}}, "5025": {"college": {"stem": "A proposed answer to Duncker's radiation problem uses several low-intensity rays, but they are aimed at different parts of the body and never meet at the tumor. Which requirement is still unmet?", "options": ["A strong enough combined intensity has to reach the tumor itself, not just weak rays at scattered spots.", "Every individual path has to carry a high-intensity ray that destroys the tissue around it.", "The tumor must first be reached through an operation on the patient before any ray, weak or strong, can have an effect on it.", "The procedure must keep all radiation away from the tumor and treat only the healthy tissue."], "correct": 0, "why": "Dividing the radiation into weak rays protects healthy tissue, but the rays must also converge so that their combined intensity destroys the tumor."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why would several weak rays that never meet at the tumor fail to solve Duncker's radiation problem?", "options": ["Their combined strength never builds up at the tumor itself.", "Each weak ray would destroy the healthy tissue it passes through.", "Weak rays cannot pass through the body under any conditions.", "The patient would need an operation before any rays were used."], "correct": 0, "why": "A single weak ray is harmless to tissue but also does not affect the tumor. Only when the weak rays converge does their combined intensity destroy it."}}, "5026": {"college": {"stem": "A participant maps the tumor onto the fortress but cannot say what corresponds to the roads or to the small groups of soldiers. Which step of analogical problem solving is incomplete?", "options": ["Mapping the full set of relevant correspondences between the fortress story and the radiation problem.", "Constructing an additional medical target that the radiation problem never specified.", "Noticing the analogy, even though the participant has already linked the tumor to the fortress.", "Applying divergent thinking, since mapping plays no part in using an analogy."], "correct": 0, "why": "Mapping requires matching elements of the fortress story to elements of the radiation problem. Linking one object is not enough if the roads and soldiers, which carry the solution, are left unmatched; noticing has clearly already occurred."}, "simple": {"stem": "In analogical problem solving, what is mapping?", "options": ["Matching the parts of one problem to the corresponding parts of another.", "Drawing a picture of the problem before trying to solve it.", "Noticing that a story might be relevant to a new problem.", "Writing down every possible solution without judging or comparing any of them."], "correct": 0, "why": "Mapping means matching elements of one problem to elements of the other. Noticing that a story is relevant is a separate, earlier step."}}, "5027": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to separate failing to notice a relevant story from never having encountered it. Which design follows the logic of Gick and Holyoak's hint manipulation?", "options": ["Give both groups the fortress story, then tell only one group to think about it while solving the target.", "Give the fortress story only to the group that also gets the hint, so exposure and hint always go together.", "Let one group change the target problem's constraints while the other group must keep them.", "Compare a group handed the finished target solution with a group that may not attempt the target."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding exposure to the story constant while varying the hint isolates noticing. If only the hint group had the story, exposure and hint would be confounded."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Gick and Holyoak's research, which step of using an analogy was the most difficult?", "options": ["Noticing that the earlier story was related to the new problem.", "Mapping the soldiers onto the rays after the link was seen.", "Applying the solution once all the matches were worked out.", "Remembering the events of the fortress story at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Noticing the analogous relationship is the crucial and most difficult step. Many people who knew the story did not think to use it until given a hint."}}, "5028": {"college": {"stem": "Gick and Holyoak found that telling participants to think about the fortress story raised their success on the radiation problem. Which interpretation best fits this result?", "options": ["A useful story can be available in memory without being spontaneously recognized as relevant to a new problem.", "A useful story produces transfer as soon as it has been encountered, so the hint played no real role.", "Because its military setting differs from a medical one, the story cannot share any structural relationship with the radiation problem.", "The hint replaced the need to generate any corresponding procedure for the radiation problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants who had the story still often failed to use it; a hint to think about it raised success. Noticing, mapping and applying remain separate steps, and the hint helped with noticing."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Gick and Holyoak's research, about what share of participants failed to see the correspondence between the fortress story and the radiation problem on their own?", "options": ["About 70% of participants failed to see the correspondence.", "About 10% of participants failed to see the correspondence.", "About 30% of participants failed to see the correspondence.", "Almost no participants failed to see the correspondence."], "correct": 0, "why": "About 30% solved the problem after the fortress story, so about 70% did not see the correspondences on their own. A hint raised success to about 75%."}}, "5029": {"college": {"stem": "A learner dismisses the fortress story as irrelevant to the radiation problem because one is military and the other medical. What relationship is the learner overlooking?", "options": ["Both need a strong combined effect at a central target while each separate approach stays safe.", "Both require the same named people and objects for one of them to serve as a valid source.", "Both allow a single unrestricted high-intensity approach through the surrounding area.", "Both are solved simply by noticing that the words fortress and tumor mean the same thing."], "correct": 0, "why": "Military and medical settings are surface features. The shared structure is that safe, separate approaches combine to produce a strong effect at the target."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why do people often fail to notice that the fortress story can help with the radiation problem?", "options": ["They focus on surface features, like a fortress versus a tumor.", "They focus too much on the shared principle of converging forces.", "The two stories have completely different underlying solutions.", "The fortress story is too short for anyone to remember it."], "correct": 0, "why": "Noticing is hard partly because people focus on surface features, such as a fortress versus a tumor, rather than the shared convergence principle."}}, "5030": {"college": {"stem": "In the lightbulb study, 81% of participants who knew Duncker's radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with 10% of a control group. Which conclusion does this support?", "options": ["A relevant earlier problem with similar surface elements can help people generate the converging-beam solution.", "Knowing the radiation problem somehow changes the real glass of the bulb so that it no longer breaks under a single strong beam.", "Surface similarity guarantees perfect transfer for every participant who knows the earlier problem.", "The control group's low success proves that the lightbulb problem has no possible solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "Knowing a problem with similar surface features was linked to much higher success. It did not guarantee success for everyone, and control failures do not show the problem is unsolvable."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did the lightbulb study show about surface similarity?", "options": ["Similar surface features between two problems helped people solve the new one.", "Similar surface features between two problems made people more confused.", "Surface features had no effect at all on solving the lightbulb problem.", "Only structural features, never surface features, can help people transfer a solution."], "correct": 0, "why": "Surface similarity helped: about 81% of people who knew the radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with about 10% of controls."}}, "5031": {"college": {"stem": "An experiment keeps the bulb, filament and laser descriptions alike but changes whether a strong beam would damage the glass or whether the laser is simply too weak. What is being manipulated?", "options": ["The structural features that determine why combining several beams is necessary.", "Only the surface category of the objects, since constraints are not part of a problem's structure.", "The number of target problems, without any change in a relationship relevant to the solution.", "Whether the solution uses a filament at all, since one version swaps the bulb for a fortress."], "correct": 0, "why": "The two versions keep the surface features the same and vary the structural features: the underlying reason the beams must be combined."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the two versions of the lightbulb problem, what stayed the same and what changed?", "options": ["The surface features stayed the same, while the structural features changed.", "The structural features stayed the same, while the surface features changed.", "Both the surface and the structural features changed completely.", "Nothing changed except the name of the research assistant."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both versions used the same bulb, filament and lasers (surface features) but differed in why a single beam would not work (structural features)."}}, "5032": {"college": {"stem": "A learner recalls that Ruth used several lasers but forgets that the glass had to be protected. Which correspondence to the radiation problem has been lost?", "options": ["The fragile glass and the healthy tissue both limit how intense a beam can safely be along any single path.", "The filament and the tumor are identical in every physical and biological respect.", "The surroundings must receive the strongest intensity before any beam reaches the target.", "Both problems allow the surroundings to be opened up first, so that the target can be reached and removed directly by hand."], "correct": 0, "why": "The need to protect fragile surroundings explains why a single strong approach fails in both problems. Remembering only the multiple lasers leaves out this structural relationship."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the fragile-glass version of the lightbulb problem, which part matches the healthy tissue in the radiation problem?", "options": ["The fragile glass that surrounds the filament inside the bulb.", "The broken filament that needs to be fused back together.", "The several lasers placed in a circle around the bulb.", "Ruth, the research assistant who finds the broken bulb."], "correct": 0, "why": "The glass, like healthy tissue, would be damaged by one strong beam and must be protected. The filament matches the tumor, and the lasers match the rays."}}, "5033": {"college": {"stem": "Transfer to the radiation problem was 69% after the fragile-glass solution but 33% after the insufficient-intensity solution, although both stories involve a bulb and converging lasers. Which explanation fits this comparison?", "options": ["Sharing the radiation problem's constraint about damage along the path aids transfer beyond similar objects and beam layout.", "Only the fragile-glass version contains any beam, so the comparison removes all structural information.", "The insufficient-intensity solution uses no convergence and so offers no shared procedure at all.", "Transfer depends only on whether the two problems carry the same title."], "correct": 0, "why": "The two versions keep the surface elements and the converging solution but differ in structure. Only the fragile-glass version shares the radiation problem's need to protect the surroundings, and it produced more transfer."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which version of the lightbulb problem helped more people solve the radiation problem afterward?", "options": ["The fragile-glass version, with about 69% solving it versus about 33%.", "The insufficient-intensity version, with about 69% solving it versus about 33%.", "Both versions helped equally, with about half of people solving it.", "Neither version helped, since only about 10% solved it after either."], "correct": 0, "why": "About 69% solved the radiation problem after the fragile-glass version, which shares its structure, compared with about 33% after the insufficient-intensity version."}}, "5034": {"college": {"stem": "Which study activity most directly promotes analogical encoding instead of memorizing isolated story details?", "options": ["Having learners compare two stories and explain the shared relationships that make both solutions work.", "Having learners list only the characters' names without comparing any of the problems.", "Preventing learners from thinking about more than one story at any point while they study.", "Having learners sort the stories only by how many sentences each one contains."], "correct": 0, "why": "Comparing stories and identifying their similarities is analogical encoding, and it directs attention to structural features. Listing names or counting sentences does not."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why does comparing two stories help people solve analogy problems?", "options": ["It makes them pay attention to the shared structural features.", "It makes them memorize the surface details of each separate story word for word.", "It lets them skip working on the target problem entirely.", "It makes the stories shorter and easier to remember."], "correct": 0, "why": "Comparing stories (analogical encoding) is an effective way to get people to focus on structural features, which aids problem solving."}}, "5035": {"college": {"stem": "A student notices that an army story and a radiation story both involve convergence. What would make this similarity structural rather than superficial?", "options": ["It links several safe, separate approaches to the strong combined effect needed at the destination.", "It rests only on both stories including a person who wants to succeed at something.", "It requires the army and the radiation to look identical on the surface.", "It concerns only whether the two stories were printed on the same page."], "correct": 0, "why": "A structural similarity is an underlying principle that explains how the solution meets the problem's constraints. Generic details or where a story appears do not supply that principle."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which similarity between the fortress and radiation problems is a structural feature?", "options": ["Weak forces from many directions combine to make a strong force at one point.", "Both stories feature a main character who is trying hard to reach an important goal.", "Both stories take place somewhere with roads or paths.", "Both stories were written as short paragraphs of text."], "correct": 0, "why": "The shared principle of combining weak forces at one point governs both solutions, so it is structural. The other choices are incidental details."}}, "5036": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher observes scientists solving problems during their regular lab meetings. Which advantage and limitation describe this in vivo approach?", "options": ["It keeps a naturalistic setting, but it is time-consuming and cannot isolate and control variables.", "It guarantees full experimental control over every contributing factor while removing the ordinary work setting entirely.", "It records only final written answers and cannot capture any reasoning as it happens.", "It removes any need to find out whether people actually use analogies in their work."], "correct": 0, "why": "In vivo research observes how people solve problems in the real world. Its advantage is the naturalistic setting; its disadvantages are that it is time-consuming and cannot isolate and control variables."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is in vivo problem-solving research?", "options": ["Observing people to see how they solve problems in the real world.", "Giving people puzzles in a lab under tightly controlled conditions.", "Asking people to sort problems into groups by similarity.", "Measuring brain activity while people solve math problems."], "correct": 0, "why": "In vivo research observes people solving problems in natural, real-world settings, such as scientists in lab meetings."}}, "5037": {"college": {"stem": "Scientists use analogies repeatedly during ordinary work, yet many participants fail to use an analogy offered in an experiment. Which interpretation accounts for both observations?", "options": ["The conditions that lead people to notice and use analogies can differ between lab tasks and real-world activity.", "Every failed lab transfer shows that analogical reasoning never happens outside the lab.", "Any analogy observed in a meeting proves that every controlled transfer task must be easy.", "The two observations involve unrelated processes, since analogies between a source and a target problem can exist only in the lab."], "correct": 0, "why": "This contrast is the analogical paradox. Neither observation cancels the other; analogy use simply appears easier in real-world settings than in laboratory tasks."}, "simple": {"stem": "About how many analogies do scientists use in a 1-hour lab meeting?", "options": ["Somewhere between about 3 and 15 analogies.", "Fewer than one analogy in a typical meeting.", "Somewhere between about 50 and 100 analogies.", "None, because analogies are used only in experiments."], "correct": 0, "why": "Scientists use about 3 to 15 analogies in a 1-hour lab meeting, which shows how routinely analogies are used in real-world settings."}}, "5038": {"college": {"stem": "A person is highly skilled at chess after years of study but has little experience in a new professional field. What prediction follows from the domain-specific nature of expertise?", "options": ["Organized chess knowledge does not by itself give this person an expert advantage on problems in the new field.", "This person will outperform beginners in the new field by the same large margin that appears when the person plays chess.", "This person's chess expertise will vanish as soon as an unrelated problem appears.", "Since expertise depends only on confidence, knowledge of the new field is irrelevant."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts are no better than novices when given problems outside their field. Chess knowledge does not disappear, but it does not transfer automatically to an unrelated domain."}, "simple": {"stem": "A chess master tries to solve a plumbing problem, a field she knows nothing about. What should be expected?", "options": ["She will likely do no better than a plumbing novice.", "She will easily outperform plumbing novices thanks to chess.", "She will lose her chess skill while working on plumbing.", "She will solve it faster, because experts never need analysis."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts are no better than novices on problems outside their field, so her chess expertise gives no special advantage in plumbing."}}, "5039": {"college": {"stem": "In Chase and Simon's (1973) chess recall study, which manipulation most directly tests whether familiar long-term chunks produce the expert's advantage?", "options": ["Comparing actual game positions with random arrangements of the same pieces after the same 5-second view.", "Letting the expert inspect a meaningful board indefinitely while the novice sees it only briefly.", "Asking the expert to play chess while the novice completes an unrelated task.", "Replacing recall with ratings of how much each participant enjoys chess."], "correct": 0, "why": "Game positions contain familiar patterns that random arrangements lack, while viewing time stays equal. Unequal viewing or different tasks would introduce another explanation for the advantage."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chase and Simon's (1973) study, how long did players look at each board before reproducing it?", "options": ["About 5 seconds before reproducing the positions.", "About 5 minutes before reproducing the positions.", "As long as they wanted before reproducing the positions.", "About half a second before reproducing the positions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Players saw each arrangement for 5 seconds and then tried to reproduce it, with either actual game positions or random placements."}}, "5040": {"college": {"stem": "Why does the Chase and Simon (1973) result not require the expert to remember each piece's location as a separate item?", "options": ["Familiar relationships let several piece positions be retrieved together as a chunk stored in long-term memory.", "Experts can reproduce any random arrangement without having to view the board first.", "Each chess piece leaves a physical mark on the square where it previously stood.", "The recall task explicitly prevents using any knowledge from earlier chess experience."], "correct": 0, "why": "Actual game positions were stored in long-term memory and accessed in chunks, so many pieces could be recalled as one meaningful chunk. Those chunks were not available for random arrangements."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chase and Simon's (1973) study, why did the chess master not do much better than the beginner with random arrangements?", "options": ["The stored chunks from real games did not fit random arrangements.", "The master was given much less time to view each of the random boards.", "Random arrangements used more pieces than real game positions did.", "The beginner had practiced random arrangements beforehand."], "correct": 0, "why": "The master's advantage came from chunks of real game positions stored in long-term memory. Random arrangements do not match those chunks."}}, "5041": {"college": {"stem": "Which procedure matches how Chi and colleagues (1982) studied the organization of physics knowledge?", "options": ["Have physics faculty members and students with one semester of physics sort the same problems by similarity, then compare the groupings.", "Give the faculty members only chess positions and the students only radiation stories, then compare how many answers each group produces.", "Require both groups to copy a ready-made grouping scheme so that no individual sorting decisions remain.", "Measure only total years of schooling without asking either group to organize any problems."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sorting the same 24 problems shows how each group relates problems to one another. The result contrasted grouping by surface features with grouping by underlying principles."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) study, who were the experts and the novices?", "options": ["Physics faculty members and students with one semester of physics.", "Chess masters and players with fewer than 100 hours of chess.", "Medical doctors and first-year students studying medicine.", "Graduate students and high school students taking physics."], "correct": 0, "why": "The experts were physics faculty members and the novices were students who had taken one semester of physics."}}, "5042": {"college": {"stem": "A student groups every problem involving a spring together, while a physics faculty member groups a spring problem with an incline problem under an energy principle. Which contrast is most accurate?", "options": ["The student is relying on surface features, while the faculty member is relying on a structural principle relevant to solving.", "The student must be using deeper structure, since every named object in a physics problem defines its own single physical principle.", "The faculty member's grouping implies that springs and inclined planes are the same physical object.", "Neither person is using knowledge organization, because sorting can reveal only factual recall."], "correct": 0, "why": "Grouping by a shared object reflects surface features; grouping different-looking problems by a shared solving principle reflects structural features, as in the expert-novice sorting pattern."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) study, how did novices sort physics problems?", "options": ["By surface features, such as what the objects looked like.", "By the underlying physics principles needed to solve them.", "By how difficult each of the problems seemed to them.", "By the order in which the problems were handed out."], "correct": 0, "why": "Novices sorted by surface features, such as similar objects. Experts sorted by underlying physics principles."}}, "5043": {"college": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) physics sorting task, problem 24 is paired with problem 23 by the novice and with problem 21 by the expert. What does this difference show?", "options": ["The same problem can be grouped by the look of its objects or by the principle needed to solve it.", "Problem 24 must have had a different physical setup in each of the two groupings.", "Every physics problem has exactly one correct partner, whatever relationship is used.", "Experts sort at random whenever the objects in two problems look different."], "correct": 0, "why": "The novice paired the two inclined-plane problems, while the expert paired the two conservation-of-energy problems. The difference lies in how knowledge is organized, not in problem 24 itself."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Chi and colleagues' (1982) physics sorting task, why did the novice group problem 23 with problem 24?", "options": ["Both problems involve the same kind of object, an inclined plane.", "Both problems rely on the very same principle, conservation of energy.", "Both problems include a spring pushing against a block.", "Both problems had the same final numerical answer."], "correct": 0, "why": "The novice grouped them because both show inclined planes, a surface feature. The expert instead grouped problem 24 with a spring problem by conservation of energy."}}, "5044": {"college": {"stem": "A procedure that works in many expert tasks fails on a new problem that demands flexible thinking. Which response best addresses the obstacle?", "options": ["Rethink how the problem is framed and whether a less frequently used procedure fits its constraints.", "Keep repeating the usual procedure, since expertise makes that procedure valid under every possible constraint the problem could impose.", "Ignore the details of the current problem and rely only on the solver's standing as an expert.", "Assume that a novice must do worse on every task, including tasks outside the expert's field."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts are less likely to be open to new ways of looking at problems, which is a disadvantage when the solution requires rejecting usual procedures in favor of less frequently used ones."}, "simple": {"stem": "When can being an expert be a disadvantage?", "options": ["When a problem requires flexible thinking and rejecting usual procedures.", "When a problem falls squarely within the expert's own field of long practice.", "When a problem can be solved with the usual procedures.", "When an expert has extra time to analyze the problem first."], "correct": 0, "why": "Experts are less open to new ways of looking at problems, which hurts when the solution requires rejecting usual procedures in favor of less common ones."}}, "5045": {"college": {"stem": "To test whether an expert's advantage is specific to a domain, which comparison is most informative?", "options": ["Comparing experts and novices on problems both inside and outside the expert's field.", "Testing only problems the expert has memorized and assuming the same result holds for every field.", "Measuring only whether colleagues recognize the expert, without observing any problem solving.", "Giving novices no opportunity to attempt the problems from outside the expert's field."], "correct": 0, "why": "Testing both domains shows whether the advantage depends on field-specific knowledge; experts are no better than novices outside their field. An inside-field result alone cannot show that."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which finding shows that expertise is specific to a field?", "options": ["Experts do no better than novices on problems outside their field.", "Experts solve problems in their own field faster than novices.", "Experts spend more time analyzing problems before starting.", "Experts sort physics problems by their underlying principles."], "correct": 0, "why": "Only the outside-field finding shows the limit of expertise. The other findings describe how experts perform within their own field."}}, "5046": {"college": {"stem": "Which description best fits divergent thinking?", "options": ["An open-ended process that produces a large number of potential solutions or new connections.", "Committing immediately to one accepted procedure before considering any alternatives.", "Carrying out a fully chosen plan without generating any new possibilities along the way.", "Recalling one familiar label with no chance to produce alternative responses."], "correct": 0, "why": "Divergent thinking is open-ended and yields a large number of potential solutions. It differs from committing to, or carrying out, a single plan."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is divergent thinking?", "options": ["Open-ended thinking that produces many possible solutions.", "Focusing on one single correct answer as quickly as possible.", "Following a fixed set of steps without any changes.", "Remembering the answer to a problem solved before."], "correct": 0, "why": "Divergent thinking is open-ended and generates a large number of potential solutions, which is part of creativity."}}, "5047": {"college": {"stem": "A group has defined a problem and proposed several ideas, then compares those ideas and builds a plan before acting. How do these activities map onto the stages of creative problem solving?", "options": ["Comparing ideas and planning belong to problem solving, while acting belongs to solution implementation.", "Acting belongs to problem generation, while comparing ideas lies entirely outside creative work.", "Every stage ends once the first idea appears, so planning and acting fall outside the process.", "Fact finding is part of solution implementation, while acting means only identifying a new problem to work on next."], "correct": 0, "why": "The stages are problem generation (problem and fact finding), problem formulation (definition and idea finding), problem solving (evaluation and planning) and solution implementation (taking action)."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which stage of creative problem solving includes problem finding and fact finding?", "options": ["Problem generation, the first stage of the process.", "Problem formulation, where the problem gets defined.", "Problem solving, where ideas are evaluated and planned.", "Solution implementation, where action is finally taken."], "correct": 0, "why": "Problem generation is the first stage and includes problem finding and fact finding. Formulation, solving and implementation come after it."}}, "5048": {"college": {"stem": "A team produces many ideas but refuses to revise an accepted procedure that conflicts with the task. Which interpretation fits the nature of creative problem solving?", "options": ["Generating ideas helps, but rigid reliance on accepted procedures can still block a workable creative solution.", "Having many ideas proves that evaluation, planning and implementation have all already been completed successfully by the team.", "Accepted procedures can never hinder a person who has enough experience in the field.", "Creativity requires cutting every connection to existing knowledge and to earlier ideas."], "correct": 0, "why": "Generating ideas is only one part of the creative process; without ideas it stops, but problems often require flexible thinking and rejecting accepted procedures."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why is generating ideas only one part of the creative process?", "options": ["Ideas are needed to keep going, but they must still be evaluated and carried out.", "Ideas are not needed at all if a person knows enough facts.", "Generating ideas automatically finishes every other stage of the creative process at once.", "Ideas matter only after a solution has already been carried out."], "correct": 0, "why": "Without ideas the process stops, but creative problem solving also includes evaluation, planning and implementation, often with a lengthy period of trial and error."}}, "5049": {"college": {"stem": "A learner recognizes that the fortress story is relevant, correctly matches its roads and fortress to parts of the radiation problem, and then aims several weak rays to converge on the tumor. Which steps of analogical problem solving has the learner completed?", "options": ["Noticing the analogy, mapping the correspondences, and applying the mapping to produce a solution.", "Applying a mapping before recognizing any relationship, then memorizing only surface names.", "Noticing alone, because producing a procedure for the target adds no further step.", "Divergent thinking alone, without forming any correspondence between the two problems."], "correct": 0, "why": "Gick and Holyoak's steps are noticing the analogous relationship, mapping correspondences between the two problems, and applying the mapping to generate a parallel solution. The learner did all three."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which three steps of analogical problem solving did Gick and Holyoak identify?", "options": ["Noticing the analogy, mapping the correspondences, and applying the mapping.", "Generating the problem, formulating the problem, and then implementing the solution.", "Hearing the story, memorizing it, and repeating it aloud later.", "Sorting problems, chunking them, and recalling them afterward."], "correct": 0, "why": "The three steps are noticing an analogous relationship, mapping correspondences, and applying the mapping. Generation, formulation and implementation are stages of creative problem solving."}}, "5050": {"college": {"stem": "Someone argues that the two lightbulb versions test only surface similarity, since both mention lasers and a filament. What feature of the manipulation contradicts this claim?", "options": ["The reason a single beam fails differs, so the underlying constraints change while the objects stay alike.", "One version swaps the filament for a chessboard and the lasers for dominoes, changing the whole set of surface objects.", "Neither version includes a solution, so no comparison of transfer can be made at all.", "Only the titles differ, and both stories describe identical constraints in every way."], "correct": 0, "why": "The fragile-glass and insufficient-intensity versions keep the same objects but differ in whether damage to the surroundings rules out a strong beam. That is a structural difference."}, "simple": {"stem": "How does the insufficient-intensity lightbulb version differ from the fragile-glass version?", "options": ["It keeps the lasers and filament but drops the danger of breaking the glass.", "It keeps the danger of breaking the glass but drops the lasers entirely.", "It changes the lasers into soldiers marching along several roads.", "It is identical to the fragile-glass version in every single respect, including the glass."], "correct": 0, "why": "Both versions use the same objects, but in the insufficient-intensity version the laser is simply too weak; there is no risk to the glass, so it lacks the radiation problem's key structural feature."}}, "6001": {"college": {"stem": "A hiker sees dark clouds and a falling barometer, concludes that a storm is probably coming, and then picks the shorter of two trails. How should these two mental steps be labeled?", "options": ["Concluding that a storm is likely is reasoning, and picking a trail is decision making.", "Concluding that a storm is likely is decision making, and picking a trail is reasoning.", "Both steps are reasoning, because any choice between trails is simply another conclusion.", "Neither step involves a judgment, because judgments happen only after an action is complete."], "correct": 0, "why": "Reasoning is drawing conclusions and decision making is choosing between alternatives, so the storm conclusion is reasoning that then guides the trail choice. Swapping or merging the labels ignores that distinction."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the difference between reasoning and decision making?", "options": ["Reasoning draws conclusions, while decision making chooses between alternatives.", "Reasoning chooses between alternatives, while decision making draws conclusions.", "Reasoning relies on memory, while decision making never relies on memory.", "They are two names for exactly the same mental process."], "correct": 0, "why": "Reasoning is the process of drawing conclusions; decision making is the process of choosing between alternatives. The reversed option swaps the two definitions."}}, "6002": {"college": {"stem": "A survey team collects 5,000 opinions, all from residents of one wealthy neighborhood, and claims that the sheer number makes its conclusion about the whole city certain. Which evaluation fits inductive reasoning?", "options": ["The conclusion is still only probable, and its strength depends on representativeness and quality as well as number.", "Five thousand observations are enough to make an inductive conclusion about the entire city definitely and permanently true.", "Representativeness matters only when a conclusion rests on a single observation rather than on many.", "Only the count of observations matters, so their source and their quality can be safely ignored."], "correct": 0, "why": "Inductive conclusions are probably, not definitely, true, and their strength depends on the representativeness, number and quality of the observations. A large but unrepresentative sample does not produce certainty."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which factors determine how strong an inductive argument is?", "options": ["How representative, how numerous, and how high in quality the observations are.", "Only how many observations were collected, regardless of anything else about them.", "Only how confident the person feels about the conclusion that was reached.", "Whether the conclusion follows with complete certainty from its starting premises."], "correct": 0, "why": "The strength of an inductive argument depends on the representativeness, number and quality of observations. Number alone is not enough, and inductive conclusions are never fully certain."}}, "6003": {"college": {"stem": "A traveler has seen hundreds of white swans and concludes that all swans are white. She then clearly observes one black swan. Why can this single observation outweigh the many earlier ones?", "options": ["A clear, high-quality observation directly contradicts the universal claim, however many white swans came before it.", "A single observation always supports a generalization more strongly than any large set of observations does.", "The black swan matters only if black swans outnumber white swans in the group she has observed.", "Inductive reasoning requires setting aside any unusual observation whenever it conflicts with a large body of past experience."], "correct": 0, "why": "The quality of observations, not just their number, affects the strength of an inductive argument, and one compelling observation can carry a lot of weight. A reliable counter-instance directly undercuts the claim that every swan is white."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why can one very convincing observation carry a lot of weight in inductive reasoning?", "options": ["Because the quality of an observation, not just the number, affects argument strength.", "Because inductive conclusions are always based on exactly one observation.", "Because the number of observations never matters in inductive reasoning.", "Because one strong observation makes any inductive conclusion definitely and permanently true."], "correct": 0, "why": "Argument strength depends on quality as well as number, so one compelling, high-quality observation can matter a great deal. It still does not make an inductive conclusion certain."}}, "6004": {"college": {"stem": "Which statement best captures how inductive reasoning operates in everyday life?", "options": ["It uses what happened in the past to predict what will happen now, sometimes automatically in familiar situations.", "It counts only when a person deliberately writes out every relevant past observation in a list before making any choice at all.", "It guides behavior only when the current situation has nothing in common with earlier situations.", "It guarantees that an outcome observed in the past will occur again in every future instance."], "correct": 0, "why": "Inductive reasoning lets past experience guide present behavior, especially in familiar situations, and some of it happens automatically. Its predictions are probable, not guaranteed."}, "simple": {"stem": "How do people use inductive reasoning in everyday life?", "options": ["They predict what will happen based on what has happened before.", "They avoid using past experience when they make predictions.", "They reason this way only when solving formal logic problems.", "They reach conclusions that are guaranteed to be true every time."], "correct": 0, "why": "Everyday induction uses past experience to predict what will happen, often in familiar situations. Its conclusions are likely rather than guaranteed."}}, "6005": {"college": {"stem": "A mental shortcut usually gives a fast, correct answer, but occasionally it leads a person astray. Which definition of a heuristic fits both outcomes?", "options": ["A rule of thumb that is likely to give the correct answer quickly but is not foolproof.", "An exhaustive procedure that guarantees a correct answer whenever past experience is used.", "A random guess that does not draw on any information stored in memory at all.", "A reasoning error by definition, so a correct answer could never have come from one."], "correct": 0, "why": "Heuristics are rules of thumb that help people reach conclusions rapidly and are likely, but not certain, to be correct. That is why the same shortcut can succeed in one case and fail in another."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a heuristic?", "options": ["A rule of thumb that is often right but not foolproof.", "A step-by-step method that always gives the correct answer.", "A random guess made without using any past experience.", "A type of reasoning error that is always wrong."], "correct": 0, "why": "A heuristic is a shortcut that is likely to give the right answer but can fail. It is neither guaranteed correct nor always wrong."}}, "6006": {"college": {"stem": "Two kinds of events happen equally often, but one is emotionally vivid and the person has recalled it many times. The person judges the vivid event more probable because instances come to mind easily. What explains this judgment?", "options": ["The availability heuristic, since emotion and repeated retrieval make instances easier to recall without making them more frequent.", "The representativeness heuristic, since the person judges the event by comparing it with the most typical member of a familiar occupational category.", "The law of large numbers, since the most emotional event must also be the most frequent one.", "The conjunction rule, since an event recalled repeatedly becomes more probable than its original occurrence."], "correct": 0, "why": "Under the availability heuristic, events that are easier to remember are judged more probable, and emotional salience and repeated retrieval make events stand out in memory. Representativeness concerns resemblance to a category, not ease of recall."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the availability heuristic?", "options": ["Judging events that are easier to remember as more probable.", "Judging how likely something is by how well it matches a category.", "Judging a combination of two events as more likely than either alone.", "Judging probability by carefully counting how often each event occurs."], "correct": 0, "why": "The availability heuristic treats ease of recall as a sign of probability. Matching a category describes representativeness instead."}}, "6007": {"college": {"stem": "When asked whether more English words begin with r or have r as the third letter, about 70% say words beginning with r, although words with r third are about three times more common. What best explains the error?", "options": ["Words are easier to retrieve by their first letter, and that ease of recall is mistaken for higher frequency.", "People know the true frequency ratio but reverse it when asked to give a category label instead.", "People wrongly believe that every English word contains the letter r in both of those positions.", "An easier retrieval cue actually changes how many words that begin with the letter r exist in the English language."], "correct": 0, "why": "It is easier to call to mind words by their first letter, so r-first words are more available and are judged more common. The actual frequencies favor r in the third position."}, "simple": {"stem": "Most people say more English words start with r than have r in third place. Why is this judgment wrong?", "options": ["Words with r third are more common, but words starting with r are easier to recall.", "Words starting with r are rarer, and people confuse r with similar-looking letters.", "Both kinds of words are equally common, so neither answer is better than the other.", "People judge correctly but misunderstand which letter position they were asked about."], "correct": 0, "why": "About three times more words have r in the third position, but words starting with r come to mind more easily. That ease of recall is the availability heuristic at work."}}, "6008": {"college": {"stem": "Asked whether a randomly chosen person in the United States is more likely to die next year of asthma or of botulism, many people pick botulism, although asthma deaths are far more common. Why?", "options": ["Botulism deaths make front-page news while asthma deaths go unnoticed, so botulism is more available.", "Botulism resembles a typical cause of death more closely, so representativeness overrides the asthma base rate.", "People correctly judge botulism as more frequent, because news coverage mirrors how often each cause occurs.", "People apply the conjunction rule, which makes a rare single cause seem more probable than a common one."], "correct": 0, "why": "The cause-of-death errors reflect availability: dramatic botulism deaths are front-page news, whereas asthma deaths go virtually unnoticed. News coverage reflects salience, not actual frequency."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why do people often judge death from botulism as more likely than death from asthma?", "options": ["Botulism deaths get big news coverage, so they come to mind more easily.", "Botulism really does kill more people each year than asthma does.", "Botulism better matches people's idea of a typical deadly illness.", "People are given the true death rates and simply misremember them."], "correct": 0, "why": "Botulism deaths are front-page news while asthma deaths go virtually unnoticed, so botulism is more available in memory. In fact asthma causes far more deaths."}}, "6009": {"college": {"stem": "The United States has about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries. Robert, a randomly chosen man, wears glasses, speaks quietly, and reads a lot. Most people say he is more likely a librarian. What makes this a representativeness error?", "options": ["His description matches the typical librarian, and that similarity leads people to ignore how many more farmers there are.", "People recall every librarian they have personally met and count those remembered instances one by one.", "The judgment depends on a conjunction of two nested categories, like the feminist bank tellers.", "The description changes the actual numbers of farms and libraries, so that librarians now outnumber farmers in the population."], "correct": 0, "why": "Representativeness judges membership by resemblance to a category, and here the resemblance leads people to ignore the base rate of farmers versus librarians. Counting recalled instances would be availability, and the base rates themselves do not change."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the representativeness heuristic?", "options": ["Judging whether something belongs to a group by how much it resembles that group.", "Judging how likely an event is by how easily instances of that event come to mind.", "Judging that larger samples are more representative than smaller samples.", "Judging a claim true only after collecting many high-quality observations."], "correct": 0, "why": "The representativeness heuristic bases judgments on how closely one thing resembles a category. Ease of recall describes the availability heuristic."}}, "6010": {"college": {"stem": "In a group of 100 people with 70 lawyers and 30 engineers, participants correctly estimate the chance of drawing an engineer. After learning that Jack enjoys carpentry, sailing, and mathematical puzzles, they say Jack is an engineer. What does the change in responses show?", "options": ["People use base rates when that is all they have but disregard them once descriptive information is available.", "People cannot understand numerical probability at all, which is also why they fail the bare question about lawyers and engineers.", "Adding the description actually changes the group into 70 engineers and 30 lawyers.", "The description has no effect, because both questions use exactly the same occupation labels."], "correct": 0, "why": "People answer the base-rate-only question correctly, but once a fitting description appears they rely on it and disregard the base rate. The group composition never changes."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the lawyer-engineer problem, how do people's answers change once a description of Jack is added?", "options": ["They stop using the 70-to-30 base rate and go by the description.", "They start using the base rate, which they had ignored before.", "Their answers stay the same, because the base rate has not changed.", "They refuse to answer, because the description seems irrelevant to them."], "correct": 0, "why": "With only the base rate, people answer correctly; with a description that fits an engineer, they disregard the base rate and say Jack is an engineer."}}, "6011": {"college": {"stem": "A respondent rates “Linda is a bank teller and is active in the feminist movement” as more probable than “Linda is a bank teller.” Why does this ranking violate the conjunction rule?", "options": ["Every feminist bank teller is also a bank teller, so adding a condition cannot raise the probability.", "Every bank teller is necessarily a feminist, so the two statements carry identical information.", "A statement with more matching personality details is always more probable than a shorter one.", "The conjunction rule applies only when the two categories share no members at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "The probability of two events together cannot exceed the probability of either one alone, and feminist bank tellers are a subset of bank tellers. A good descriptive fit does not change that."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the conjunction rule state?", "options": ["Two events together cannot be more probable than either one alone.", "Two events together are always more probable than either one alone.", "Two events together are exactly as probable as each one alone.", "Detailed descriptions are more probable than short, general descriptions."], "correct": 0, "why": "The conjunction rule says the probability of two events together cannot be higher than the probability of each single event."}}, "6012": {"college": {"stem": "A town's larger hospital has about 45 births a day and its smaller hospital about 15. For a year, each records the days on which more than 60% of the babies born were boys. What must be compared to decide which hospital records more such days?", "options": ["How often each hospital's daily sample yields an extreme percentage, given its daily sample size.", "The total number of boys each hospital delivers across the full year of records.", "Nothing at all, since both daily samples must contain exactly 50% boys like the population.", "Only the number of births, because a bigger hospital produces more of every kind of day."], "correct": 0, "why": "The question concerns how often a daily proportion crosses a 60% threshold, which depends on daily sample size. Annual totals of boys do not answer it."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the hospital problem (15 versus 45 births a day), how did most people answer?", "options": ["Most said both hospitals would record about the same number of days.", "Most said the larger hospital would record more such days.", "Most said the smaller hospital would record more such days.", "Most said neither hospital would ever record such a day."], "correct": 0, "why": "56% answered “about the same,” with 22% each choosing the larger or the smaller hospital. The correct answer, the smaller hospital, was picked by only a minority."}}, "6013": {"college": {"stem": "In the hospital problem, both hospitals draw from a population that is about half boys. Why should the hospital with 15 daily births record more days above 60% boys than the one with 45?", "options": ["Smaller daily samples vary more, so their proportions stray from the population value more often.", "Fewer births a day means the underlying population of babies must contain more boys.", "The smaller hospital has more total births, so each of its daily percentages is higher.", "The larger hospital can never exceed 60% boys, because large samples remove all random day-to-day variation."], "correct": 0, "why": "Small samples fluctuate more, so the smaller hospital more often departs from 50% boys. The population proportion is the same for both, and large samples still vary somewhat."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which hospital records more days with over 60% boys, and why?", "options": ["The smaller hospital, because small samples swing away from 50% more often.", "The larger hospital, because it has more babies born each day.", "Both equally, because each hospital's population of newborns is about half boys.", "Neither, because over 60% boys can never happen on any day."], "correct": 0, "why": "The correct answer is the smaller hospital: small samples are less representative of the population, so extreme days are more common there."}}, "6014": {"college": {"stem": "Which statement expresses the law of large numbers?", "options": ["The more individuals randomly drawn from a population, the more representative the group tends to be.", "Every random sample exactly reproduces its population as soon as the first individual is selected.", "Increasing the size of a sample guarantees that every individual observation has an identical value.", "Random selection makes sample size irrelevant to how closely a group resembles its population."], "correct": 0, "why": "The law of large numbers says larger random samples are more representative of the population. Small random samples can still depart substantially from it."}, "simple": {"stem": "What error do people make about small samples?", "options": ["They assume small samples represent the population as well as large ones.", "They assume small samples are less accurate than any single observation.", "They assume small samples must contain only extreme or unusual cases.", "They assume small samples can never be drawn at random from any population."], "correct": 0, "why": "People often assume representativeness holds for small samples, which leads to reasoning errors. By the law of large numbers, larger samples are the more representative ones."}}, "6015": {"college": {"stem": "Someone holding a stereotype notices and remembers behaviors that fit it, overlooks behaviors that do not, and concludes that the group and the behavior are strongly linked. What process is at work?", "options": ["Selective attention makes fitting behaviors more available, creating an illusory correlation that reinforces the stereotype.", "An accurate correlation has been measured from a representative and unbiased sample of the group's full range of everyday behavior.", "The stereotype keeps fitting behaviors out of memory entirely, so availability plays no role at all.", "The conjunction rule shows that a group must display every behavior that is attributed to it."], "correct": 0, "why": "Attention to stereotype-consistent behaviors makes them more available, producing an illusory correlation that reinforces the stereotype. The perceived link is absent or weaker than assumed, not accurately measured."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is an illusory correlation?", "options": ["A relationship that seems to exist but is absent or much weaker than assumed.", "A real relationship that has been measured accurately in a large sample.", "A relationship that people notice only after it has been proven statistically.", "A relationship between two events that always happen at exactly the same time."], "correct": 0, "why": "An illusory correlation is a perceived relationship that does not exist or is much weaker than people assume. It often arises when people expect two things to be related."}}, "6016": {"college": {"stem": "Lord and colleagues (1979) had people who favored and people who opposed capital punishment read the same article. Supporters judged the article as favoring their view, and opponents judged it as opposing theirs. What does giving both groups the same article establish?", "options": ["Prior attitudes can steer how identical information is judged, with each side focusing on what agreed with it.", "The two groups must have received different articles, since a single article cannot be interpreted in two opposite ways.", "Exposure to identical evidence removes any influence of prior beliefs on how that evidence is judged.", "Myside bias appears only when participants are prevented from seeing any information about the issue."], "correct": 0, "why": "Because the evidence was identical, the opposite evaluations must come from the readers' attitudes: each side focused on agreeing information and disregarded the rest. This is the myside bias."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the study by Lord and colleagues (1979), what happened when supporters and opponents of capital punishment read the same article?", "options": ["Each side judged the article as supporting its own view.", "Both sides judged the article as supporting capital punishment.", "Both sides changed their minds after seeing the evidence.", "Neither side found the article related to the issue at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Those in favor found the article in favor and those against found it against, showing the myside bias. Neither side changed its mind."}}, "6017": {"college": {"stem": "In the 2, 4, 6 number-rule task, a participant keeps testing triples that increase by two, such as 8, 10, 12, and is told each one fits the rule. Why does this feedback fail to confirm the participant's hypothesis?", "options": ["Those triples also fit the broader actual rule of any three increasing numbers, so they cannot tell the rules apart.", "Positive feedback means the experimenter has confirmed the participant's stated rule in every detail.", "The actual rule requires decreasing numbers, so every increasing triple should have been rejected.", "The feedback reports whether the participant has found a new mathematical law, rather than whether a particular triple fits the rule."], "correct": 0, "why": "Feedback only says whether a triple fits the hidden rule, which was any three numbers in increasing order. Triples increasing by two fit both that rule and the narrower hypothesis, so they cannot distinguish them."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the 2, 4, 6 number-rule task, what was the actual rule?", "options": ["Any three numbers in increasing order of magnitude.", "Three even numbers that increase by two each time.", "Any three numbers that add up to an even number.", "Three numbers in which the last equals the sum of the others."], "correct": 0, "why": "The actual rule was simply three numbers in increasing order. The most common hypothesis, increasing intervals of two, was too narrow."}}, "6018": {"college": {"stem": "Under expected utility theory, what will a rational person who has all the relevant information choose?", "options": ["The option that yields the maximum expected utility, such as the greatest expected monetary payoff.", "The option with the single largest possible prize, whatever its chance of actually occurring.", "The option containing the most favorable items, regardless of how many items there are in total.", "The option most recently retrieved from memory, even if its expected benefit is lower."], "correct": 0, "why": "Expected utility theory assumes people are rational and, with all relevant information, choose the option with maximum expected utility, such as maximum monetary payoff. The biggest possible prize ignores its probability."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does expected utility theory assume about people?", "options": ["They are rational and choose whatever gives the maximum expected utility.", "They are emotional and choose whatever option feels safest to them at the moment.", "They rely on heuristics and choose whatever comes to mind first.", "They ignore payoffs and choose whatever other people are choosing."], "correct": 0, "why": "The theory assumes rational decision makers who, given all relevant information, maximize expected utility. Relying on heuristics is what the theory leaves out."}}, "6019": {"college": {"stem": "A person who knows that casino odds are heavily against them still decides to gamble. What problem does this pose for the utility approach?", "options": ["Many choices do not maximize the chance of the best outcome, and people value more than money.", "It shows that anyone who chooses to gamble at a casino must be unaware of the odds against winning.", "It shows that the expected monetary payoff of casino gambling is actually positive.", "It shows that every outcome people value must be converted into equal sums of money."], "correct": 0, "why": "People gamble even though they know the odds are against them, so they are not utility-maximization machines and may find value in things other than money. Knowing the odds does not stop the choice."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which is a problem for the utility approach to decision making?", "options": ["People often make choices that do not maximize their expected payoff.", "It provides no procedure at all for identifying the best choice.", "People always choose the option with the highest monetary payoff.", "People are perfectly consistent in every decision they make."], "correct": 0, "why": "A key problem is that many decisions do not maximize the best outcome, as when people gamble despite bad odds. Having specific procedures for the best choice is an advantage of the approach, not a problem."}}, "6020": {"college": {"stem": "Participants could earn $1 for each red jellybean drawn and chose between a bowl with 1 red bean in 10 and a bowl with 7 red beans in 100. Most chose the 7-in-100 bowl. What does a correct comparison of the bowls show?", "options": ["Seven red beans is the larger count, but 7 out of 100 is a lower chance than 1 out of 10.", "Seven red beans gives a 70% chance, since each bowl should be treated as holding ten beans.", "The bowls offer equal chances, because each one contains at least one red jellybean.", "The bowl with more beans in total always offers the better chance, whatever their colors."], "correct": 0, "why": "The chances are 10% versus 7%, so the popular bowl is worse; the larger number of red beans draws attention away from the larger total. This is why the choice was influenced by considerations other than probabilities."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the jellybean study, most participants chose the bowl with 7 red beans out of 100 instead of 1 out of 10. What does this show?", "options": ["Their choices were influenced by considerations other than probabilities.", "They correctly picked the bowl with the better chance of winning.", "They could not tell the red jellybeans from the white ones.", "They preferred to earn as little money as they possibly could."], "correct": 0, "why": "The 7-in-100 bowl has worse odds than 1-in-10, so choosing it shows that something other than probability guided the decision."}}, "6021": {"college": {"stem": "After September 11, 2001, air travel decreased and driving increased. Why could this shift in behavior be harmful?", "options": ["Dying in a car accident is far more likely than dying in a flight, so drivers faced greater danger.", "Switching to a different mode of travel removes any possibility of dying on the journey itself.", "A frightening event is, by definition, more probable than a less noticeable kind of event.", "Risk depends only on whether a person has personally witnessed a crash of that particular type."], "correct": 0, "why": "The probability of dying in a car accident is far greater than in flying, so avoiding a salient threat raised exposure to a likelier one. Salience does not make an event more probable."}, "simple": {"stem": "What happened after 9/11, when many Americans drove instead of flying?", "options": ["More people died on the roads than were killed on the hijacked flights.", "Road deaths fell, because people drove more carefully than before.", "Air travel became riskier than driving for the following few years after the attacks.", "The number of people traveling by car did not change at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "The number of Americans who died on the road to avoid flying was higher than the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights."}}, "6022": {"college": {"stem": "In Ritov and Baron's (1999) convoy dilemma, rerouting food trucks to a second camp would save 1,000 people but cause 100 deaths at the first camp. Sixty-three percent refused to reroute. What does this response suggest?", "options": ["A larger net number of lives saved does not by itself decide whether people take an action that harms others.", "Respondents were maximizing the net number of lives saved, since refusing to reroute actually saves more people in total.", "Rerouting would save no one at the second camp, so the two options had identical outcomes.", "Respondents saw the two routes as identical, with no difference in who would receive the food."], "correct": 0, "why": "Rerouting has the better net balance (1,000 saved versus 100 lost), yet 63% refused. Their choice therefore rests on something beyond maximizing the net count of lives."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Ritov and Baron's (1999) convoy dilemma, what did most people decide?", "options": ["They would not send the convoy to the second camp.", "They would send the convoy to the second camp.", "They would split the convoy between the two camps.", "They would send the convoy back without delivering food."], "correct": 0, "why": "63% did not choose to send the convoy to the second camp, even though rerouting would save 1,000 people at a cost of 100."}}, "6023": {"college": {"stem": "In Tetlock and colleagues' (2000) Sacred Value Protection Model, how do taboo and tragic tradeoffs differ?", "options": ["A taboo tradeoff mixes a sacred value with a secular one, while a tragic tradeoff pits two sacred values against each other.", "A taboo tradeoff pits two sacred values against each other, while a tragic tradeoff sets a sacred value against an amount of money.", "Both involve offers that are too small, and they do not differ in the kinds of values being compared.", "Both are defined by a willingness to exchange sacred values for the largest payment that is available."], "correct": 0, "why": "Mixing the sacred and secular domains is taboo and triggers moral outrage; comparing two sacred values is tragic and produces less outrage. The second option reverses the two terms."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Tetlock and colleagues' (2000) model, how do people respond when a sacred value is traded against a secular value such as money?", "options": ["They feel threatened and respond with moral outrage.", "They feel relieved and respond with gratitude toward the decider.", "They feel nothing, because secular values never matter to them.", "They see it as a tragic choice and feel little outrage."], "correct": 0, "why": "When sacred and secular values are mixed, people feel threatened and respond with moral outrage, which makes it a taboo tradeoff. Little outrage goes with tragic tradeoffs between two sacred values."}}, "6024": {"college": {"stem": "In the scenario used by Tetlock and colleagues (2000), Robert can save five-year-old Johnny with a liver transplant or spend its $1,000,000 cost on other hospital needs. Which change would turn this taboo tradeoff into a tragic one?", "options": ["Replacing the hospital spending with a second child who needs the only available liver.", "Raising the amount of money at stake while keeping the choice between money and Johnny's life.", "Keeping both options the same but describing the hospital budget in much greater detail.", "Removing Johnny's need for a transplant, so that no life depends on either of the options."], "correct": 0, "why": "The tragic scenario has two children needing a transplant and only one liver, pitting a sacred value against another sacred value. Changing the amount of money keeps a sacred-versus-secular, taboo tradeoff."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Tetlock and colleagues' (2000) tragic tradeoff scenario, what choice did the decision maker face?", "options": ["Two children needed a liver transplant, but only one liver was available.", "One child's transplant competed with spending money on hospital equipment.", "A hospital had to choose between two expensive pieces of new equipment.", "A doctor had to choose between two higher salaries for new staff."], "correct": 0, "why": "The tragic scenario involves two children who need a liver transplant but only one liver. A child's life versus hospital spending is the taboo scenario."}}, "6025": {"college": {"stem": "Which pattern of moral outrage did Tetlock and colleagues (2000) find for decisions about saving Johnny?", "options": ["Under taboo, not saving Johnny drew far more outrage than saving him; under tragic, both outcomes drew similar outrage.", "Saving Johnny drew more outrage than not saving him, and by an equal margin in both kinds of tradeoff.", "The tragic tradeoff showed the same large difference between saving and not saving him as the taboo one did.", "Outrage depended only on whether Johnny survived and was unaffected by which type of tradeoff was involved."], "correct": 0, "why": "In the taboo condition, outrage was about 5.4 when Johnny was not saved versus about 2.4 when he was saved; in the tragic condition both outcomes were near 3.8. So tradeoff type changed the effect of the outcome."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Tetlock and colleagues' (2000) results, when was moral outrage highest?", "options": ["When Johnny was not saved in the taboo tradeoff.", "When Johnny was saved in the taboo tradeoff.", "When Johnny was not saved in the tragic tradeoff.", "When Johnny was saved in the tragic tradeoff."], "correct": 0, "why": "Outrage was highest (about 5.4 on the scale) when Johnny was not saved in the taboo tradeoff, where money competed with his life. In the tragic tradeoff, the two outcomes drew similar outrage."}}, "6026": {"college": {"stem": "Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007) interviewed Israeli settlers, Palestinian refugees, and Palestinian students affiliated with political groups about possible peace deals. What set this research apart from earlier work?", "options": ["It extended sacred-value findings to a realistic setting, with conflict-involved groups judging real issues.", "It tested randomly chosen laboratory volunteers who had no personal, political, or historical connection to the conflict at all.", "It measured only memory for word lists and never asked about any proposed tradeoff.", "It assumed every participant held identical sacred values because all came from one group."], "correct": 0, "why": "The study extended previous findings to a more realistic setting by asking people involved in the conflict about tradeoff deals. It did not assume all of them held the issues as sacred."}, "simple": {"stem": "Whom did Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007) interview?", "options": ["Israeli settlers, Palestinian refugees, and Palestinian students.", "American college students recruited for a laboratory study.", "Government negotiators from several countries in the region.", "Iranian participants who completed an online survey written in Farsi."], "correct": 0, "why": "The study interviewed Israeli settlers (n = 601), Palestinian refugees (n = 535) and politically affiliated Palestinian students (719). The online Farsi survey belongs to the Iranian nuclear studies."}}, "6027": {"college": {"stem": "In the deals proposed by Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), how does the Symbolic offer differ from the Taboo+ offer?", "options": ["The Symbolic offer adds Israeli recognition of Palestinian rights and an apology, while Taboo+ adds a yearly payment.", "The Symbolic offer adds a larger yearly payment to Palestine, while Taboo+ contains no monetary term of any kind at all.", "Both offers keep identical terms and differ only in the wording used to introduce each of them.", "The Symbolic offer removes all recognition of historical claims from the basic two-state agreement."], "correct": 0, "why": "Taboo+ adds a payment of 1 billion U.S. dollars a year for 10 years, while the Symbolic offer adds Israel's recognition of the Palestinians' right to a state and an apology. One is monetary, the other symbolic."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Taboo+ deal of Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), what was added to the basic deal?", "options": ["Israel would pay Palestine $1 billion a year for 10 years.", "Israel would apologize for all wrongs done to the Palestinians.", "Israel would give up its claim to all of Jerusalem.", "The United Nations would impose sanctions on both sides."], "correct": 0, "why": "Taboo+ added a monetary payment of $1 billion a year for 10 years. The apology belongs to the Symbolic offer."}}, "6028": {"college": {"stem": "Two participants in the study by Ginges and colleagues (2007) strongly oppose giving up land. One says it is never permissible no matter how great the benefit; the other would weigh the benefits. How does the sacred-value measure sort them?", "options": ["Only the first counts as a moral absolutist; strong opposition by itself does not make a value sacred.", "Both count as moral absolutists, because any strong preference is the same thing as a sacred value.", "The second counts as the absolutist, because sacred values are the ones that maximize monetary gain.", "Neither can hold a sacred value, because the measure requires indifference to the disputed issue."], "correct": 0, "why": "Moral absolutists refuse compromise no matter how great the benefit, whereas non-absolutists can strongly oppose compromise without treating the issue as sacred. Strength of preference alone does not decide the category."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the study by Ginges and colleagues (2007), who counted as a moral absolutist?", "options": ["Someone who would never compromise on the issue, no matter the benefit.", "Someone who strongly dislikes compromise but would consider large benefits.", "Someone who has no strong opinion about the disputed issue at all.", "Someone who would accept any deal that offered enough money."], "correct": 0, "why": "Moral absolutists turned the disputed issues into sacred values that could never be given up, however great the benefit. Strong opposition that still weighs benefits marks a non-absolutist."}}, "6029": {"college": {"stem": "In the results of Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), adding money to a taboo deal (Taboo+) increased anger and disgust among moral absolutists but slightly decreased it among non-absolutists. What does this imply?", "options": ["A monetary offer's effect depends on whether the issue is held as sacred; money has no single uniform effect.", "Monetary additions are always welcomed by every group of participants, as long as the amount offered is positive.", "Absolutists and non-absolutists changed in identical ways, so sacred status made no difference.", "The monetary addition turned every participant's preference into a sacred value before the ratings."], "correct": 0, "why": "The same added money moved the two groups in opposite directions, so its effect depends on sacred-value status. For moral absolutists the incentive backfired."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the results of Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), how did moral absolutists react when money was added to a taboo deal?", "options": ["They showed more anger and more support for violence.", "They showed less anger and less support for violence.", "They showed no change in anger or support for violence.", "They accepted the deal once the amount offered was large enough."], "correct": 0, "why": "For moral absolutists, Taboo+ raised anger and disgust and raised support for violence compared with the plain taboo deal. Money backfired for them."}}, "6030": {"college": {"stem": "In the studies of Iranians who treat their nuclear program as a sacred value (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), how do added economic funds and added sanctions differ as manipulations?", "options": ["Funds add a benefit for accepting the deal, while sanctions add a cost for refusing the deal.", "Funds and sanctions both add only symbolic recognition, with no economic consequences attached.", "Sanctions are an extra financial reward that participants' country receives for accepting the deal.", "Funds directly change who holds a sacred value, while sanctions leave the deal itself unchanged."], "correct": 0, "why": "An incentive (economic funds) makes accepting more rewarding, while a disincentive (sanctions) makes refusing more costly. The studies asked how such monetary pressures affect people holding a sacred value."}, "simple": {"stem": "What question did the Iranian nuclear-program studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009) investigate?", "options": ["Whether incentives or sanctions could get Iranians who hold the program sacred to compromise.", "Whether Iranians could accurately recall the details of past nuclear agreements.", "Whether Iranians preferred nuclear energy to nuclear weapons when asked directly.", "Whether Israeli settlers would give up land in exchange for a large yearly cash payment."], "correct": 0, "why": "The studies tested whether Iranians who treat the nuclear program as a sacred value would respond to incentives or disincentives to compromise. The land question belongs to Ginges and colleagues (2007)."}}, "6031": {"college": {"stem": "In the online Farsi study of 1,997 Iranian participants (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), people were asked, “What do you think about Iran giving up its nuclear energy/weapons?” Which answer marked a sacred value?", "options": ["This shouldn't be done no matter how great the consequences.", "This should be done only if the benefits of stopping the program are great enough.", "I do not object to Iran doing this at all.", "I think this definitely needs to happen soon."], "correct": 0, "why": "Only the answer refusing regardless of consequences counted as a sacred value (14% for energy, 13% for weapons). Accepting the deal if the benefits are great enough is a consequence-based position."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Iranian nuclear-program studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), which participants were counted as holding a sacred value?", "options": ["Those who said giving it up shouldn't happen no matter the consequences.", "Those who said giving it up should happen only for great enough benefits.", "Those who said they did not object to Iran giving it up.", "Those who said giving up the program definitely needs to happen."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants who answered that the program should not be given up no matter how great the consequences were treated as holding a sacred value. Wanting enough benefits first is not sacred-value thinking."}}, "6032": {"college": {"stem": "The Iranian scenarios (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009) crossed program type (nuclear energy vs. nuclear weapons) with deal type (Taboo, Taboo+, Taboo−). Which description of the design is accurate?", "options": ["It has six conditions; Taboo+ adds $40 billion in funds and Taboo− adds sanctions if Iran refuses.", "It has three conditions, because program type was a dependent variable rather than a manipulation.", "It has six conditions; Taboo+ adds more sanctions and Taboo− adds $40 billion in funds.", "It has two conditions, because every deal type offered exactly the same set of terms."], "correct": 0, "why": "A 2 × 3 design gives six conditions. Taboo+ adds $40 billion from the United Nations, and Taboo− adds the threat of extra UN sanctions if Iran does not accept."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the basic Taboo deal of the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), what would Israel do in return for Iran giving up its program?", "options": ["Israel would also give up its program and surrender its facilities to the UN.", "Israel would pay Iran $40 billion in economic funds in exchange for the deal.", "Israel would apologize for all the past wrongs done to Iran.", "Israel would lift all of its own sanctions against Iran."], "correct": 0, "why": "In the Taboo deal, Israel would give up its own nuclear energy or weapons program and surrender its facilities to the UN. The $40 billion came from the UN, and only in Taboo+."}}, "6033": {"college": {"stem": "In the nuclear-energy results of the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), how did participants holding a sacred value respond to Taboo+ and Taboo− compared with the plain Taboo deal?", "options": ["Both additions lowered approval and raised expected anger, with the added funds producing the most anger.", "Both additions raised approval above the plain deal and removed nearly all expected anger.", "Neither addition changed their approval, which stayed as high as that of participants without a sacred value.", "Added funds raised their approval, while added sanctions lowered approval and increased expected anger."], "correct": 0, "why": "For sacred-value participants, approval dropped from about 2.9 (Taboo) to about 1.9 (Taboo+) and 2.3 (Taboo−), and anger rose from about 3.2 to about 4.6 and 3.9. Funds did not raise approval."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the nuclear-energy results of the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), how did participants without a sacred value respond?", "options": ["They gave fairly high approval and low anger to all three deals.", "They gave low approval and high anger to all three deals.", "They approved only the deal that added economic funds.", "They approved only the deal that added extra sanctions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants without a sacred value gave approval of about 4.5–4.8 and anger of about 2.2 across all three deals. The big shifts appeared only in the sacred-value group."}}, "6034": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to show that ease of recall, not true frequency, drives judgments of probability. Which design most directly tests this?", "options": ["Keep two events equally frequent, make one more distinctive or more often recalled, then collect probability judgments.", "Change both frequency and exposure together, then credit any difference in judgment to availability alone.", "Have participants pick an occupation from a personality sketch without ever measuring how easily any relevant instance is recalled.", "Count the conjunctions in a sentence and treat that count as a measure of how accessible memories are."], "correct": 0, "why": "Distinctiveness and repeated retrieval can make an event more available without changing its frequency, so holding frequency constant isolates availability. Changing both together confounds the two."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which factors can make an event easier to remember, and so seem more probable?", "options": ["Distinctiveness, emotional salience, and how often it is encoded or retrieved.", "Its actual frequency in the population, and nothing else about the event at all.", "How well it matches the typical member of a category.", "Whether it combines two separate events into a single one."], "correct": 0, "why": "Distinctiveness, emotional salience and frequency of encoding or retrieval make events stand out in memory, which feeds the availability heuristic. Category match is representativeness."}}, "6035": {"college": {"stem": "A respondent can define a base rate correctly but ignores it after hearing a description that fits a category. How does this explanation differ from an availability account?", "options": ["Category membership is judged by resemblance to a typical member, not by how easily instances are recalled.", "The judgment rests on counting memorable news stories about each group and using their ease of retrieval as the cue.", "The judgment depends only on which event occurred most recently in a list the person recalls.", "The judgment follows the rule that a conjunction cannot exceed the probability of either part."], "correct": 0, "why": "Ignoring a base rate because a description fits a category is representativeness, which relies on similarity. Availability relies on how easily instances come to mind, as with memorable news stories."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which situation shows the representativeness heuristic rather than availability?", "options": ["Guessing that a quiet, bookish man is a librarian rather than a farmer.", "Thinking plane crashes are common after seeing vivid news coverage of one.", "Thinking more words start with r because those words come to mind easily.", "Thinking botulism is a common killer because its deaths make headlines."], "correct": 0, "why": "Judging Robert a librarian because he resembles one is representativeness. The other three judge probability by how easily instances come to mind, which is availability."}}, "6036": {"college": {"stem": "When judging whether descriptive information about Jack has displaced the base rate, which fact must stay in view?", "options": ["The group still contains 70 lawyers and 30 engineers after Jack's personality description is added.", "Jack's stated age of 45 should be read as the probability that he is an engineer.", "Jack's hobbies should be counted as extra engineers and added to the group total.", "The group must be redefined to hold only engineers before any judgment about Jack's occupation can be made."], "correct": 0, "why": "The description changes what people attend to, not the numbers: there are still 70 lawyers and 30 engineers. Base-rate neglect is an error precisely because that information remains available."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Jack problem, what does the description of Jack change?", "options": ["What people focus on, but not the 70-to-30 makeup of the group.", "The group itself, so that most members are now engineers.", "Nothing at all about how people answer the question.", "The group itself, so that it now contains only lawyers."], "correct": 0, "why": "The description shifts attention to Jack's engineer-like traits, but the group still has 70 lawyers and 30 engineers. People's answers do change: they say Jack is an engineer."}}, "6037": {"college": {"stem": "Picture all bank tellers as one circle, with a smaller circle of feminist bank tellers inside it. What does this set relationship settle about the Linda problem?", "options": ["The joint event cannot be more probable than being a bank teller, however well Linda fits the added description.", "The smaller circle is more probable, because a more specific description always contains more people than a general one.", "Linda cannot possibly be a bank teller, because she does not fit the typical bank teller.", "The two circles are unrelated, because sharing one condition cannot limit who belongs to each."], "correct": 0, "why": "Every feminist bank teller is inside the bank-teller circle, so the conjunction cannot exceed the single event. That holds regardless of how well the description fits."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did most people choose in the Linda problem?", "options": ["Most chose that Linda is a bank teller and a feminist.", "Most chose that Linda is simply a bank teller.", "Most said both options were equally likely for Linda.", "Most refused to choose because the description was vague."], "correct": 0, "why": "82% picked “bank teller and active in the feminist movement,” violating the conjunction rule. The single event, bank teller, is at least as probable."}}, "6038": {"college": {"stem": "A person picks the larger hospital because it delivers more boys over the year. Which correction addresses the hospital question as it was asked?", "options": ["The outcome is days above a percentage cutoff, so compare how variable daily proportions are, not yearly totals.", "The outcome is the yearly count of boys, so the percentage cutoff can be dropped as irrelevant.", "The smaller hospital must have a different underlying sex ratio, since it has fewer births per day.", "Neither hospital can exceed half boys on any day, since the population proportion is one half."], "correct": 0, "why": "More births can mean more boys in absolute terms while producing fewer extreme daily percentages. The question is about daily proportions crossing 60%, which vary more in small samples."}, "simple": {"stem": "Why is “the larger hospital” the wrong answer to the hospital problem?", "options": ["Larger daily samples stay closer to 50% boys, so extreme days are rarer.", "Larger hospitals keep less accurate records of the babies who are born there each day.", "Larger hospitals deliver fewer boys than girls over a full year.", "Larger hospitals never have a day with more boys than girls."], "correct": 0, "why": "Larger samples are more representative of the population, so the larger hospital rarely strays above 60% boys. The smaller hospital records more such days."}}, "6039": {"college": {"stem": "Which finding would most directly support the claim that stereotypes produce illusory correlations?", "options": ["Attention and recall favor stereotype-consistent behaviors, and the perceived link exceeds the actual relationship.", "Every behavior receives equal attention from the observer, and the perceived link matches the observed relationship exactly.", "The observer never notices or remembers any behavior performed by members of the group.", "The perceived link appears only when the group happens to contain more than thirty people."], "correct": 0, "why": "The proposed mechanism is selective attention to stereotypical behaviors, which makes them more available and inflates the perceived correlation. Equal attention with an accurate link would show no illusion."}, "simple": {"stem": "How can a stereotype create an illusory correlation?", "options": ["People pay extra attention to behaviors that fit the stereotype.", "People record every behavior of the group with equal care.", "People forget every behavior that fits the stereotype.", "People form stereotypes only about very large groups."], "correct": 0, "why": "A stereotype draws attention to matching behaviors, making them more available and creating an illusory correlation that reinforces the stereotype."}}, "6040": {"college": {"stem": "In the study by Lord and colleagues (1979), supporters and opponents of capital punishment read the same article, and each side discounted the parts that disagreed with it. Which concept best describes this?", "options": ["Myside bias, a tendency to evaluate evidence in a way biased toward one's own opinions.", "The conjunction rule, since the article combined two separate claims about punishment.", "The law of large numbers, since the two sides formed samples of different sizes.", "Expected utility theory, since each person maximized a monetary payoff for agreeing."], "correct": 0, "why": "Myside bias is the tendency to generate and evaluate evidence in a way biased toward one's own opinions and attitudes. The other concepts concern probability or payoffs, not attitude-driven evaluation."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the myside bias?", "options": ["Evaluating evidence in a way that favors your own opinions.", "Judging events as likely when they are easy to remember.", "Ignoring base rates when a description fits a category.", "Choosing the option with the highest expected payoff."], "correct": 0, "why": "Myside bias, a type of confirmation bias, is generating and evaluating evidence in a way biased toward one's own opinions and attitudes."}}, "6041": {"college": {"stem": "In the 2, 4, 6 number-rule task, which kind of test triple gives more information than another triple increasing by two?", "options": ["A triple such as 1, 5, 20, which could separate “increasing by two” from “any increasing numbers.”", "Another triple chosen only because it fits every feature of the current increasing-by-two hypothesis.", "A request that the experimenter stop saying whether each triple fits the rule or not.", "A decision to announce the current hypothesis as correct without considering any other rule."], "correct": 0, "why": "A triple that the narrow hypothesis forbids but the broader rule allows can reveal that the narrow rule is wrong. More confirming triples leave the two hypotheses indistinguishable."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is confirmation bias?", "options": ["Looking for evidence that supports your hypothesis and overlooking evidence against it.", "Testing your hypothesis by deliberately searching for evidence that could prove it wrong.", "Changing your hypothesis every time you receive any new feedback.", "Judging an event as likely because it resembles a typical case."], "correct": 0, "why": "Confirmation bias is the tendency to selectively look for information that conforms to our hypothesis and overlook information against it. Seeking disconfirming evidence is the opposite strategy."}}, "6042": {"college": {"stem": "A person describing the jellybean choice says only “seven red beans versus one red bean.” What information is missing for comparing the two bowls?", "options": ["The total number of beans in each bowl, needed to compare chances of 7/100 and 1/10.", "The age of the person who filled the bowls, which sets all of the drawing probabilities.", "The total number of dollars earned by every participant over the whole experiment.", "Whether the color red was named using two separate adjectives combined together."], "correct": 0, "why": "The chance of winning depends on the number of red beans relative to the total in the bowl. Seven of 100 is worse than one of 10 even though seven is the larger count."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the jellybean study, which bowl gave the better chance of drawing a red bean?", "options": ["The bowl with 1 red bean out of 10.", "The bowl with 7 red beans out of 100.", "Both bowls gave exactly the same chance.", "Neither bowl contained any red beans at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "One in 10 is a 10% chance, while 7 in 100 is only 7%. Most participants nonetheless chose the 7-in-100 bowl."}}, "6043": {"college": {"stem": "In Ritov and Baron's (1999) convoy dilemma, 12% of respondents would not reroute the convoy even if only 1 person at the first camp would die while 1,000 were saved. Which account does this response most directly contradict?", "options": ["One in which decisions are determined solely by maximizing the net number of lives saved.", "One in which the first camp receives no food after the convoy has been rerouted.", "One in which decisions can involve considerations beyond the numerical balance of outcomes.", "One in which the convoy is able to choose between two different refugee camps."], "correct": 0, "why": "With only one life lost against 1,000 saved, a pure net-lives calculation strongly favors rerouting, so refusing contradicts that account. It is consistent with decisions involving other considerations."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Ritov and Baron's (1999) convoy dilemma, what did 12% of respondents say?", "options": ["They would not reroute even if only one person would die.", "They would reroute even if 500 people at the first camp died.", "They would refuse to answer the question about the convoy.", "They would send half of the trucks to each of the camps."], "correct": 0, "why": "12% said they would not change the convoy's route even if only one person in the first camp would die. Such refusals show that outcome counts alone do not drive these choices."}}, "6044": {"college": {"stem": "Someone summarizes the results of Tetlock and colleagues (2000) as “saving Johnny affects outrage equally in both tradeoff types.” Which observation contradicts this summary?", "options": ["The gap between saving and not saving Johnny is large under taboo but nearly absent under tragic.", "The tragic condition has no outcome in which any child receives a liver transplant at all.", "The taboo condition includes no money and so cannot be compared with the tragic condition.", "Every bar in the results shows exactly the same outrage rating across all of the conditions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Under taboo, outrage was about 5.4 versus 2.4 for not saving versus saving Johnny; under tragic, both were near 3.8. The outcome's effect depends on tradeoff type."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Tetlock and colleagues' (2000) tragic tradeoff, how did outrage compare for saving versus not saving Johnny?", "options": ["Outrage was about the same whether or not Johnny was saved.", "Outrage was much higher when Johnny was not saved.", "Outrage was much higher when Johnny was saved.", "There was no outrage at all in either case."], "correct": 0, "why": "In the tragic tradeoff, outrage was similar (around 3.7–3.8) for both outcomes, because another child also needed the liver. The large gap appeared only in the taboo tradeoff."}}, "6045": {"college": {"stem": "In the results of Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), how did responses to the Symbolic offer differ from responses to adding money to a taboo deal?", "options": ["The Symbolic offer lowered anger and support for violence, while added money raised both among moral absolutists.", "The Symbolic offer was the same yearly payment as Taboo+, only stated in a different currency.", "Both additions raised anger and support for violence by equal amounts in every participant group.", "Neither addition changed any substantive term of the agreement, so responses were identical across all of the offers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Compared with the taboo deal, the Symbolic offer produced less anger or disgust and less support for violence, whereas Taboo+ increased both among moral absolutists. Symbolic recognition and money engage different kinds of value."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the results of Ginges, Atran, Medin & Shikaki (2007), what happened when Israel's side of the deal included recognition and an apology?", "options": ["Anger and support for violence went down compared with the taboo deal.", "Anger and support for violence went up compared with the taboo deal.", "Anger went down, but support for violence rose sharply compared with the taboo deal.", "Nothing changed, because symbolic gestures had no effect at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "The Symbolic offer lowered anger or disgust and dropped support for violence below zero, compared with the taboo deal. Money had the opposite effect for moral absolutists."}}, "6046": {"college": {"stem": "A non-absolutist participant in the study by Ginges and colleagues (2007) strongly opposes a proposed agreement. Why would it be wrong to call this person indifferent just because the issue is not sacred to them?", "options": ["Non-absolutists can strongly oppose compromise while still letting benefits and consequences shape their judgment.", "Lacking a sacred value means that a person cannot hold any strong opinion, positive or negative, about the disputed issue.", "Everyone who dislikes an agreement refuses it regardless of every possible consequence.", "Moral absolutism is set by a person's group membership rather than by their own answers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Non-absolutists had strong preferences against compromise but did not regard the issues as sacred. The difference from absolutists is about trading off consequences, not about caring."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the study by Ginges and colleagues (2007), what was true of non-absolutists?", "options": ["They strongly opposed compromise but did not treat the issues as sacred.", "They had no opinion at all about the disputed issues.", "They refused any compromise on the issues, no matter how great the benefit offered.", "They were all Palestinian students from one political group."], "correct": 0, "why": "Non-absolutists had strong preferences against compromise but did not regard the issues as sacred values. Refusing regardless of benefit describes moral absolutists."}}, "6047": {"college": {"stem": "In the Iranian nuclear-program studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), which statement keeps the sacred-value measure separate from the dependent variables?", "options": ["Refusal regardless of consequences identified a sacred value, while approval, anger, and misleadingness measured reactions.", "Approval alone determined how many participants were included, while all of the other responses were unrelated filler items.", "Every participant held a sacred value, simply because the study was conducted in Farsi.", "The number 1,997 gives the year the study was run rather than the number of participants."], "correct": 0, "why": "The sacred-value measure sorted participants by whether they would refuse no matter the consequences; approval, expected anger and perceived misleadingness were the dependent variables. The study had 1,997 participants."}, "simple": {"stem": "What were the dependent variables in the Iranian nuclear-program studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009)?", "options": ["Approval of the deal, expected public anger, and whether it seemed misleading.", "How much money each participant would personally accept to give up the nuclear program.", "How quickly participants could recall the details of each deal.", "Whether participants spoke Farsi or English at home with family."], "correct": 0, "why": "The dependent variables were people's approval, people's anger and a misleading rating. Sacred-value status was measured separately."}}, "6048": {"college": {"stem": "In the Iranian nuclear scenarios (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), what exactly were participants asked when anger was measured?", "options": ["Whether people would be angry if the Iranian government approved such a deal.", "How angry they themselves had felt after seeing an unrelated news article.", "How many sanctions the United Nations had already imposed on Iran.", "Whether nuclear facilities produce a measurable physical anger response in workers."], "correct": 0, "why": "The anger question asked whether people would be angry if the Iranian government approved the deal. It is separate from personal approval and from the misleading question."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Iranian nuclear scenarios (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), which question measured anger?", "options": ["If the government approves the deal, will people be angry?", "Do you personally approve of this deal being made?", "If this deal is really proposed, is it misleading?", "Should Iran give up its program no matter what?"], "correct": 0, "why": "Anger was measured by asking whether people would be angry if the Iranian government approved the deal. The other questions measured approval, misleadingness, or sacred-value status."}}, "6049": {"college": {"stem": "In the nuclear-energy results of the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), participants without a sacred value showed similar low anger across all three deals, while those with a sacred value were angriest when funds were added. Which conclusion stays within these observations?", "options": ["Reactions to economic additions depend on sacred-value status, at least for energy approval and anger.", "The weapons results and misleading ratings must show exactly the same values as the energy results.", "Every Iranian participant regards nuclear energy as a sacred value that cannot be traded away.", "Adding funds raised approval among sacred-value participants above the level for the plain deal."], "correct": 0, "why": "The energy results show sacred-value status changing reactions to added funds and sanctions. They do not report weapons or misleading ratings, only 14% held a sacred value for energy, and funds lowered approval in that group."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the nuclear-energy results of the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), which deal made sacred-value participants angriest?", "options": ["The Taboo+ deal, which added $40 billion in economic funds.", "The Taboo deal, with no funds or sanctions added at all.", "The Taboo− deal, which added the threat of extra UN sanctions.", "All three deals produced exactly the same amount of anger."], "correct": 0, "why": "Anger among sacred-value participants was highest for Taboo+ (about 4.6), then Taboo− (about 3.9), then plain Taboo (about 3.2)."}}, "6050": {"college": {"stem": "Which comparison best isolates the effect of adding an economic reward to the proposed nuclear deal in the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009)?", "options": ["Taboo versus Taboo+ for the same program type and sacred-value status, keeping the reciprocal deal in both.", "Energy under Taboo versus weapons under Taboo−, crediting every single difference between them to the added funds alone.", "The number of participants versus the number of deal types, calling the gap an approval effect.", "Approval of the deal versus whether a participant speaks Farsi, without changing the offer at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Taboo+ is the Taboo deal plus $40 billion in funds, so comparing them with program type and sacred-value status held constant leaves the money as the only difference."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Iranian studies (Dehghani, Atran et al., 2010; Dehghani, Iliev et al., 2009), what did the Taboo+ deal add to the basic Taboo deal?", "options": ["The United Nations would give Iran $40 billion in economic funds.", "The United Nations would impose additional sanctions if Iran refused.", "Israel would apologize to Iran for all of its past wrongs.", "Iran would be allowed to keep its nuclear weapons program."], "correct": 0, "why": "Taboo+ added $40 billion in UN economic funds to the reciprocal Taboo deal. Extra sanctions for refusing defined Taboo−."}}, "7001": {"college": {"stem": "After an injury to part of the prefrontal cortex, a man reports that very little stirs him emotionally, and his everyday choices become noticeably worse. What does this case suggest about emotion and decision making?", "options": ["Emotion appears to support sound decisions, since weakened emotion accompanies poorer choices.", "Emotion appears to interfere with decisions, so weakened emotion should produce better choices.", "Prefrontal damage harms choices only in people who were very optimistic beforehand.", "Weakened emotion alters memory for earlier choices but leaves new choices untouched."], "correct": 0, "why": "People with damage to an area of the prefrontal cortex who suffer from flattened emotions have impaired decision making, so emotion contributes to good choices. The idea that removing emotion improves choices runs opposite to that finding."}, "simple": {"stem": "People with damage to part of the prefrontal cortex can have flattened emotions. How does this affect their decisions?", "options": ["Their ability to make good decisions is impaired.", "Their decisions become more accurate and careful.", "Their decisions stay exactly the same as before.", "They lose the ability to make any choice."], "correct": 0, "why": "Flattened emotions after prefrontal damage go with impaired decision making. Losing emotion does not make decisions better or leave them unchanged."}}, "7002": {"college": {"stem": "A nervous homeowner turns down every investment that carries any chance of a large loss, even when success is likely. Which pattern, typical of anxious people, does this describe?", "options": ["Risk avoidance, steering away from choices that could bring large negative consequences.", "Status quo bias, sticking with whatever option happens to be the default.", "Belief bias, accepting a conclusion mainly because it sounds believable.", "Excessive optimism, ignoring negative information and attending only to positive information."], "correct": 0, "why": "Anxious people tend to avoid decisions that could lead to large negative consequences, which is risk avoidance. Excessive optimism is the opposite pattern, in which negative information is ignored."}, "simple": {"stem": "Anxious people tend to avoid choices that could lead to big negative outcomes. What is this called?", "options": ["This pattern is called risk avoidance.", "This pattern is called the framing effect.", "This pattern is called belief bias.", "This pattern is called the status quo bias."], "correct": 0, "why": "Avoiding decisions that could bring large negative consequences is risk avoidance. The framing effect, belief bias and the status quo bias describe other tendencies."}}, "7003": {"college": {"stem": "Before agreeing to a wager, a woman pictures how disappointed she will be if she loses. Which term best fits the feeling she is anticipating?", "options": ["An expected emotion, one a person predicts feeling about an outcome.", "An incidental emotion, one unrelated to the decision and arising from surroundings.", "A permission schema, a familiar rule linking a condition to an allowed action.", "A flattened emotion, one dulled by damage to the prefrontal cortex."], "correct": 0, "why": "Expected emotions are emotions people predict they will feel concerning an outcome, and they help determine risk aversion. An incidental emotion is unrelated to the decision itself."}, "simple": {"stem": "What are expected emotions?", "options": ["Emotions people predict they will feel about an outcome.", "Emotions caused by the weather or surroundings that day.", "Emotions people felt in the past about a similar choice.", "Emotions that other people show while making a decision."], "correct": 0, "why": "Expected emotions are the feelings people predict they will have about an outcome. Feelings caused by the weather or surroundings are incidental emotions."}}, "7004": {"college": {"stem": "A man declines a coin-flip bet to win or lose $20 because he is sure that losing would feel far worse than winning would feel good. Which principle explains the refusal?", "options": ["Believing a loss will weigh more than an equal gain increases risk aversion.", "Believing a gain will weigh more than an equal loss increases risk aversion.", "Expected emotions influence risk taking but play no part in avoiding risk.", "People forecast their feelings accurately, so his choice mirrors his true reactions."], "correct": 0, "why": "The belief that a loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size increases risk aversion. People also predict their emotions inaccurately, so the forecast need not match his real reaction."}, "simple": {"stem": "Some people believe a loss will affect them more than a gain of the same size. What does this belief do?", "options": ["It makes them more likely to avoid taking risks.", "It makes them more likely to seek out risks.", "It makes them predict their feelings perfectly.", "It has no effect on the risks they take."], "correct": 0, "why": "Believing a loss will hurt more than an equal gain helps increases risk aversion. It does not make people seek risk, and people predict their feelings inaccurately."}}, "7005": {"college": {"stem": "A researcher wants to test whether people misjudge how they will feel about outcomes. Which comparison would test this most directly?", "options": ["Compare the feelings people forecast for an outcome with those they report once it occurs.", "Compare how often people pick a sure option when outcomes are worded as gains versus losses.", "Compare organ-donor consent rates in countries using opt-in versus opt-out procedures.", "Compare how often people turn over the E card versus the 4 card in the four-card problem."], "correct": 0, "why": "Showing that predictions are inaccurate requires placing predicted feelings beside actual feelings, the comparison Kermer and colleagues (2006) made. The other comparisons concern framing, defaults and conditional reasoning."}, "simple": {"stem": "How could a researcher check whether people predict their own feelings accurately?", "options": ["Compare predicted feelings with the feelings people report later.", "Ask people only how they feel before anything happens.", "Ask people only how they feel after the outcome happens.", "Compare how the same people feel on sunny days and on cloudy days."], "correct": 0, "why": "Accuracy can be judged only by comparing the predicted feeling with the actual feeling. Asking at just one time point gives nothing to compare."}}, "7006": {"college": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) study, participants received $5 and were told a coin flip would decide what happened next. What were the two possible results of the flip?", "options": ["They would win an additional $5 or else lose $3.", "They would win an additional $3 or else lose $5.", "They would double their $5 or else lose all of it.", "They would keep their $5 or else hand it to someone else."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants were given $5 and told that a coin flip would decide whether they won another $5 or lost $3. The amounts were not reversed, and no outcome involved losing everything."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) coin-flip study, what could participants win or lose?", "options": ["They could win $5 more or lose $3.", "They could win $3 more or lose $5.", "They could win $10 more or lose $10.", "They could win a prize or lose nothing."], "correct": 0, "why": "After receiving $5, participants learned they would either win an additional $5 or lose $3 on a coin flip."}}, "7007": {"college": {"stem": "When Kermer and colleagues (2006) compared predicted and actual happiness after losing the coin flip, what did they find?", "options": ["Participants overestimated their negative feeling, because the loss hurt far less than they had forecast.", "Participants underestimated their negative feeling, because the loss hurt far more than they had forecast.", "Predicted and actual unhappiness after the loss turned out to be the same size.", "Losing raised happiness above baseline, whereas winning lowered it below baseline."], "correct": 0, "why": "Before the flip, people expected losing to lower their happiness by roughly 4 points, but the actual drop was only about 1 point. They overestimated their negative feeling rather than underestimating it."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Kermer and colleagues (2006) find about how people expected to feel after losing?", "options": ["People expected to feel worse than they really did.", "People expected to feel better than they really did.", "People predicted their feelings exactly right.", "People felt happier after losing than after winning."], "correct": 0, "why": "People overestimated their negative feeling: losing hurt much less than they predicted."}}, "7008": {"college": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) coin-flip study, why were participants’ forecasts of their feelings after losing so far off?", "options": ["Coping mechanisms kick in after a loss, but people ignore them when predicting emotions.", "Participants forgot how much money was at stake before making their forecasts.", "The happiness scale gave participants no way at all to report negative feelings about losing.", "Participants had been told they would win, so the loss came as a shock."], "correct": 0, "why": "We usually have coping mechanisms that get activated after a loss, but we do not take them into account when predicting emotions. Those mechanisms soften the actual feeling."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) study, why did people overestimate how bad losing would feel?", "options": ["They did not consider that coping would soften the feeling.", "They forgot how much money they could lose.", "They were told ahead of time that they would surely win the bet.", "They were unable to feel any negative emotions."], "correct": 0, "why": "Coping mechanisms get activated after a loss, but people do not consider them when predicting how they will feel."}}, "7009": {"college": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) study, participants expected losing $3 to lower their happiness about twice as much as winning $5 would raise it. Why is this result striking?", "options": ["The loss was the smaller sum of money, yet it was expected to matter more than the larger gain.", "The gain was the smaller sum of money, so a smaller expected rise fits the amounts exactly.", "The two sums were equal, so any gap in expected feeling must be random error.", "The forecasts were collected after the flip, so they reflect actual rather than predicted feelings."], "correct": 0, "why": "Losing $3 is less money than winning $5, yet people expected the loss to change their happiness about twice as much. This fits the belief that losses have a greater impact than gains."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Kermer and colleagues’ (2006) study, which outcome did people expect to affect their happiness more?", "options": ["Losing $3, even though it was less money than the $5 gain.", "Winning $5, because it was the larger amount of money.", "Both outcomes were expected to matter exactly the same.", "Neither outcome was expected to change their happiness."], "correct": 0, "why": "People expected losing $3 to lower happiness about twice as much as winning $5 would raise it, even though the loss was the smaller amount."}}, "7010": {"college": {"stem": "After a frustrating commute, a manager makes a hasty hiring decision later that morning. What kind of emotion may be shaping the choice?", "options": ["An incidental emotion, one unrelated to the decision and arising from a recent experience.", "An expected emotion, one the manager forecasts feeling about how the new hire eventually turns out.", "A permission schema, a familiar rule stating when an action may be carried out.", "A mental model, an imagined situation used to check whether a conclusion is valid."], "correct": 0, "why": "Incidental emotions are not specifically related to the decision and may come from disposition, a recent experience or the surroundings. An expected emotion would concern the outcome of the hire itself."}, "simple": {"stem": "What are incidental emotions?", "options": ["Emotions not specifically related to the decision being made.", "Emotions that people predict they will feel about an outcome later.", "Emotions that come only from the decision itself.", "Emotions that appear only after damage to the brain."], "correct": 0, "why": "Incidental emotions are not specifically related to the decision; they can come from personality, a recent experience or the surroundings. Predicted feelings about an outcome are expected emotions."}}, "7011": {"college": {"stem": "Simonsohn (2007) analyzed university admissions decisions made under different weather conditions. What pattern emerged?", "options": ["Applicants’ academic attributes received more weight on cloudy days than on sunny days.", "Applicants’ academic attributes received more weight on sunny days than on cloudy days.", "The weather on the day of review had no detectable effect on the decisions.", "Applicants who toured the campus on cloudy days were less likely to be admitted."], "correct": 0, "why": "Simonsohn (2007) found academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days, so an incidental factor shifted what decision makers emphasized. The cloudy-day campus visit finding is a separate study about enrollment, Simonsohn (2009)."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Simonsohn (2007) find about university admissions decisions and the weather?", "options": ["Academic qualities counted more on cloudy days.", "Academic qualities counted more on sunny days.", "The weather made no difference to admissions decisions.", "Admissions officers rejected more applicants on rainy days."], "correct": 0, "why": "Applicants’ academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than on sunny days, showing an incidental influence on decisions."}}, "7012": {"college": {"stem": "In Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) study, participants watched one of three film clips before pricing highlighters. Which clip was used to induce disgust?", "options": ["A clip showing a person using a dirty toilet.", "A clip showing a person who was dying.", "A clip showing fish at the Great Barrier Reef.", "A clip showing a person selling a box of highlighters."], "correct": 0, "why": "The disgust clip showed a person using a dirty toilet. The clip of a person dying created sadness, and the Great Barrier Reef clip was neutral."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) study, which film clip was meant to make people feel disgusted?", "options": ["A person using a dirty toilet.", "A person who was dying.", "Fish swimming at the Great Barrier Reef.", "A person shopping for highlighters."], "correct": 0, "why": "The dirty-toilet clip was the disgust condition; the dying person was the sad condition and the reef was neutral."}}, "7013": {"college": {"stem": "Relative to the neutral group in Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) study, how did sad participants price the highlighters?", "options": ["They set a lower selling price and offered a higher buying price.", "They set a higher selling price and offered a lower buying price.", "They set lower prices for both selling and buying the highlighters.", "They set selling and buying prices equal to those of the neutral group."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sad participants asked about $3.00 to sell, below the neutral group’s roughly $4.50, and offered about $4.50 to buy, above the neutral roughly $3.50. Lowering both prices fits the disgust group more closely than the sad group."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) study, how did sadness change what people would pay to buy the highlighters?", "options": ["Sad people offered more to buy than neutral people.", "Sad people offered less to buy than neutral people.", "Sad people offered the same as neutral people.", "Sad people refused to buy the highlighters at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Sad participants offered about $4.50 to buy, compared with about $3.50 in the neutral group."}}, "7014": {"college": {"stem": "Why do the highlighter prices in Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) study count as evidence about incidental emotions?", "options": ["The clips were unrelated to highlighters, so price shifts reflect mood unrelated to the decision.", "The clips were all about highlighters, so price shifts reflect expected feelings about owning one.", "Every group watched the same clip, so price shifts must come from personality alone.", "Participants picked their own clip, so each mood matched the price they already preferred."], "correct": 0, "why": "Each clip created a mood unrelated to the highlighters, so a change in price shows that a person’s mood can influence decision making. The clips were not about highlighters, and the groups saw different clips."}, "simple": {"stem": "What did Lerner and colleagues’ (2004) film-clip study show about decisions?", "options": ["A person’s mood can influence their decisions.", "Prices depend only on the real value of the item.", "Mood affects decisions only when it concerns the item.", "Film clips make people refuse to buy anything."], "correct": 0, "why": "Unrelated clips changed the prices people set, which shows that a person’s mood can influence decision making."}}, "7015": {"college": {"stem": "Physicians judged the same hypothetical cesarean candidate under three conditions. After which preceding cases did significantly more of them recommend a cesarean?", "options": ["After first reviewing four fairly routine cases without serious complications.", "After first reviewing four cases that each involved serious medical complications.", "When the test case came first, with no cases before it.", "After reviewing the same test case twice in a row."], "correct": 0, "why": "Significantly more doctors recommended a cesarean after first reviewing non-serious cases. The test case itself never changed, so the preceding cases changed the decision."}, "simple": {"stem": "Doctors judged a possible cesarean case after looking at other cases. When did more doctors recommend a cesarean?", "options": ["When the earlier cases were routine and not serious.", "When the earlier cases had serious complications.", "When the test case came with no earlier cases.", "When the doctors saw the test case two times."], "correct": 0, "why": "More doctors recommended a cesarean when the test case followed four fairly routine cases."}}, "7016": {"college": {"stem": "Why does the cesarean study with physicians support the claim that decisions depend on the context in which they are made?", "options": ["The test case stayed identical across conditions, and only the cases reviewed before it differed.", "The test case differed across conditions, while the cases reviewed before it stayed the same.", "Physicians in each condition were told which recommendation the researchers expected.", "Each physician judged a different patient, so differences reflect the patients themselves."], "correct": 0, "why": "Holding the test case constant and varying only the preceding cases isolates the effect of context on the recommendation. If the test case itself had changed, context could not be singled out."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the cesarean study with physicians, what stayed the same across all three conditions?", "options": ["The test case that the doctors had to judge.", "The cases that the doctors reviewed beforehand.", "The number of serious cases the doctors saw first.", "The recommendation that every doctor gave."], "correct": 0, "why": "The test case was the same in every condition; only the cases before it changed, and the recommendations differed."}}, "7017": {"college": {"stem": "A country changes its organ-donation system from requiring people to sign up to counting everyone as a donor unless they ask not to be. What shift in consent would be expected?", "options": ["Consent would rise sharply, since people tend to stay with the default and do nothing.", "Consent would drop sharply, since people resist a default that has been imposed.", "Consent would stay unchanged, since donation choices rest only on their benefits.", "Consent would rise only among people who already felt anxious about donating."], "correct": 0, "why": "Opt-in countries have consent rates below 28%, whereas opt-out countries have rates above 99%. The status quo bias, doing nothing when facing a difficult decision, keeps people in whichever option is the default."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which organ-donation system leads to more people becoming donors?", "options": ["Opt-out, where people are donors unless they ask not to be.", "Opt-in, where people must take an active step to sign up as donors.", "Both systems lead to the same number of donors.", "Neither system has any effect on how many donate."], "correct": 0, "why": "Opt-out countries have far higher consent rates than opt-in countries, because people tend to do nothing and keep the default."}}, "7018": {"college": {"stem": "Faced with a hard choice among retirement plans, an employee simply leaves her savings in the plan she was automatically enrolled in. Which tendency does this illustrate?", "options": ["The status quo bias, the tendency to do nothing when facing a difficult decision.", "The framing effect, the tendency to choose differently depending on how options are worded.", "Belief bias, the tendency to accept conclusions that sound believable.", "Risk taking under a loss frame, the tendency to favor a gamble."], "correct": 0, "why": "The status quo bias is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision, so the default plan stays in place. Nothing about the wording of the plans or a gamble is involved."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the status quo bias?", "options": ["The tendency to do nothing when a decision is difficult.", "The tendency to always choose the newest option.", "The tendency to take risks when facing losses.", "The tendency to trust conclusions that sound believable."], "correct": 0, "why": "The status quo bias is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision. Trusting believable conclusions is belief bias."}}, "7019": {"college": {"stem": "In the short story “The International Community,” a king’s chef asks the summoned birds which sauce they would like to be eaten with, and dismisses a bird that does not want to be eaten at all. What point about decisions does the story illustrate?", "options": ["The options offered can limit a choice, since not being eaten was never among them.", "The birds could decline to be eaten simply by asking the chef politely.", "The chef let the birds choose between being eaten and being set free.", "The birds picked their preferred sauce long before they were ever summoned to the summit."], "correct": 0, "why": "The chef rules out not being eaten, so the only choice offered is the sauce. Decisions depend on how choices are set up, and here the most important option was never on the table."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the story about the chef and the birds, what choice were the birds really given?", "options": ["They could choose only the sauce they would be eaten with.", "They could choose whether or not to be eaten.", "They could choose to leave the summit and fly safely back home.", "They could choose which bird would be eaten first."], "correct": 0, "why": "The chef offered a choice of sauce only and refused the option of not being eaten, showing how the way choices are set up shapes a decision."}}, "7020": {"college": {"stem": "One patient hears that a surgery has a 90% survival rate, another hears that it has a 10% death rate, and the two decide differently. What phenomenon does this illustrate?", "options": ["The framing effect, in which decisions depend on how a choice is stated.", "The status quo bias, in which people do nothing when a choice is difficult.", "Belief bias, in which believable conclusions are judged to be valid.", "An incidental emotion, in which an unrelated mood shapes a choice."], "correct": 0, "why": "The framing effect is that decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated, which can highlight one aspect such as survival or death. The two statements describe the same odds."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the framing effect?", "options": ["Decisions are influenced by how a choice is worded.", "Decisions depend only on the odds of each outcome.", "Decisions are influenced by the weather that day.", "Decisions depend on how many premises an argument has."], "correct": 0, "why": "The framing effect means decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated, not only by the odds."}}, "7021": {"college": {"stem": "Tversky and Kahneman (1981) studied how framing alters risky choices. When a situation is framed in terms of losses, which strategy do people tend to adopt?", "options": ["They lean toward a risk-taking strategy and favor the gamble.", "They lean toward a risk-averse strategy and favor the sure thing.", "They do nothing at all because of the status quo bias.", "They reject every option that is placed in front of them."], "correct": 0, "why": "With a loss frame people tend toward risk taking, and with a gain frame they tend toward risk aversion. Favoring the sure thing is the gain-frame pattern."}, "simple": {"stem": "Tversky and Kahneman (1981) studied framing. When choices are framed as losses, what do people tend to do?", "options": ["They tend to take more risks.", "They tend to avoid risks more.", "They tend to make no choice at all.", "They tend to pick options at random."], "correct": 0, "why": "Loss frames push people toward risk taking; gain frames push them toward risk aversion."}}, "7022": {"college": {"stem": "A disease is expected to kill 600 people. When the two programs to combat it were described in terms of lives saved, which did most people choose?", "options": ["Program A, which saves 200 people for certain, chosen by 72%.", "Program B, the 1/3 chance of saving all 600, chosen by 72%.", "Program A, which saves 200 people for certain, chosen by 28%.", "Program B, the gamble that might save everyone, chosen by 78%."], "correct": 0, "why": "With outcomes stated as lives saved, 72% chose the sure Program A and 28% chose the gamble, Program B. This is the risk-averse pattern expected under a gain frame."}, "simple": {"stem": "A disease is expected to kill 600 people. When the choices were worded as lives saved, what did most people pick?", "options": ["The sure option of saving 200 people.", "The gamble with a 1/3 chance of saving everyone.", "Neither program, because both seemed too risky.", "Both programs, each picked by about half."], "correct": 0, "why": "72% chose Program A, the sure saving of 200 people, over the gamble."}}, "7023": {"college": {"stem": "A disease is expected to kill 600 people. When the two programs were described in terms of deaths, which did most people choose?", "options": ["Program D, the gamble with a 1/3 chance that no one dies, chosen by 78%.", "Program C, under which 400 people die for certain, chosen by 78%.", "Program D, the gamble with a 1/3 chance that no one dies, chosen by 22%.", "Program C, under which 200 people are saved, chosen by 72%."], "correct": 0, "why": "With outcomes stated as deaths, 78% chose the gamble, Program D, and only 22% chose the sure Program C. This is the risk-taking pattern expected under a loss frame."}, "simple": {"stem": "A disease is expected to kill 600 people. When the choices were worded in terms of deaths, what did most people pick?", "options": ["The gamble with a 1/3 chance that no one dies.", "The sure option in which 400 people die.", "Neither program, since both involved deaths.", "Both programs, each picked by about half."], "correct": 0, "why": "78% chose Program D, the gamble, over the sure loss of 400 lives."}}, "7024": {"college": {"stem": "Program A saves 200 of 600 people for certain; Program C means 400 of 600 people die for certain. Why does the drop from 72% choosing A to 22% choosing C demonstrate framing?", "options": ["A and C produce the same outcome, so only the wording differed between versions.", "A saves more people than C, so the drop reflects the better outcome of A.", "C has higher odds of success than A, so the drop is a rational shift.", "A and C concern different diseases, so the two choices cannot be compared."], "correct": 0, "why": "Saving 200 of 600 means 400 die, so A and C are the same outcome. The majority choice changed only because the outcome was stated as saving rather than dying."}, "simple": {"stem": "Program A says 200 of 600 people will be saved. Program C says 400 of 600 people will die. How do these two programs compare?", "options": ["They describe the same result in different words.", "Program A saves more people than Program C.", "Program C saves more people than Program A.", "Program C involves a gamble, while Program A does not."], "correct": 0, "why": "If 200 of 600 are saved, 400 die, so the programs are identical; only the wording differs, which is why the shift in choices shows framing."}}, "7025": {"college": {"stem": "Neuroeconomics brings together research from which three fields?", "options": ["Research from psychology, neuroscience, and economics combined.", "Research from psychology, linguistics, and philosophy combined.", "Research from neuroscience, logic, and computer science combined.", "Research from economics, sociology, and anthropology combined."], "correct": 0, "why": "Neuroeconomics combines research from psychology, neuroscience, and economics. The other lists each leave out at least one of those fields."}, "simple": {"stem": "What fields does neuroeconomics combine?", "options": ["Psychology, neuroscience, and economics", "Psychology, linguistics, and philosophy", "Neuroscience, logic, and computer science", "Economics, sociology, and anthropology"], "correct": 0, "why": "Neuroeconomics combines psychology, neuroscience, and economics."}}, "7026": {"college": {"stem": "A team scans participants’ brains while they decide whether to accept offers of money. Which neuroeconomics finding would guide their predictions?", "options": ["Decisions are swayed by emotions, which are linked to activity in specific brain areas.", "Money decisions are purely rational calculations and are unrelated to emotional brain activity.", "Brain activity during choices tracks only the size of the sum offered.", "Emotion sways decisions only when people are told how a choice is framed."], "correct": 0, "why": "One finding of neuroeconomics is that decisions are influenced by emotions, and those emotions are associated with activity in specific brain areas. Treating money decisions as purely rational ignores that finding."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is one finding of neuroeconomics?", "options": ["Emotions influence decisions and are tied to certain brain areas.", "Decisions are made without any influence at all from a person’s emotions.", "Only the amount of money matters in a decision.", "Brain activity has no connection to decision making."], "correct": 0, "why": "Neuroeconomics finds that emotions influence decisions and that those emotions are tied to activity in specific brain areas."}}, "7027": {"college": {"stem": "In the ultimatum game used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003), a proposer receives $10 and offers the responder a split. What happens if the responder rejects the offer?", "options": ["Neither player receives any of the money.", "The proposer keeps the entire $10 alone.", "The responder receives the entire $10 instead.", "The money is divided evenly between both players."], "correct": 0, "why": "The responder can accept or reject the proposed split, and if the responder rejects it, neither player receives anything. The proposer does not keep the money."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the ultimatum game used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003), what happens when the responder says no to the offer?", "options": ["Both players walk away with nothing.", "The proposer keeps all of the money.", "The responder gets all of the money.", "The two players split the money evenly."], "correct": 0, "why": "A rejection means neither player receives anything."}}, "7028": {"college": {"stem": "Why does the rule that a rejection leaves both players with nothing make the ultimatum game, as used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003), useful for studying emotion?", "options": ["A rejection gives up real money, so it signals that something other than money is at work.", "Rejecting earns the responder extra money, so rejections reveal simple greed.", "The proposer must accept any counteroffer the responder makes, so both players always come out ahead.", "The total sum changes every round, so responders cannot compare the offers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Because a rejection costs both players, choosing to reject means giving up money. That makes it possible to see when something like anger, rather than the money, drives the decision."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the ultimatum game used by Sanfey and colleagues (2003), why is it surprising when a responder rejects a low offer?", "options": ["Rejecting means giving up money they could have kept.", "Rejecting gives the responder even more money.", "Rejecting forces the proposer to pay double.", "Rejecting is not allowed under the official rules of the game."], "correct": 0, "why": "A rejection leaves both players with nothing, so the responder gives up money; choosing that anyway points to emotion such as anger."}}, "7029": {"college": {"stem": "In Sanfey and colleagues’ (2003) study, how did participants treat low offers from computer partners compared with the same offers from human partners?", "options": ["They accepted low computer offers more often, being less angry with an unfair computer.", "They rejected low computer offers more often, because they distrusted the machines.", "They treated low offers the same way no matter who the partner was.", "They never received low offers from computers, so no comparison existed."], "correct": 0, "why": "An $8 and $2 split was accepted roughly 53% of the time from a human but about 85% of the time from a computer. Participants were less angry with an unfair computer."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sanfey and colleagues’ (2003) study, why were unfair offers from a computer accepted more often?", "options": ["People were less angry with an unfair computer.", "Computer offers always involved more money.", "People could not tell the offers were unfair.", "Computers punished people who rejected offers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Low offers were often rejected out of anger at unfairness, and people were less angry when a computer made the unfair offer."}}, "7030": {"college": {"stem": "In Sanfey and colleagues’ (2003) study, which brain result accompanied rejecting more offers?", "options": ["Greater activation of the right anterior insula, an area tied to emotional states.", "Reduced activation of the right anterior insula, an area tied to language.", "Greater activation of the hippocampus, an area tied to memory for numbers and amounts.", "Damage to the prefrontal cortex, which flattens emotional responses."], "correct": 0, "why": "Participants with more activation of the right anterior insula, which is connected with emotional states, were more likely to reject more offers. The direction was greater, not reduced, activation."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Sanfey and colleagues’ (2003) study, more activity in which brain area went with rejecting more offers?", "options": ["The right anterior insula, linked to emotion.", "The hippocampus, which is linked to memory.", "The cerebellum, which helps control movement.", "The visual cortex, which processes what we see."], "correct": 0, "why": "More activation of the right anterior insula, an area connected with emotional states, went with rejecting more offers."}}, "7031": {"college": {"stem": "Which description best captures deductive reasoning?", "options": ["Determining whether a conclusion follows logically from a set of premises.", "Drawing likely conclusions from many repeated observations of events.", "Choosing among alternatives based on the emotions one expects to feel.", "Judging how likely an event is by how easily instances come to mind."], "correct": 0, "why": "Deductive reasoning is determining whether a conclusion logically follows from premises, and the syllogism is its basic form. Drawing likely conclusions from observations is a different kind of reasoning."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is deductive reasoning?", "options": ["Deciding if a conclusion logically follows from premises.", "Guessing what is likely to happen based on your past experience.", "Choosing based on how you expect to feel.", "Deciding how a choice should be worded."], "correct": 0, "why": "Deductive reasoning means determining whether a conclusion logically follows from the premises."}}, "7032": {"college": {"stem": "Premise 1: All birds are animals. Premise 2: All animals have four legs. Conclusion: All birds have four legs. How should this syllogism be judged?", "options": ["Valid, because the conclusion follows from the premises even though it is false.", "Invalid, because a syllogism with a false conclusion cannot be valid.", "Valid and true, because it shares its form with a true syllogism.", "Invalid, because categorical syllogisms are not allowed to describe physical features of animals."], "correct": 0, "why": "Validity concerns whether the conclusion follows from the premises, and this syllogism has the same form as the valid syllogism concluding that all birds eat food. Its second premise is false, so its conclusion is false despite being valid."}, "simple": {"stem": "All birds are animals. All animals have four legs. So, all birds have four legs. Is this syllogism valid?", "options": ["Yes, because the conclusion follows from the premises.", "No, because birds do not have four legs.", "No, because the second premise is false.", "Yes, because every single statement in it is completely true."], "correct": 0, "why": "It is valid because the conclusion follows logically from the premises. A false premise makes the conclusion false, but it does not make the reasoning invalid."}}, "7033": {"college": {"stem": "A friend claims that a valid syllogism always has a true conclusion. What correction should be made?", "options": ["A valid syllogism must have a true conclusion only when its premises are true.", "A valid syllogism has a true conclusion no matter what its premises say.", "A valid syllogism never has a true conclusion, because validity concerns form only.", "A valid syllogism has a true conclusion only when it uses the word some."], "correct": 0, "why": "If the two premises of a valid syllogism are true, its conclusion must be true. A valid form with a false premise can still produce a false conclusion."}, "simple": {"stem": "A syllogism is valid and both of its premises are true. What must be true of its conclusion?", "options": ["The conclusion must also be true.", "The conclusion must turn out false.", "The conclusion could be either true or false.", "The conclusion must be believable but may be false."], "correct": 0, "why": "When a syllogism is valid and its premises are true, the conclusion must be true."}}, "7034": {"college": {"stem": "Premise 1: All of the students are tired. Premise 2: Some tired people are irritable. Conclusion: Some of the students are irritable. What does this syllogism illustrate?", "options": ["Each statement sounds reasonable, yet the syllogism is not valid.", "Each statement is false, yet the syllogism is still valid.", "The syllogism is valid because its conclusion is believable.", "The syllogism is invalid simply because it contains the word some."], "correct": 0, "why": "Syllogisms can be invalid even though each premise and the conclusion seem reasonable. Believability does not make it valid, and using the word some is not what makes it invalid."}, "simple": {"stem": "All of the students are tired. Some tired people are irritable. So, some of the students are irritable. Why is this not valid?", "options": ["The irritable tired people need not be any of the students.", "The first premise is clearly false about most real students.", "The conclusion is too hard to believe.", "It contains more than two separate premises."], "correct": 0, "why": "The tired people who are irritable could all be people other than the students, so the conclusion does not follow even though every statement sounds reasonable."}}, "7035": {"college": {"stem": "A person accepts an invalid argument as logically sound because its conclusion matches what they already think is true. What is this tendency called?", "options": ["Belief bias, judging a syllogism valid when its conclusion is believable.", "Status quo bias, sticking with the default when a decision is hard.", "The framing effect, letting the wording of a choice shape the decision.", "Risk avoidance, steering away from choices with large negative consequences."], "correct": 0, "why": "Belief bias is the tendency to think a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable; 71% of participants judged invalid, believable conclusions as valid. The other terms describe decision biases, not reasoning about validity."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is belief bias?", "options": ["Thinking a syllogism is valid because its conclusion is believable.", "Thinking a syllogism is invalid because its premises are very long.", "Believing that a loss will hurt more than a gain.", "Sticking with the default choice when deciding is hard."], "correct": 0, "why": "Belief bias is judging a syllogism valid because its conclusion is believable."}}, "7036": {"college": {"stem": "A puzzle asks where the cue ball lies in a person’s line of sight, given the positions of a black, green, and red ball on a pool table. How do most people solve it?", "options": ["They imagine how the balls are arranged on the table.", "They apply a formal rule for checking whether syllogisms are valid.", "They count how many balls sit on each side of the table.", "They recall an earlier game that had the same arrangement."], "correct": 0, "why": "Most people solve the pool table problem by imagining the arrangement of the balls. That ability to imagine situations is the basis of Johnson-Laird’s mental model proposal."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the pool table puzzle about the black, green, red, and cue balls, what do most people do to find the answer?", "options": ["They picture the balls on the table in their mind.", "They use a formal rule from logic to work it out.", "They guess, because the puzzle has no real answer.", "They remember a game they once watched on television."], "correct": 0, "why": "Most people imagine how the balls are arranged, which is the idea behind the mental model approach."}}, "7037": {"college": {"stem": "In Johnson-Laird’s (1999) mental model approach, what is a mental model?", "options": ["A specific situation held in the mind that helps determine whether a syllogism is valid.", "A general rule stored in long-term memory that grants permission once a stated condition is met.", "A list of premises that all take the form if p, then q.", "An emotion that a person predicts feeling about some outcome."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental model is a specific situation held in a person’s mind that can be used to help determine the validity of syllogisms. A rule granting permission is a permission schema."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Johnson-Laird’s (1999) approach, what is a mental model?", "options": ["A situation pictured in the mind to help judge validity.", "A rule that tells you when something is permitted to happen.", "A feeling you expect to have about an outcome.", "A premise written in the form if p, then q."], "correct": 0, "why": "A mental model is a specific situation held in the mind that helps determine whether a syllogism is valid."}}, "7038": {"college": {"stem": "Using Johnson-Laird’s (1999) mental model approach, a reasoner finds one arrangement in which the premises are true but the conclusion is false. What follows?", "options": ["It is not valid, since a valid conclusion cannot be refuted by any model of the premises.", "The conclusion is valid, since at least one model of the premises can be built.", "The conclusion is valid only if the reasoner spotted the exception quickly.", "The exception should be ignored, since tentative conclusions are always accepted as they stand."], "correct": 0, "why": "A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises. A single case that refutes it is enough to show that the conclusion does not follow."}, "simple": {"stem": "Under Johnson-Laird’s (1999) mental model approach, when is a conclusion valid?", "options": ["When no model of the premises can refute it.", "When at least one model of the premises fits it.", "When the conclusion sounds believable to most people.", "When the reasoner reaches it very quickly."], "correct": 0, "why": "A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises."}}, "7039": {"college": {"stem": "Which step of Johnson-Laird’s (1999) mental model approach is aimed directly at finding a case that refutes the conclusion?", "options": ["Looking for exceptions that would falsify the model.", "Creating an initial model of the situation described.", "Generating tentative conclusions about the model.", "Rewriting the first premise in the form if p, then q."], "correct": 0, "why": "The steps are creating a model, generating tentative conclusions, looking for exceptions to falsify the model, and determining validity. Looking for exceptions is the search for a case that refutes the conclusion."}, "simple": {"stem": "In Johnson-Laird’s (1999) mental model approach, what is done right after generating tentative conclusions?", "options": ["Look for exceptions that could falsify the model.", "Create a model of the situation.", "Decide right away that the syllogism must be valid after all.", "Add another premise to the syllogism."], "correct": 0, "why": "The order is: create a model, generate tentative conclusions, look for exceptions that falsify the model, then determine validity."}}, "7040": {"college": {"stem": "Conditional syllogisms have two premises and a conclusion. What form does the first premise take?", "options": ["It takes the form “If p, then q.”", "It takes the form “All p are q.”", "It takes the form “Some p are not q.”", "It takes the form “No p are q.”"], "correct": 0, "why": "In a conditional syllogism the first premise has the form if p, then q, and there are four major types. Statements with all, some or no belong to categorical syllogisms."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is the form of the first premise in a conditional syllogism?", "options": ["If p, then q.", "All p are q.", "Some p are not q.", "No p are q."], "correct": 0, "why": "Conditional syllogisms begin with a premise of the form if p, then q."}}, "7041": {"college": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I studied. Therefore, I’ll get a good grade.” Which type of conditional syllogism is this, and how often was it judged correctly?", "options": ["Modus ponens, a valid form that 97% of participants judged correctly.", "Modus tollens, a valid form that 60% of participants judged correctly.", "An invalid form that only 40% of participants judged correctly.", "Modus ponens, an invalid form that 40% of participants judged correctly."], "correct": 0, "why": "Syllogism 1, modus ponens, has the second premise p and the conclusion q. It is valid and was judged correctly 97% of the time."}, "simple": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I studied. So, I’ll get a good grade.” Is this valid, and do people judge it well?", "options": ["Yes, and almost everyone judges it correctly.", "Yes, but most people judge it incorrectly.", "No, and most people notice the error.", "No, but most people think it is valid."], "correct": 0, "why": "This is modus ponens, a valid form judged correctly by 97% of participants."}}, "7042": {"college": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I didn’t get a good grade. Therefore, I didn’t study.” How should this conditional syllogism be evaluated?", "options": ["It is valid modus tollens, but only 60% of participants judged it correctly.", "It is invalid, and as many as 97% of participants judged it correctly.", "It is invalid, because not q can never lead to the conclusion not p.", "It is valid modus ponens, which 97% of participants judged correctly."], "correct": 0, "why": "Syllogism 2, modus tollens, has the second premise not q and the conclusion not p. It is valid, but only 60% judged it correctly."}, "simple": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I didn’t get a good grade. So, I didn’t study.” Is this valid?", "options": ["Yes, it is valid, though only 60% judge it correctly.", "No, it is invalid, though 97% judge it correctly.", "No, because a bad grade proves nothing about studying.", "Yes, and nearly everyone judges it correctly."], "correct": 0, "why": "This is modus tollens. It is valid, but only 60% of participants judged it correctly."}}, "7043": {"college": {"stem": "“If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona. I live in Arizona. Therefore, I live in Tucson.” What makes this conditional syllogism invalid?", "options": ["Someone can live in Arizona without living in Tucson, so q does not establish p.", "Tucson is not in Arizona, so the first premise of the argument is false.", "The conclusion is believable, and belief bias is what makes it invalid.", "Its second premise is not p, which cannot support any conclusion at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "This has the form of syllogism 3, with second premise q and conclusion p. The first premise does not say Tucson is the only place in Arizona, so the conclusion does not follow."}, "simple": {"stem": "“If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona. I live in Arizona. So, I live in Tucson.” Why is this not valid?", "options": ["A person could live somewhere else in Arizona.", "Tucson is not actually located in Arizona.", "The first premise is written in the wrong order.", "A conclusion is never allowed to mention a city."], "correct": 0, "why": "Living in Arizona does not mean living in Tucson, because there are other places in Arizona."}}, "7044": {"college": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I didn’t study. Therefore, I didn’t get a good grade.” How should this conditional syllogism be evaluated?", "options": ["It is not valid, and only 40% of participants judged it correctly.", "It is valid, and 97% of participants judged it correctly.", "It is valid, because not p always leads to the conclusion not q.", "It is not valid, and 60% of participants judged it correctly."], "correct": 0, "why": "Syllogism 4 has the second premise not p and the conclusion not q. The first premise says nothing about what happens without studying, so it is not valid, and only 40% judged it correctly."}, "simple": {"stem": "“If I study, I’ll get a good grade. I didn’t study. So, I didn’t get a good grade.” Is this valid?", "options": ["No, because a good grade might come without studying.", "Yes, because not studying always means a bad grade.", "Yes, because it has the same form as modus ponens.", "No, because the first premise is obviously false."], "correct": 0, "why": "The first premise says only what happens if I study; a good grade could still happen without studying, so the conclusion does not follow."}}, "7045": {"college": {"stem": "Which pattern holds across the accuracy results for the four types of conditional syllogism?", "options": ["Both valid forms were judged correctly more often than both invalid forms.", "Both invalid forms were judged correctly more often than both valid forms.", "All four forms were judged correctly about equally often.", "Modus tollens was judged correctly more often than modus ponens."], "correct": 0, "why": "The valid forms were judged correctly at 97% and 60%, and the invalid forms at 40% each, so both valid forms exceeded both invalid ones. Modus ponens, not modus tollens, was the easiest."}, "simple": {"stem": "Which conditional syllogism did people judge correctly most often?", "options": ["Modus ponens, judged correctly 97% of the time.", "Modus tollens, judged correctly 60% of the time.", "Syllogism 3, judged correctly 40% of the time.", "Syllogism 4, judged correctly 40% of the time."], "correct": 0, "why": "Modus ponens had the highest accuracy at 97%, followed by modus tollens at 60% and the two invalid forms at 40%."}}, "7046": {"college": {"stem": "To test the rule “If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other” with cards showing E, K, 4, and 7, which cards must be turned over?", "options": ["E and 7, since only those cards could reveal a violation of the rule.", "E and 4, since those cards match the vowel and the even number.", "Only the E, since the rule begins by mentioning a vowel.", "All four cards, since any one of them could hide a vowel."], "correct": 0, "why": "E must be turned over, and so must the 7, because a vowel behind it would break the rule. A 4 cannot break the rule, yet 46% chose it and only 7% chose the 7."}, "simple": {"stem": "In the Wason four-card problem, the cards show E, K, 4, and 7, and the rule is “if vowel, then even number.” Which two cards must be turned over?", "options": ["The E card and the 7 card.", "The E card and the 4 card.", "The K card and the 4 card.", "The K card and the 7 card."], "correct": 0, "why": "Only E and 7 could show the rule is broken. Many people wrongly pick the 4, which cannot break the rule."}}, "7047": {"college": {"stem": "A scientist tests the claim that every swan is white by searching specifically for a swan that is not white. Which principle is she following?", "options": ["The falsification principle, seeking cases that could show the rule is false.", "A confirmation strategy, seeking cases that show the rule holds true.", "Belief bias, accepting the rule because its conclusion is believable.", "The status quo bias, leaving the rule untested because the choice is hard."], "correct": 0, "why": "The falsification principle says that to test a rule, you must look for situations that falsify it. Most participants fail to do this in the abstract four-card problem."}, "simple": {"stem": "What does the falsification principle say?", "options": ["To test a rule, look for cases that could prove it false.", "To test a rule, look only for the cases that agree with it.", "A rule is correct if it sounds believable.", "A rule is true if most people accept it."], "correct": 0, "why": "To test a rule, you must look for situations that could falsify it, not cases that confirm it."}}, "7048": {"college": {"stem": "When the Wason four-card problem was restated as “If drinking beer, then 21 years old,” how did performance change compared with the vowel-and-number version?", "options": ["Correct responses greatly increased once the rule was put in concrete everyday terms.", "Correct responses dropped, because the drinking rule was harder to follow.", "Correct responses stayed at the same level as in the abstract version.", "Participants declined to answer, because the drinking rule felt unfamiliar and confusing to them."], "correct": 0, "why": "When the problem is stated in concrete everyday terms, correct responses greatly increase. People are familiar with rules like the drinking-age rule, so the improvement is the opposite of a drop."}, "simple": {"stem": "What happens when the Wason four-card problem uses an everyday rule about drinking age?", "options": ["Many more people answer it correctly.", "Far fewer people answer it correctly.", "The same number of people answer correctly.", "People refuse to answer the problem at all."], "correct": 0, "why": "Stating the problem in concrete everyday terms greatly increases correct responses."}}, "7049": {"college": {"stem": "The rule “If you are 21 or older, you may drink beer” matches which kind of structure that helps people with the concrete four-card problems?", "options": ["A permission schema, in which satisfying condition A allows action B.", "A reversed conditional, in which carrying out B proves that A was satisfied.", "Belief bias, in which a believable conclusion makes an argument valid.", "Risk aversion, in which a loss is expected to weigh more than a gain."], "correct": 0, "why": "A permission schema states that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. Such schemas are used in the concrete versions because people are familiar with these rules."}, "simple": {"stem": "What is a permission schema?", "options": ["A rule that if A is satisfied, B can be done.", "A rule that if B is done, A must be true.", "A rule that believable conclusions are valid.", "A rule that losses matter more than gains."], "correct": 0, "why": "A permission schema says that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out."}}, "7050": {"college": {"stem": "Which conclusion about the concrete four-card problems fits both the point that context is important and the point that familiarity is not always important?", "options": ["A setting that calls up a permission schema helps, but familiarity alone does not explain it.", "Familiarity alone explains every improvement observed, so the context of the rule does not matter at all.", "Context matters only for abstract letters and numbers, not for everyday rules.", "Neither context nor familiarity has any measurable effect on performance."], "correct": 0, "why": "Context is important, because the same logic becomes easier in a setting that calls up a permission schema. Familiarity is not always important, so it cannot account for the improvement on its own."}, "simple": {"stem": "What two conclusions follow from the concrete versions of the four-card problem?", "options": ["Context is important, but familiarity is not always important.", "Familiarity is always important, but context does not matter.", "Neither context nor familiarity affects performance.", "Context matters only for letters and numbers."], "correct": 0, "why": "Context is important, and familiarity is not always important."}}};
